9998,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"ChessInstruct_18","prompt":"Please read the following question about chess moves and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: Given some set of chess moves, write who is more advantaged (white or black)\n\nQuestion: {{\"moves\": [\"e2e4\", \"e7e5\", \"d2d4\", \"e5d4\", \"g1f3\", \"b8c6\", \"c2c3\", \"d7d6\", \"c3d4\", \"f8e7\", \"b1c3\", \"g8f6\", \"f1e2\", \"d6d5\", \"e4e5\", \"f6e4\", \"d1b3\", \"c8e6\", \"e1g1\", \"e8g8\", \"c1e3\", \"c6a5\", \"b3c2\", \"e4c3\", \"c2c3\", \"a5c4\", \"e3c1\", \"a7a5\", \"e2d3\"]}}\n\nOptions: \nA. White\nB. Black\n\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given move sequence reaches a complex but balanced middlegame position arising from a Scotch Gambit. All pieces remain on the board (knights were exchanged on c3) and both sides have castled, but White enjoys a clear space advantage thanks to the cramping e5-pawn, better-centralized bishops (especially the newly developed Bd3 eyeing c4), and a more active queen on c3. Black’s knight on c4 is therefore under pressure and the queenside pieces remain somewhat uncoordinated.\n\nConsequently White holds the advantage.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":383,"output_tokens":113},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001331,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"ChessInstruct_19","prompt":"Please read the following question about chess moves and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: Given some set of chess moves, write who is more advantaged (white or black)\n\nQuestion: {{\"moves\": [\"c2c4\", \"e7e5\", \"g2g3\", \"g8f6\", \"f1g2\", \"b8c6\", \"b1c3\", \"f8c5\", \"a2a3\", \"e8g8\", \"d2d3\", \"d7d6\", \"b2b4\", \"c5d4\", \"c1b2\", \"e5e4\", \"e2e3\", \"d4c3\", \"b2c3\", \"c8f5\", \"c3f6\", \"d8f6\", \"d3d4\", \"c6e7\", \"d1c2\", \"d6d5\", \"c4d5\", \