tokens":202,"output_tokens":100},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.04e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_704","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this work, a man is impressed into filling trenches dug by rebels in a nearby neighborhood.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement is a plot clue pointing to a specific literary work (likely drawn from an identification quiz, exam, or trivia set). The scene matches a key episode in Graham Greene's *The Power and the Glory*, in which the unnamed priest is conscripted by Red Shirts (the novel's secular rebels) to help fill in trenches containing the bodies of executed Cristeros.  \n\nNo other widely taught novel contains this precise incident, confirming the identification.\n\n\\boxed{The Power and the Glory (Greene)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":199,"output_tokens":104},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.18e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_294","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Thomas Dekker based his play Satiromastix on the feud between John Marston and this author, which was dubbed \"The War of the Theatres.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The author referenced in the question is Ben Jonson. Thomas Dekker's play *Satiromastix* drew from the satirical conflict between playwrights during the \"War of the Theatres,\" primarily targeting Ben Jonson, who was a central figure in the feud with John Marston. \n\n\\boxed{Ben Jonson}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.349e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1108","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one passage, this process is caused by things like a starched napkin, a clattering spoon, and some uneven paving stones.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes several specific sensory triggers (starched napkin, clattering spoon, uneven paving stones) that appear in Marcel Proust's *In Search of Lost Time*. These are classic examples of the sudden, spontaneous recovery of long-forgotten memories that the narrator experiences, a phenomenon Proust explicitly labels *mémoire involontaire*. The process is therefore involuntary memory (distinct from both voluntary recollection and the more famous madeleine episode).\n\n\\boxed{involuntary memory (Proust)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":97},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.99e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_460","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A novel set at one of these places opens by describing the annual arrival of an \"assembly of station wagons.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel described is *The Wasp Factory* by Iain Banks, but the context of station wagons arriving annually is more famously associated with summer camps. The most common answer to this question, based on the opening line, is a summer camp.\n\n\\boxed{summer camp}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":82,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.546e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1503","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After Cody is killed in an event of this type, a white-haired \"stickman\" holds Cody in his arms and tells him God loves him at the end of George Saunders's story \"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query is a partial clue (likely from a quiz or crossword) that requires identifying the relevant George Saunders story and the specific calamity that befalls the recurring character Cody. The details match the story “Brad Carrigan, American” (from the collection *In Persuasion Nation*), whose final scene depicts Cody’s death in an aviation disaster followed by the consoling apparition of the white-haired stickman. Consequently the “event of this type” named by the clue is a plane crash.\n\n\\boxed{plane crash}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.51e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1889","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Lionel Trilling contrasted the liberal reception of this author with that of Henry James in his polemical essay \"Reality in America.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The essay in question is the title piece of Lionel Trilling's 1950 collection *The Liberal Imagination*. In it Trilling attacks the mid-century liberal-critical establishment for championing Theodore Dreiser's clumsy naturalism as \"reality\" while condescending to Henry James's psychological and formal sophistication. The contrast is therefore between Dreiser and James, and the author whose reception Trilling is criticizing is Dreiser.\n\n\\boxed{Theodore Dreiser}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.5e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_966","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this country, a slaughterhouse where the workers drink blood employs Kurt, whose friends share Lola's diary after her suicide.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query is a compact plot mashup of multiple films by Rainer Werner Fassbinder, all made and set in West Germany:\n\n- The slaughterhouse whose workers drink fresh blood is the pivotal scene in In a Year of 13 Moons.\n- The exploited everyman whose circle of “friends” strips him of everything is the title character of Fox and His Friends (original German title: Faustrecht der Freiheit).\n- Lola and the posthumously shared diary evoke the films Lola and Veronika Voss.\n\nHence the single country that contains all of these elements is Germany.\n\n\\boxed{Germany}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":118},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.999999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1306","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A bureaucrat from this country forges a manuscript about a 17th-century timekeeper to justify the title government agency in the satirical novel  \n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a plot element from a satirical novel whose title refers to a government agency (a \"ministry\"). The key details—a forged 17th-century manuscript concerning a chronometer or clock—point directly to the Czech novel *The Ministry of Time* (2014) by Hany Rashid. Its protagonist is a Czech bureaucrat, so the country referenced by the clue is the Czech Republic (or Czechia).\n\n\\boxed{Czech Republic}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":91},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.73e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1002","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Officials carrying out this European country's policies place an activist on trial in the novel  \n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to a novel in which officials from a European country place an activist on trial. This describes the plot of *The Trial* by Franz Kafka, where the bureaucratic authorities of Austria-Hungary (a European empire) prosecute the protagonist, Josef K., though the exact charges are never revealed. The most common answer to the country associated with the novel's setting and Kafka's background is Austria.\n\n\\boxed{Austria}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":76,"output_tokens":88}},"cost":6.267999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1189","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: character by this name is shown a photograph of a messenger practicing the high jump by the Bridge Inn's landlady.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a very specific scene from Franz Kafka's novel *The Castle*. In that work the Bridge Inn (Brückenhof) landlady shows the protagonist a photograph of one of the Count's messengers practicing the high jump, evidently to illustrate the messenger's physical prowess and suitability for castle service. The only character who receives this demonstration is the land-surveyor known simply as K.\n\nThus the name required by the query is K.\n\n\\boxed{K}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":92},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.66e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1609","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Supposed \"supplements\" to this text, published by François Nodot (noh-doh) and José Marchena, were later shown to be forgeries.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue describes a famous literary work whose incomplete surviving manuscripts prompted later attempts at reconstruction. Both Nodot (in 1694) and Marchena (in 1800) published lengthy “supplements” purporting to fill the lacunae; these were exposed as fabrications in the nineteenth century. The work in question is therefore Petronius’ Satyricon.\n\n\\boxed{Satyricon (Petronius)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":216,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.72e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_667","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: While staying in this city, a man hears the story of Curran, a circus manager whose passion was founding a church exclusively for dogs.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city being referenced is Dublin, as the story of Curran, a circus manager wanting to found a church for dogs, is a fictional anecdote associated with Dublin's eccentric tales. This story is often linked to the character of Curran in James Joyce's *Ulysses*, set in Dublin. \n\n\\boxed{Dublin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":87,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.3309999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_267","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Denis Diderot revealed his atheism in a letter to a person with this condition.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to Diderot's 1749 work *Lettre sur les aveugles à l'usage de ceux qui voient*. This text was framed as a letter discussing a blind person (the mathematician Nicolas Saunderson) and explicitly advanced materialist arguments that amounted to atheism, resulting in Diderot's arrest. Hence the condition referenced is blindness.\n\n\\boxed{blindness}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":197,"output_tokens":78},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.84e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1381","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one of this author's books, British women style their hair in either ringlets or spit curls to show support for the opposing sides of the Turkish-Serbian War.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a specific historical and cultural detail from the opening chapters of *Daniel Deronda* (1876), in which fashionable British women signal partisan views on the contemporaneous Eastern Crisis by adopting either “Serbian” ringlets or “Turkish” spit-curls. That novel is the work of George Eliot (Marian Evans), whose fiction is the only major Victorian corpus that incorporates this precise sartorial reference to the 1876 Serbo-Turkish War. No other nineteenth-century novelist matches the combination of date, setting, and topical allusion.\n\n\\boxed{George Eliot}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":116},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001006,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_916","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one story by this author, the 104-year-old general George Poker Sash dies onstage in a wheelchair at a graduation before his granddaughter Sally collects her diploma.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene is the climax of Flannery O’Connor’s short story “A Late Encounter with the Enemy,” in which the centenarian Confederate veteran General George Poker Sash collapses during his granddaughter Sally’s college graduation. O’Connor’s recurring Southern Gothic motifs—grotesque elderly figures, mordant humor, and sudden death—make the identification unmistakable. No other author matches both the specific character names and the precise episode.\n\n\\boxed{Flannery O'Connor}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":95},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.01e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1681","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A play on this title names Michael Gorra's literary biography about the \"making of an American masterpiece.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to Michael Gorra's 2012 study *Portrait of a Novel*, whose title is an explicit variation on Henry James's 1881 novel. That novel is the \"American masterpiece\" whose composition Gorra examines. Hence the title being played upon is *The Portrait of a Lady*.\n\n\\boxed{The Portrait of a Lady}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":69},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.47e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1071","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A \"poor chap\" who \"always loved larking\" suffers this fate in a Stevie Smith poem that notes \"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrases are taken directly from Stevie Smith’s poem “Not Waving but Drowning.” In the poem the “poor chap” who “always loved larking” is the drowned man whose frantic gestures were misread as playfulness; the title and refrain therefore supply the fate itself. The question is therefore a compact reference to that well-known title.\n\n\\boxed{not waving but drowning}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":79},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.01e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1471","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Although this character's creator claimed he was a purely imaginative product, he was partly modeled on David Shipman.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a literary character whose author (James Fenimore Cooper) publicly insisted was invented out of whole cloth. Historical scholarship, however, has established that Cooper drew partial inspiration for the frontier scout from David Shipman, a real-life hunter and trapper who lived near Cooperstown, New York. The figure in question is therefore Natty Bumppo (also known as Leatherstocking, Hawkeye, or Deerslayer).\n\n\\boxed{Natty Bumppo (Leatherstocking)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":100},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.02e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1172","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one story, eight masqueraders dressed as these animals in costumes made of flax and tar are hoisted thirty feet upward by a chain affixed to a pulley and are burnt alive by a torch taken from a caryatid (CARE-yah-tid) .\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific scene from Edgar Allan Poe's short story \"Hop-Frog\" (1850). In it, the cruel king and his seven ministers (exactly eight masqueraders) are tricked into wearing highly flammable costumes of flax and tar to resemble orangutans; they are then suspended from a chain and pulley and set ablaze by a torch from a caryatid figure.  \n\nThus the animals referenced are orangutans (sometimes styled \"orang-outangs\" in period spelling).  \n\n\\boxed{orangutans (Hop-Frog)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":233,"output_tokens":112},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001026,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_974","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A man who engages in this activity with \"pure Teutonic pleasure\" is one of the two subjects of John McPhee's \"double profile\"  Levels of the Game .\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The book *Levels of the Game* (1969) is John McPhee’s paired profile of tennis players Arthur Ashe and Clark Graebner, built around their 1968 Forest Hills semifinal. The quoted phrase “pure Teutonic pleasure” is McPhee’s description of Graebner’s methodical, aggressive style on court. Consequently the activity in question is tennis.\n\n\\boxed{tennis}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":83},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.49e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1045","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A town described by this adjective contains the Weary Gentleman saloon, which is where a drummer and the barkeep discuss the possibility of \"some shootin' Â– some good shootin'.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given quote matches a scene from Stephen Crane's short story \"The Bride Comes to Yellow Sky,\" which is set in a town of that name containing the Weary Gentleman saloon. The town's name is itself an adjectival phrase, so the adjective in question is the descriptor \"yellow.\" No other adjective is used to characterize the town in the story.\n\n\\boxed{yellow}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":76},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.16e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1423","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Sarah Brigham's 2016 revival of this play prefaces it with an hour-long monologue titled \"Jinny.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given text is a quiz-style clue (e.g., from Quiz Bowl or a similar trivia format) whose referent is the title of a stage play. The distinctive detail of an hour-long prefatory monologue titled “Jinny” points directly to an adaptation of Virginia Woolf’s novel The Waves, whose six interior monologues include the character Jinny; Sarah Brigham’s 2016 production at the University of San Diego opened with precisely that monologue.  \n\nThus the play referenced by “this play” is The Waves.\n\n\\boxed{The Waves}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":108},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.46e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_697","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a 2000 novel by Joyce Carol Oates, this real-life person is murdered by a government agent called the Sharpshooter.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The 2000 novel by Joyce Carol Oates is *Blonde*, a fictionalized account of Marilyn Monroe's life. In the novel, Marilyn Monroe is portrayed as being murdered by a government agent known as the Sharpshooter, reflecting conspiracy theories about her death. The real-life person in question is Marilyn Monroe.\n\n\\boxed{Marilyn Monroe}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":89,"output_tokens":75}},"cost":5.657e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_356","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The first and last stanzas of Philip Larkin's poem \"Annus Mirabilis\" mention this novel before \"The Beatles' first LP.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel mentioned before \"The Beatles' first LP\" in the first and last stanzas of Philip Larkin's \"Annus Mirabilis\" is *Lady Chatterley's Lover*. This reference highlights the cultural significance of the 1960 obscenity trial surrounding the novel's publication. \n\n\\boxed{Lady Chatterley's Lover}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.61e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_346","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: William Rothenstein encouraged this author to journey to London, where he nearly lost his only manuscript of a poetry collection on the metro.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anecdote refers to Rabindranath Tagore’s 1912 visit to London. William Rothenstein, having read Tagore’s Bengali manuscripts, urged him to travel from India; once there, Tagore misplaced the sole copy of the English Gitanjali on the Underground, but it was recovered and subsequently championed by Rothenstein, Yeats, and others. No other writer fits the documented conjunction of Rothenstein’s sponsorship and the lost-manuscript incident.\n\n\\boxed{Rabindranath Tagore}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":101},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.17e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1508","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a story credited to Laurentius Abstemius (law-REN-tee-uss ab-STEE-mee-us), one of these animals allows his daughter to marry a much smaller creature, and the daughter steps on her husband on the wedding night.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific fable by Abstemius in which a father elephant consents to his daughter's marriage to a tiny mouse; she accidentally crushes him on their wedding night. This matches the details exactly, so the animals referenced are elephants. The fable is a Renaissance-era moral tale illustrating mismatched unions.\n\n\\boxed{elephants}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":231,"output_tokens":66},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.92e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1250","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: David Wilbern wrote a psychoanalytic essay on Shakespeare's usage of this word 3ttingly entitled \"Shakespeare's [this word].\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a specific psychoanalytic study of Shakespearean language and wordplay. David Willbern’s well-known essay examines the multivalent term “nothing” (its links to negation, the female body, zero, and Lear’s “nothing will come of nothing”). The essay is therefore titled “Shakespeare’s Nothing,” making that the word referenced in the query.\n\n\\boxed{nothing}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":77},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.97e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1464","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an essay for Harper's Weekly, this writer recalled a train ride on which she hid the fact that she had a Jewish grandmother from an anti-Semitic colonel who later ordered roast beef hash for lunch.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anecdote matches Mary McCarthy’s 1953 essay “Artists in Uniform,” in which she describes concealing her Jewish grandmother from an anti-Semitic Army colonel during a train journey; the colonel later orders roast-beef hash in the dining car. Although the essay originally appeared in Harper’s Magazine rather than Harper’s Weekly, the details align precisely with the question. No other mid-century American writer is associated with this episode.\n\n\\boxed{Mary McCarthy}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":90},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.9e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_761","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In Henry Green's novel Loving, Raunce, a character with this profession, begins a relationship with his younger coworker Edith.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Raunce is a butler in Henry Green's novel *Loving*. He works at a country house and develops a romantic interest in Edith, a younger housemaid. His profession is central to the social dynamics of the story.\n\n\\boxed{butler}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.358e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_173","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Apart from \"A,\" this is the first word in the title of a poem that opens: \"I'll tell thee now (dear Love) what thou shalt do /\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are the opening of a poem by John Donne. Among Donne’s works the only poem whose title begins “A …” and that matches those lines is “A Valediction of the Book.” Consequently the word required by the query is simply the first word of that title after the initial article.\n\n\\boxed{Valediction}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":68},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.68e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_803","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a short story set in this country, a ten-year-old girl stops brushing her teeth to preserve the feeling of the candy she receives from the title character, a botany professor.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question requires identifying the country where a short story unfolds, with the plot centered on its title character (a botany professor) who gives candy to a 10-year-old girl; she then deliberately stops brushing her teeth so the sensation will linger.  \n\nThis matches the distinctive story “O Professor de Botânica,” whose author (Lygia Fagundes Telles) and setting are Brazilian; the narrative detail about the candy is sufficiently idiosyncratic to rule out other candidates.  \n\nHence the country in question is Brazil.\n\n\\boxed{Brazil}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":109},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.790000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_692","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a play set in this country, a woman opts to ride through town on a chair carried by two men instead of riding in a car; that woman earlier pulled an emergency cord to stop the \"Flying Dutchman.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clues reference two specific incidents involving the central character Shen Te in Bertolt Brecht's *The Good Woman of Szechwan* (also translated *The Good Person of Szechwan*). In scene 4 she pulls the emergency brake on the train nicknamed the Flying Dutchman; in scene 8 she elects to be carried through the streets in a traditional sedan chair rather than a motor car. Both episodes occur in the play's explicitly identified setting of Szechwan province, China.\n\nThus the country in question is China.\n\n\\boxed{China}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":224,"output_tokens":111},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001003,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1384","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this play, a man is instructed to crown bowls with freshly shorn wool before pouring out three drink-o ferings from a holy spring and laying olive sprays on the wet ground.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described ritual matches the instructions given by a local inhabitant to Oedipus for making offerings to the Eumenides (Furies) at their sacred grove. This scene occurs early in Sophocles’ final play, Oedipus at Colonus, and the details (spring water, three libations, freshly shorn wool to “crown” the bowls, and olive branches) are quoted almost verbatim from the text. No other extant Greek tragedy reproduces this precise sequence of actions performed by a male protagonist.\n\n\\boxed{Oedipus at Colonus (Sophocles)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":111},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.89e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_206","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Three sadistic children in this novel force a rabbit to run as fast as it can, then tug its collapsed body along a string until it nearly dies.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene does not occur in any well-known novel and appears to be fabricated or misattributed. There is no widely recognized literary work featuring three sadistic children torturing a rabbit in this manner. Therefore, the premise is likely incorrect or fictional.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{None}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":59}},"cost":4.71e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1228","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At the conclusion of a book by this author, the protagonist receives her diamond engagement ring back from her captors, whose other gi t to her was the 9 millimeter bullet they didn't shoot her with.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene occurs at the end of *Gone Girl* by Gillian Flynn, where Amy Dunne manipulates events to return to her husband Nick, who gives her back the diamond engagement ring. The \"gift\" of the unshot 9mm bullet refers to Amy's psychological control and the violence that was implied but not carried out. The author is Gillian Flynn.\n\n\\boxed{Gillian Flynn}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":85}},"cost":6.426e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_845","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This man wrote an article about the employees at the Office of Economic Opportunity in San Francisco, whom he dubbed \"Flak Catchers\" taking abuse from \"mau-mauers.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The man who wrote the article about the employees at the Office of Economic Opportunity in San Francisco, referring to them as \"Flak Catchers\" dealing with \"mau-mauers,\" was Tom Wolfe. This piece, titled \"The Me Decade and the Third Great Awakening,\" showcased his signature style of New Journalism. The term \"mau-mauers\" was used controversially to describe aggressive activists.\n\n\\boxed{Tom Wolfe}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":90}},"cost":6.648e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1329","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A ter moving to this city, Zhuang meets her older lover in a movie theater and refers to herself only as \"Z\" in a Xiaolu Guo (SHYAO-lu GWOH) novel.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes the plot of Xiaolu Guo’s 2007 novel *A Concise Chinese-English Dictionary for Lovers*. Its narrator, a young Chinese woman named Zhuang (who signs herself only “Z”), moves to London to study English and begins an affair with an older Englishman she first meets in a cinema. Hence the city in question is London.\n\n\\boxed{London}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":221,"output_tokens":75},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.170000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1530","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The stage directions for this play's longest speech prescribe that it be delivered with \"a great deal of action\" to cast a \"hypnotic effect\" over both the listening character and the audience.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question describes a highly specific set of stage directions attached to a single extended monologue, which immediately narrows the field to modern American one-act drama. Among the handful of plays that contain such a lengthy, deliberately mesmeric speech, only Edward Albee’s The Zoo Story matches the quoted phrasing verbatim: Jerry’s “The Story of Jerry and the Dog” is accompanied by the explicit direction that it be performed “with a great deal of action” so as to exert a “hypnotic effect” on both Peter and the audience. No other canonical play (O’Neill, Albee’s later works, Shaffer, etc.) reproduces both quoted phrases together.\n\nThus the play in question is The Zoo Story.\n\n\\boxed{The Zoo Story}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":149},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001185,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_506","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The McGuffey Readers used many passages from this author to teach elocution to American students.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The McGuffey Readers used passages from William Shakespeare to teach elocution to American students. His works were valued for their rich language and dramatic expression, making them ideal for teaching reading and speaking skills. \n\n\\boxed{William Shakespeare}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":79,"output_tokens":49}},"cost":3.967e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_278","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Acts 1 and 2 of an early version of this play include a non-speaking ghost character named Innogen.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a Shakespeare play containing a non-speaking “ghost” character named Innogen (Leonato’s wife) who appears only in stage directions of the 1600 quarto. This figure is absent from later texts and from the performed version of the play, matching the textual history of Much Ado About Nothing exactly. No other Shakespeare play fits the combination of the name, the silent status, and the restriction to Acts 1–2 of an early edition.\n\n\\boxed{Much Ado About Nothing}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":102},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.16e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_109","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel written in this format, mosquitoes die upon contact with the \"dumb-ox\" Yanzao due to the massive amounts of pesticides that he spreads around the village of Maqiao.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific novel whose structure consists of discrete, alphabetized entries (rather than conventional chapters) that define local terms, customs, and characters from the fictional village of Maqiao. That novel is Han Shaogong’s *A Dictionary of Maqiao* (1996), whose protagonist-narrator records, among many other items, the pesticide-laden “dumb-ox” Yanzao. Consequently the format alluded to is a dictionary.\n\n\\boxed{dictionary}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":97},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.25e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_948","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This poet declared that \"two poetries are ever competing, a cooked and a raw\" in an acceptance speech for a 1960 National Book Award.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote in question originates from Robert Lowell's 1960 National Book Award acceptance speech for *Life Studies*. In it, Lowell distinguished between \"raw\" (confessional, free-form) and \"cooked\" (formal, academic) poetry, a dichotomy that became a touchstone for mid-century American poetics. No other poet's NBA remarks from that year match the phrasing or context.\n\n\\boxed{Robert Lowell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":84},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.42e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_360","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This author depicted a dictator named Kamini, who tries to solve a financial crisis by printing more money, in A Play of Giants.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The author who depicted a dictator named Kamini in *A Play of Giants* is Wole Soyinka. This play satirizes African dictators and their misuse of power, with Kamini representing the absurdity of authoritarian rule. Soyinka, a Nigerian Nobel laureate, is known for his political critiques through drama.\n\n\\boxed{Wole Soyinka}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.558e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1771","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a job interview, one of this author's characters is asked to translate a note written in French but is too nervous to say that he cannot read the handwriting and insists that doigts (dwah) means \"⎜ngers.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific scene involving a nervous job candidate who is asked to translate handwritten French but cannot admit his difficulty with the script, leading him to overconfidently gloss “doigts” as “fingers.” This episode occurs in Jonathan Franzen’s 2001 novel *The Corrections*, during Chip Lambert’s disastrous interview with a market-research firm. No other contemporary author’s work contains a matching incident, confirming Franzen as the referent.\n\n\\boxed{Jonathan Franzen}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":226,"output_tokens":97},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.37e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1314","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A famous dramatist and translator of this language advocated its classical forms in the \"Epistle on Poetry.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question requires identifying a language based on a historical literary reference. The phrasing points to neoclassical advocacy via an \"Epistle on Poetry,\" which matches the two epistles written by Russian dramatist/translator Alexander Sumarokov (one on poetry, one on language) to promote classical Russian forms. No other language has a comparably direct match for both the authorial figure and the named work.\n\n\\boxed{Russian}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":86},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.32e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1009","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem written in this modern country, the speaker declares \"make me, O Lord, Thy Spinning Wheel Complete\".\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted line is the opening of the poem \"Huswifery\" by Edward Taylor, a Puritan minister who emigrated from England and spent his adult life in colonial Massachusetts. Taylor composed the work in the late seventeenth century, so the territory in which it was written is the modern United States. No other historically attested poem matches the quoted phrasing.\n\n\\boxed{United States}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":74},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.8e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_758","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At one point, a man tells the head of this place that he is holding his \"tool\" in his hand while lying under a blanket, implying that he is pleasuring himself rather than meditating.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The scene described occurs in the movie *The Matrix*, where the character Cypher betrays the crew of the Nebuchadnezzar while pretending to be meditating. He speaks to Agent Smith, saying, \"I'm tired of this damn place... I'm going to show these people what I'm really made of,\" while implying he is pleasuring himself under a blanket. This moment symbolizes his rejection of the harsh reality and embrace of illusion.\n\n\\boxed{The Matrix}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":99}},"cost":7.24e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_876","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Late in a play, this character reverses an insult about being called a \"squashed cabbage leaf.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query refers to a key moment in George Bernard Shaw’s *Pygmalion*. Early in the play Professor Higgins insults the flower-girl Eliza Doolittle by calling her “a squashed cabbage leaf.” In the final act she deliberately hurls the same phrase back at him, thereby “reversing” the insult and demonstrating her new social and verbal equality.\n\nThus the character in question is Eliza Doolittle.\n\n\\boxed{Eliza Doolittle (Pygmalion)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":200,"output_tokens":99},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.950000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_271","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Richard Wilbur produced the first rhymed English translation of a play by this author, though he did not keep that play's alexandrine couplets.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Richard Wilbur translated Molière's play *Tartuffe*, creating a rhymed English version while abandoning the original French alexandrine couplets. Molière is the most common answer associated with this translation feat. \n\n\\boxed{Molière}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":56}},"cost":4.5430000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_386","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One of this author's title characters declares \"We shall yet make these United States a moral nation!\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote is spoken by the title character of the 1927 novel *Elmer Gantry*. That novel was written by Sinclair Lewis, whose other title characters (Babbitt, Arrowsmith, Dodsworth, etc.) likewise embody his satirical portraits of American moral and social hypocrisy.  \n\nThus the author referenced by the query is Sinclair Lewis.\n\n\\boxed{Sinclair Lewis}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":199,"output_tokens":76},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.78e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_369","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A racist in this novel cites Goddard's book The Rise of the Colored Empires to argue that white \"Nordics\" have created \"all the things that go to make civilization.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel in question is *The Great Gatsby* by F. Scott Fitzgerald. The character Tom Buchanan expresses racist views and cites the fictional book *The Rise of the Colored Empires* by Goddard to support white supremacy. This moment highlights the novel’s critique of 1920s American elitism and racial anxiety.\n\n\\boxed{The Great Gatsby}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":78}},"cost":5.967e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1315","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel from this modern-day nation, a relative of Chopin home from Singapore calls the tropics a \"disease\" with no cure and recalls how 4000 Malaysian workers spontaneously went on strike one day at noon.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The phrasing of the clue indicates that the novel's setting (and thus the author's nationality) is the place to which the character has returned home after time in Singapore. The reference to a relative of Chopin therefore points to a Polish character and a Polish author. The tropical details (incurable \"disease,\" Malaysian workers) are incidental