es in classical myth (Arachne, Philomela, Helen, Penelope, Athena), only Arachne is credited with covering her loom with multiple divine and heroic crimes, including episodes from the House of Atreus.  \n\nThus the character is Arachne.\n\n\\boxed{Arachne}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":117},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001005,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1792","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel that shares its title with this poem, Dario founds the Edinburgh Fascist Club after immigrating from Sicily to Scotland.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement describes a key plot point from a specific novel, whose title is shared with a well-known poem. The unique detail of the Sicilian immigrant Dario founding the Edinburgh Fascist Club directly identifies that novel as James Thomson’s *The City of Dreadful Night* (there is also a 1994 novel of exactly the same title that contains the described episode). Hence the poem referenced by the query must be the same work.\n\n\\boxed{The City of Dreadful Night}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":95},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.89e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_930","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Characters in this novel make banana jam despite it being illegal for being \"too thin for jelly and too thick for jam.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted description of illegal banana jam matches a key plot point from Arundhati Roy’s 1997 novel, in which the characters operate a pickle factory that also produces the nonconforming preserve. No other well-known work of fiction contains this specific legal and culinary detail.  \n\nThus the novel in question is *The God of Small Things*.\n\n\\boxed{The God of Small Things}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":77},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.93e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_243","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a story by this author, after the painter Clara Glencairn dies of a stroke, a rival contemporary paints a portrait of her, then never paints again.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question is a standard quiz-bowl style “identify the author” clue whose sole concrete datum is the anecdote about Clara Glencairn. That anecdote occurs in Silvina Ocampo’s short story “El retrato malévolo” (collected in Los días de la noche, 1970), in which a rival painter completes a posthumous portrait of Glencairn and then permanently abandons his art. No other well-known author’s work contains this precise incident, confirming Ocampo as the referent.\n\n\\boxed{Silvina Ocampo}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":111},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.83e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_258","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A play named after this character opens with a crowd carrying olive branches wrapped in wood to ward off the plague.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The play described is *Oedipus Rex* by Sophocles, which opens with Theban citizens bringing olive branches wrapped in wool to appease the gods during a plague. The character after whom the play is named is Oedipus. \n\n\\boxed{Oedipus}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":60}},"cost":4.6530000000000003e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_381","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A play set in this country ends with news of the Actor's suicide, causing Satine to respond that it \"spoiled the song.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to Maxim Gorky's play *The Lower Depths* (1902). Its cast includes the down-and-out characters Satine and the Actor; the play is set in a squalid boarding-house in Russia, and it ends with the Actor's off-stage suicide, prompting Satine's famous line that the death \"spoiled the song.\"\n\nThus the country in question is Russia.\n\n\\boxed{Russia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":84},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.36e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1569","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Members of this family title a Peter Ackroyd novel in which the bookseller William Ireland presents them with a lost Shakespeare play called Vortigern.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to Peter Ackroyd’s 2004 novel *The Lambs of London*, whose title characters are the siblings Charles and Mary Lamb. In the plot the forger William Henry Ireland attempts to pass off his fabricated “lost Shakespeare play” *Vortigern* on the Lambs and their literary circle. The family name that therefore titles the novel is simply the Lambs.\n\n\\boxed{Lambs (of London)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.58e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_475","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At the end of Zola's novel La bete humaine, Lantier and Pecqueux fall to their deaths from one of these objects.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"At the end of Émile Zola's novel *La Bête humaine*, Lantier and Pécqueux die in a train accident, falling from a locomotive during a dramatic confrontation. The \"object\" referred to is a train, which is central to the novel's themes of industrialization and human passion. \n\n\\boxed{train}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.502999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1526","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This language was used to write the novel Memoirs of a Porcupine and an essay addressed to James Baldwin titled Letter to Jimmy.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel *Memoirs of a Porcupine* was written by Alain Mabanckou, and the essay \"Letter to Jimmy\" was also authored by him, both originally in French. Alain Mabanckou is a Francophone writer from the Republic of the Congo, and his works are primarily composed in French. Therefore, the language used is French.\n\n\\boxed{French}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":81}},"cost":5.978e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_123","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A book by this author discusses the spiritual activism of Mildred Lisette Norman, or Peace Pilgrim, in a chapter on taking a pilgrimage to the Santuario de Chimayó (\"san-too-AH-ree-oh de chee-mah-YO\") in New Mexico.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a specific chapter in Rebecca Solnit's 2000 book *Wanderlust: A History of Walking*, which covers the annual pilgrimage to the Santuario de Chimayó and uses that setting to discuss the life and nonviolent activism of Peace Pilgrim (Mildred Norman). No other prominent author matches this precise combination of subject, location, and textual reference.  \n\nThus the author in question is Rebecca Solnit.\n\n\\boxed{Rebecca Solnit}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":234,"output_tokens":94},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.38e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_889","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the second stanza of a poem addressed to one of these things, the speaker says \"I would, before my time to go, / Sing of old Eire and the ancient ways.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are from W. B. Yeats’s “To the Rose upon the Rood of Time,” whose opening and closing apostrophes make clear that the poem is addressed to a rose. In quiz-bowl or crossword contexts the same lines are conventionally used as a clue for the answer “roses.”  \n\nThus the referent of “these things” is roses.\n\n\\boxed{roses}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":80},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.36e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_349","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A girl in this play explains that German \"isn't a becoming language\" because she looks \"quite plain\" after her German lesson.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted dialogue is spoken by the character Cecily Cardew during a conversation with Miss Prism in Act II. This exchange occurs in no other well-known play and matches the text of Oscar Wilde's *The Importance of Being Earnest* verbatim.  \n\nThus the play in question is *The Importance of Being Earnest*.\n\n\\boxed{The Importance of Being Earnest}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":73},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.79e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1497","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A novel from this country concludes with the protagonist in a mental asylum imagining himself on Mars where inhabitants only speak the word \"kah-ray-kuh-kuh-ko-kex.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel described is *Solaris* by Stanisław Lem, a renowned work of Polish science fiction. The protagonist, after experiencing psychological distress, ends up in a mental facility and imagines being on Mars where the inhabitants speak in a nonsensical phrase resembling \"kah-ray-kuh-kuh-ko-kex.\" This surreal ending is consistent with the philosophical and psychological themes in Lem's writing from Poland.\n\n\\boxed{Poland}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":91}},"cost":6.708e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_514","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This author mourned \"Down, down, down into the darkness of the grave /\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given quote is the opening line of the poem \"Dirge Without Music,\" first published in 1928. Its author is the Pulitzer Prize-winning poet Edna St. Vincent Millay, who uses the verse to mourn the deaths of loved ones. No other well-known writer is associated with this specific phrasing.\n\n\\boxed{Edna St. Vincent Millay}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":197,"output_tokens":73},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.59e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1127","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This author wondered in his poem \"The Solution\" whether it wouldn't be easier \"for the government /\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The author referred to is Bertolt Brecht, who wrote the poem \"The Solution\" (\"Die Lösung\" in German). In the poem, he ironically suggests that it might be easier for the government to dissolve the people and elect a new one. This satirical line critiques authoritarian responses to public dissent.\n\n\\boxed{Bertolt Brecht}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":80,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.3599999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_188","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An Italian prisoner in this novel paces his cell floor on his knees, licking the feet of a brass figurine of Christ.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel being referred to is *The Thief's Journal* by Jean Genet. In this autobiographical work, the Italian prisoner performs the described act of devotion and degradation, blending religious imagery with themes of suffering and desire. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{The Thief's Journal}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":59}},"cost":4.6450000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_234","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Due in part to a developmental disorder that renders her incapable of doing math, an office worker in this country is demoted to cleaning bathrooms in the novel Fear and Trembling by Amélie Nothomb.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel *Fear and Trembling* by Amélie Nothomb is set in Japan, where the protagonist, a Belgian woman working in a Japanese corporation, faces extreme cultural and professional challenges. Her inability to perform certain tasks, compounded by rigid workplace expectations, leads to her demotion. The country is Japan.\n\n\\boxed{Japan}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.4529999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1281","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This author described traveling around London with a companion app programmed with W. G. Sebald's personality in an account of the Olympics for Grantland.com, \"Hard Times in the Uncanny Valley.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific Grantland article on the 2012 London Olympics whose author inserts a running fictional conceit about a Sebald-programmed travel app. That piece was written by Brian Phillips (a frequent Grantland contributor whose long-form essays often blend literary references with sports and travel). No other Grantland writer matches both the byline and the Sebald-app anecdote.\n\n\\boxed{Brian Phillips}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":80},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.400000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_651","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 2004, the author of this play wrote a prequel in which one of its characters is slapped across the face by his wife Ann.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue identifies a well-known one-act play whose author later supplied it with an explicit prequel. In 2004 Edward Albee wrote the curtain-raiser “Homelife,” in which the protagonist Peter (the same character who later appears in the park in “The Zoo Story”) is struck across the face by his wife Ann. The two pieces were subsequently performed together under the joint title “Peter and Jerry,” confirming that “The Zoo Story” is the original work referenced.\n\nThus the play in question is The Zoo Story.\n\n\\boxed{The Zoo Story}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":113},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.85e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_534","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An author with this last name responded to the Brexit referendum by writing a 2016 novel that begins \"it was the worst of times, it was the worst of times.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The author being referred to is John Lanchester, who wrote the 2016 novel *The Debt to Pleasure*, though more accurately, his Brexit-related novel is *The Wall*, published later. However, the quote \"It was the worst of times, it was the worst of times\" is a play on Dickens' *A Tale of Two Cities* and appears in Lanchester's 2012 novel *Capital*, which critiques consumerism and societal issues in London—thematically linked to post-Brexit concerns. The correct last name is Lanchester.\n\n\\boxed{Lanchester}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":128}},"cost":8.928e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_653","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This person is actually the title character of the novel The Prince of India by Lew Wallace, in which he helps bring about the fall of Constantinople.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The title character of *The Prince of India* by Lew Wallace is Prince Otto of Bodenwald, a fictional character who plays a key role in the narrative surrounding the fall of Constantinople. However, the question contains a factual inaccuracy—Lew Wallace is best known for *Ben-Hur*, and *The Prince of India* is actually a novel by **Thomas Beer**, not Lew Wallace. The premise about the character helping bring about the fall of Constantinople is also incorrect, as the novel is a historical fantasy not centered on that event.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{None of the above}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":89,"output_tokens":122}},"cost":8.477e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1359","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a story from this country, a young man bursts a blood vessel a ter being stripped and tied to a table by a crowd that has gathered to watch the killing of 49 steers.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The story referenced is Esteban Echeverría’s 1838–1840 short story “El matadero” (“The Slaughterhouse”), an Argentine work of political allegory set during the Rosas dictatorship. In it, a young Unitarian is seized by a Federalist mob at a Buenos Aires slaughterhouse where 49 steers have been killed; he is stripped, bound to a table, and dies of a ruptured blood vessel from rage and humiliation. No other national literature contains a matching episode with these precise details.\n\nThus the country in question is Argentina.\n\n\\boxed{Argentina}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":116},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001016,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1241","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The poem \"To Return to the Trees\" concludes with the image of an \"obdurate almond going under the sand with [one of these things], slowly, by sand grains, by centuries.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted line is the final image of a poem (likely by a modern Arabic poet such as Adonis or Saadi Youssef) that depicts gradual desert burial over geological time. The only noun that completes the line both literally and symbolically—an obdurate almond sinking grain by grain while its dark silhouette is likewise effaced—is “its shadow.”  \n\nThus the completed phrase is the single, most common poetic solution.\n\n\\boxed{its shadow}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.85e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1015","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This work discusses a solution to the over-hunting of deer offered by a \"true Lover of his Country,\" but cites more approvingly the accounts of a \"very knowing American\" and \"famous Salmanaazor, a Native of the Island Formosa.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The work described is *A Modest Proposal* by Jonathan Swift, though the details mentioned are a misdirection; the essay actually addresses poverty and Irish oppression, not deer hunting. The references to \"Salmanaazor\" and a \"very knowing American\" parody pseudo-scholarly citations common in 18th-century tracts. However, the correct answer to the question, based on the satirical style and cited figures, is:\n\n\\boxed{A Modest Proposal}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":111,"output_tokens":98}},"cost":7.323e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1665","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This poet wrote that, although \"a prince can make a belted knight, / a marquis, [or a] duke,\" \"the pith of sense and pride of worth / are higher rank than all that.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The poet who wrote these lines is Robert Burns. In his poem \"A Man's a Man for A' That,\" Burns emphasizes the inherent worth of individuals regardless of social rank. The lines reflect his democratic ideals and critique of aristocracy.\n\n\\boxed{Robert Burns}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":104,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.652e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_740","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character created by this author contrasts \"spiritually married\" and \"spiritually unmarried\" men while defending socialism to Mr. Ferrenby.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted dialogue is spoken by Amory Blaine during a train-car debate near the end of the novel *This Side of Paradise*. Because Amory is a fictional creation of F. Scott Fitzgerald, the author referenced by the clue is Fitzgerald.\n\n\\boxed{F. Scott Fitzgerald}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":55},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":6.93e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_273","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A homeless character created by this author dreams of going to jail on Blackwell's Island for the winter in order to stay warm.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The homeless character who dreams of going to jail on Blackwell's Island for the winter to stay warm appears in O. Henry's short story \"The Cop and the Anthem.\" This story follows Soapy, a homeless man, as he tries to get arrested to avoid the harsh winter. The author, O. Henry, is known for his ironic and twist-filled tales.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{O. Henry}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":83}},"cost":6.085e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1577","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character nicknamed a⧔er this animal narrates his rise from poverty in a Booker-winning novel framed as a letter to Chinese premier Wen Jiabao.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel referenced here is Aravind Adiga's *The White Tiger* (2008 Man Booker Prize winner). Its narrator/protagonist Balram Halwai is explicitly nicknamed \"the white tiger\" and addresses his life story in the form of a letter to Wen Jiabao. The animal in question is therefore the tiger (specifically the white tiger that supplies the nickname and title).\n\n\\boxed{tiger (white tiger)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":87},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.61e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_601","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character in this play remembers a time when his son hit him in the face, before exclaiming \"Rotten kid!\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue is a standard trivia-style prompt (e.g., from quiz bowl or a similar knowledge test) whose expected referent is the title of the dramatic work in which the described scene occurs.  \n\nThe incident matches a flashback monologue delivered by the father, John Cleary, in Frank D. Gilroy’s 1964 play The Subject Was Roses; after returning from military service, his son Timmy strikes him during an argument, prompting the exclamation “Rotten kid!”  \n\nNo other well-known American play contains this precise combination of action and quoted line, confirming the identification.\n\n\\boxed{The Subject Was Roses}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":123},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001025,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1752","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay by this author recalls a woman wearing a \"dirty crepe-de-Chine (crep-duh-sheen) wrapper\" who tells a bartender that Estelle is the reason she and George Sharp separated.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The essay described is \"Goodbye to All That\" by Joan Didion, in which she recounts a memory of a woman in a dirty crepe-de-Chine wrapper discussing her separation from George Sharp, blaming Estelle. This personal and reflective piece is characteristic of Didion’s early nonfiction, set in New York City. The author is Joan Didion.\n\n\\boxed{Joan Didion}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":81}},"cost":6.199000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1204","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One character in this novel skips school every Thursday by wearing an eyepatch and pretending he has to go to the ophthalmologist.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described behavior matches a specific detail about the character Francis Abernathy in Donna Tartt's 1992 novel. Francis routinely fabricates ophthalmology appointments (complete with an eyepatch) to avoid Thursday classes while concealing other activities from his classmates. No other widely read novel contains this precise recurring ruse, confirming the identification.\n\n\\boxed{The Secret History (Tartt)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":76},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.92e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_324","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In what is considered this author's first poem, he praises the Roman historian Cornelius Nepos, who \"took courage to set forth the whole history of the world in three volumes.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted passage matches the text of Catullus 1 (the dedicatory poem to Cornelius Nepos that opens the surviving collection), in which the poet explicitly compliments Nepos for compressing world history into three rolls.  No other ancient author’s opening poem contains this reference, so the poet in question is Catullus (Gaius Valerius Catullus).  \n\n\\boxed{Catullus (poem 1)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":87},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.65e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1069","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character in this play boasts that he \"got the power of death\" after murdering a cat as a child when a priest failed to resurrect his dog.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anecdote about acquiring the \"power of death\" by strangling a cat after a priest refused to pray for a dead dog is recounted by the defrocked minister Lawrence Shannon in Tennessee Williams's The Night of the Iguana. The speech occurs in Act 2 as Shannon explains the childhood origin of his loss of faith and his subsequent compulsive behavior. No other well-known play contains this specific episode or phrasing.\n\n\\boxed{The Night of the Iguana (Williams)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":95},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.97e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_40","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A bearded figure in this novel \"wails with the vehemence of the ocean\" as he smites a giant crayfish with a bicycle pump.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted scene is a hallmark of Flann O’Brien’s comic-absurdist style, which routinely juxtaposes archaic/mythic diction (“wails with the vehemence of the ocean”) with modern mechanical objects (bicycle pump) and grotesque sea life. Among O’Brien’s novels only *At Swim-Two-Birds* fuses the Finn MacCool/Ossianic material—whose hero is repeatedly described as a bearded, ocean-voiced giant—with the novel’s running bicycle motif and its deliberately anachronistic set pieces. The other two O’Brien novels that feature bicycles (*The Third Policeman*, *The Dalkey Archive*) contain neither the crayfish episode nor the Finn-like figure.\n\nThus the novel in question is *At Swim-Two-Birds*.\n\n\\boxed{At Swim-Two-Birds}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":164},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001242,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_757","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A novel about this profession ends with Henry Wiggen lamenting \"from here on in, I rag nobody\" after Bruce Pearson dies of Hodgkin's disease.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel described is *Bang the Drum Slowly* by Mark Harris, which centers on professional baseball players. Henry Wiggen, the narrator, is a pitcher, and the story follows his relationship with teammate Bruce Pearson, a catcher diagnosed with Hodgkin's disease. The profession central to the novel is therefore baseball.\n\n\\boxed{baseball}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.323e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_25","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This book's opening word was first rendered in Greek as mataiotes (\"mah-tah-YO-tays\"), which may explain its corruption from an original meaning of \"breath.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The book being referenced is Ecclesiastes, which begins with the word \"Vanity\" in many English translations. This word translates the Hebrew \"hevel,\" meaning \"breath\" or \"vapor,\" and was rendered in the Greek Septuagint as \"mataiotes,\" conveying futility. The opening word's philosophical tone reflects the transient nature of life.\n\n\\boxed{Ecclesiastes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":84}},"cost":6.288e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_656","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poet with this surname wrote about flowers that have \"forgotten\" their \"Eastern origin\" and become \"decent\" and \"reticent\" in America in a poem that describes them as \"False Blue, White, Purple.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrases identify the opening of Amy Lowell’s poem “Lilacs,” which personifies the flowers as having “forgotten” their “Eastern origin” and adopted the restrained demeanor (“decent,” “reticent”) of New England. Because the poet’s surname is therefore the required answer, the solution is Lowell.\n\n\\boxed{Lowell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":227,"output_tokens":70},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.04e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_573","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A man in this play who was interrupted while trying to kill an animal claims to have exterminated only half of a rat.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a specific, absurd exchange in which one character was interrupted mid-act while attempting to kill a vermin and therefore boasts only of a partial success. This exchange occurs in Harold Pinter’s The Birthday Party, during the menacing interrogation scene in which McCann is questioned by Goldberg about having been stopped while “exterminating” a rat. No other well-known modern play contains a matching line or incident.\n\nThus the play referenced by the question is The Birthday Party.\n\n\\boxed{The Birthday Party}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":101},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.15e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1016","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One poem in this collection exclaims, \"Eclaircise the myths Asiatic!\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted line is taken directly from the poem \"Passage to India,\" which first appeared in the 1871–72 expansion of Walt Whitman’s lifelong poetic project. That project is universally known by the title Leaves of Grass, the only collection in which the poem (and therefore the line) appears.  \n\nThus the collection referenced by the question is Leaves of Grass.\n\n\\boxed{Leaves of Grass (Whitman)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":197,"output_tokens":84},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.14e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1482","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poet from this country concluded a poem, \"I am light honed to a still point in the incandescent onrush, a  ne ash in the beast's sudden desiccation when the sun explodes.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given excerpt is the final line of the poem \"Equanimity\" by Les Murray (published in the collection *The People's Otherworld*). Murray was born in rural New South Wales and is universally identified as an Australian poet; the cosmic/apocalyptic imagery and Latinate diction are characteristic of his style.  \n\nThus the country referenced by the question is Australia.\n\n\\boxed{Australia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":77},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.25e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_169","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This novel enjoins the reader to consult a \"metaphysical professor\" should they \"ever be athirst in the great American desert,\" since \"as everyone knows, meditation and water are wedded forever.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted passage is from Herman Melville's *Moby-Dick*, where the narrator, Ishmael, reflects on the connection between water and contemplation. The novel famously suggests seeking a \"metaphysical professor\" in times of spiritual or existential thirst in the \"great American desert.\" This alludes to the deep philosophical themes interwoven with the sea and meditation in the book.\n\n\\boxed{Moby-Dick}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.533e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_180","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay by Rebecca West argues that people cannot be dissuaded from this practice when it seems to their advantage unless they have been brought up with \"common worship\" and \"folk songs.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The practice referred to in Rebecca West's essay is lying. She argues that only a shared cultural and moral foundation, such as \"common worship\" and \"folk songs,\" can dissuade people from lying when it seems advantageous.\n\n\\boxed{lying}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":52}},"cost":4.381e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_197","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In Epistemology of the Closet, Eve Sedgwick writes that this novel anticipates the discourse surrounding the AIDS epidemic by presenting a fantasy of life \"after the homosexual.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Eve Sedgwick argues in *Epistemology of the Closet* that Oscar Wilde’s *The Picture of Dorian Gray* anticipates the discourse of the AIDS epidemic by imagining a world where the homosexual exists as a spectral, posthumous figure. The novel’s portrayal of Dorian’s hidden life and moral decay reflects anxieties about sexuality and secrecy that later resurface in discussions of AIDS. Thus, the novel that best fits this description is *The Picture of Dorian Gray*.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{The Picture of Dorian Gray}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":113}},"cost":8.002e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_217","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This author's attempts to efface the \"realistic sources\" of his works represent an \"intent of undoing,\" according to Stanley Gontarski.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question points to a specific literary critic (Gontarski) and a characteristic creative practice (“effacing realistic sources” via an “intent of undoing”). Gontarski’s scholarship centers on Samuel Beckett; his analyses of the manuscripts repeatedly describe Beckett’s successive drafts as a systematic “undoing” that strips away biographical or realistic detail in favor of abstraction. No other major author is associated with this critical formulation.\n\nThus the author in question is Samuel Beckett.\n\n\\boxed{Samuel Beckett}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":98},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.08e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_509","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a series of lectures, this author argued that \"the king died and then the queen died\" is a story, while \"the king died and then the queen died of grief\" is a plot.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted distinction between \"story\" and \"plot\" originates in E. M. Forster's 1927 Clark Lectures at Trinity College, Cambridge (later published as *Aspects of the Novel*). Forster uses precisely those two sentences to illustrate how a plot introduces causality and motivation where a mere story records only sequence. No other major critic employs this exact, oft-cited illustration.\n\n\\boxed{E.M. Forster}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":221,"output_tokens":83},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.57e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_842","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This novel's narrator laments that \"whatever lithophanic eternities\" might be provided for him cannot erase his deeds, leaving him nothing but the \"very local palliative of articulate art.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted passage employs characteristically Nabokovian diction (“lithophanic eternities,” “local palliative of articulate art”) and is spoken by a posthumous narrator who reflects on the limits of both immortality and artistic consolation. These elements converge uniquely in the 1972 novel Transparent Things, whose ghostly narrator comments on the life and death of Hugh Person from beyond the grave. No other Nabokov work (Pale Fire, Lolita, Ada) contains this precise phrasing or narrative situation.\n\nThus the novel in question is Transparent Things.\n\n\\boxed{Transparent Things (Nabokov)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":119},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001031,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1307","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This essay argues that from the perspective of the intellect, sin has no essence, but is \"diminution or  less ,\" whereas the conscience feels sin \"as essence,\" viewing it as \"pravity or  bad .\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given text is a partial quotation (with two obvious blanks) describing a philosophical distinction between intellectual and moral views of sin. The first blank must be completed by the noun \"evil\" to yield the standard phrase \"diminution or less evil\"; the second blank must be completed by the suffix \"ness\" to yield the matching noun \"badness.\"  \n\nTogether the restored sentence is a recognizable paraphrase of a passage that appears in several 19th-century theological essays (most often attributed to Coleridge or Kierkegaard).  \n\nThus the single, most common answer expected by the prompt is the pair of missing words.\n\n\\boxed{evil; ness}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":225,"output_tokens":129},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001095,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1309","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an essay by Sigmund Freud, this state is characterized by a cleavage between the critical ego and the ego altered by identification, which is caused by the withdrawing of free libido from an object into the ego.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The state described in the question refers to melancholia, where Freud explains the internal conflict resulting from the loss of a loved object and the subsequent identification with it, leading to self-reproach and depression. The withdrawal of libido from the object into the ego causes a split between the critical agency (superego) and the degraded self-representation. This process is central to Freud's essay \"Mourning and Melancholia.\"\n\n\\boxed{melancholia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":122,"output_tokens":118}},"cost":8.666e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1727","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem by Richard Brautigan reimagines this event as a rape at the hands of \"an evil  sherman who was dressed in clothes that had no childhood.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific Richard Brautigan poem that figuratively recasts a historical turning point in terms of sexual violence perpetrated by an industrialized “evil Sherman.” The only event that aligns with both the military/industrial imagery of Sherman and the reference to machine-made “clothes that had no childhood” is the Industrial Revolution. Brautigan therefore uses the Civil-War general as a symbol of mechanized modernity to dramatize that revolution as a foundational act of violation.\n\n\\boxed{Ind. Rev. (Sherman's March)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":104},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.5e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1798","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Addressing this place, a character calls himself \"one who brings / A mind not to be changed by place or time,\" since \"The mind is its own place.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are spoken by Satan upon his arrival in Hell (Milton, Paradise Lost, Book I, lines 253–255). He is directly addressing the infernal realm itself, declaring that his unyielding mind renders external location irrelevant. Thus the place in question is simply Hell.\n\n\\boxed{Hell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":62},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.36e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_0","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The lines \"Rude, and ungrateful, though my country be,\" in Book 5 of this work alludes to Scipio Africanus as a reference to its author's multiple exiles.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines evoke an author's sense of mistreatment by an ungrateful homeland, a sentiment historically linked to Scipio Africanus (who withdrew into voluntary exile after political accusations in Rome). This motif aligns with the biography and poetic voice of Luís de Camões, who endured repeated banishments and overseas postings. Consequently the work containing the lines in its fifth book is Camões’s epic Os Lusíadas (The Lusiads).\n\n\\boxed{The Lusiads (Camões)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":219,"output_tokens":96},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.180000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_12","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel by this author, a character who thinks he has been poisoned when he is served locally-grown coffee is overthrown in a coup that anticipated a series of real-life coups in this author's home country later the same year.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a scene from Chinua Achebe’s 1966 novel *A Man of the People*, whose corrupt politician Chief Nanga (and the government he serves) is suddenly removed by a military coup. The book appeared in January 1966; Nigeria experienced its first real coup later that month and a second in July, making the fictional event an eerie anticipation of actual history. No other author–country–novel combination matches the coffee-poisoning detail, the coup motif, and the precise chronology.\n\n\\boxed{Chinua Achebe}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":226,"output_tokens":111},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010070000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_24","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A. C. Bradley argued that this character \"is denied the dignity of verse\" while performing a certain action.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"A.C. Bradley's *Shakespearean Tragedy* (1904) analyzes the linguistic texture of the plays and specifically notes that Lady Macbeth, during her sleepwalking scene (Act 5, Scene 1), is “denied the dignity of verse” and therefore speaks only in broken prose. This observation underscores Bradley’s broader point that Shakespeare reserves verse for moments of heightened tragic dignity and deliberately strips it away here to convey psychological disintegration. No other Shakespearean character matches the quoted phrase or the associated action.\n\n\\boxed{Lady Macbeth (sleepwalking)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":110},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.58e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_42","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Osip Mandelstam wrote that this play's cast of characters constituted an \"inexpressive and colorless rebus\" that illustrated its author's tendency to portray \"propinquity with its resultant unpleasantness.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Osip Mandelstam was referring to Anton Chekhov's play *The Seagull*, criticizing its ensemble cast as a \"rebus\" reflecting emotional proximity and discomfort. His comment highlights Chekhov's focus on interpersonal tensions and unfulfilled desires. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{The Seagull}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":5.4189999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_81","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem set \"at the fourth watch\" opens with the striking image of \"the mountains disgorging\" this object.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a classical Chinese poem (likely by Du Fu) whose setting is explicitly the fourth night-watch. In that poem the opening line deploys the vivid verb “disgorging” (吐) to describe mountains releasing the moon, an image that immediately establishes both the nocturnal hour and the poem’s visual drama. Hence the object in question is the moon.\n\n\\boxed{the moon}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.15e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_105","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The protagonist of a novel in this genre entertains fellow passengers on a stalled train by describing frogs being hunted en masse to supply restaurants with legs.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a specific embedded anecdote in William Gaddis’s novel *JR* (1975). That novel is a canonical exemplar of postmodern fiction, whose hallmarks include lengthy digressive monologues, nested narratives, and a refusal of conventional linearity—precisely the features that allow a stalled-train monologue about mass frog harvesting to appear. Hence the genre named by the clue is postmodernism (or postmodern fiction).\n\n\\boxed{postmodernism (fic./lit.)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.83e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_127","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The protagonist of a novel by this author is too absorbed in thinking about how Keats felt being spurned by Wordsworth to notice that assassins enter the restaurant he's in and gun down a man at the next table.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene occurs in *White Noise* by Don DeLillo, where the protagonist, Jack Gladney, is preoccupied with academic and existential thoughts, including those about literary figures like Keats and Wordsworth, while remaining oblivious to surrounding dangers.\n\nThis moment exemplifies the novel's themes of distraction, fear of death, and the disconnect between intellectualization and reality.\n\n\\boxed{Don DeLillo}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":84}},"cost":6.405e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_136","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One of these poems states that the \"burden of a great / sadness\" arises from realizing that \"what one is pursuing now / was / once / nearer, truer, and joined to us / with infinite tenderness.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The poem described is \"The Burden of a Great Sadness\" by W.S. Merwin, which reflects on loss and the distant memory of a once-intimate connection. The lines emphasize a longing for something that was once close and tender but has since become unattainable. The correct answer is the poem by W.S. Merwin that matches this theme and language.\n\n\\boxed{W.S. Merwin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.585e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_138","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This play's author wrote that he was resisting the \"bourgeois conception of the immutability of the soul\" by having its characters be \"somewhat lacking in ‘character.'\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The play being referred to is *The Good Person of Szechwan* by Bertolt Brecht. Brecht intentionally created characters who shift and change to challenge the traditional notion of fixed personality, aligning with his epic theatre principles. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{The Good Person of Szechwan}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":95,"output_tokens":64}},"cost":5.075e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_161","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Regina Gagnier's (\"GAHN-yay's\") book The Idylls of the Marketplace argues that this author's work functioned as a protest against the commodification of art through notions of utility.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Regina Gagnier's book *The Idylls of the Marketplace* discusses how Oscar Wilde's work served as a protest against the commodification of art and the imposition of utility on artistic expression. Wilde championed aestheticism and the idea of \"art for art's sake,\" directly opposing the notion that art must serve a practical purpose. \n\n\\boxed{Oscar Wilde}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":78}},"cost":5.9930000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_210","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character created by this author stops believing in the supernatural and hears the speech of birds as mere chatter, after which he blots a manuscript and is imprisoned in a crystal bottle.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described events match the plot of E.T.A. Hoffmann’s 1814 novella “The Golden Pot,” whose protagonist Anselmus first loses faith in the supernatural realm, ceases to understand the language of birds, ruins a magical manuscript by blotting it, and is consequently confined inside a glass bottle by the hostile forces opposing Archivarius Lindhorst.  \nNo other well-known author’s work unites precisely these four motifs (loss of supernatural belief, avian speech reduced to chatter, blotting a manuscript, and crystalline imprisonment).  \nHence the author in question is E.T.A. Hoffmann.\n\n\\boxed{E.T.A. Hoffmann}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":122},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010400000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_228","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The cover of Sherry Turkle's book Reclaiming Conversation is a reference to an anecdote in this other book, in which the narrator writes that he and his friend \"gradually shoved our chairs farther apart till they touched the wall in opposite corners.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anecdote describes two people who, lacking anything to talk about, physically retreat from one another until they occupy opposite corners of a room. This scene appears in Jerry Mander’s *Four Arguments for the Elimination of Television* (Argument 2), where Mander uses it to illustrate how the absence of a mediating screen leaves people unable to sustain ordinary face-to-face contact. Turkle’s cover design—two empty chairs pushed against opposite walls—visually cites that passage, thereby linking her argument about digital devices to Mander’s earlier critique of television.\n\n\\boxed{Four Arguments for the Elimination of Television (Mander)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":228,"output_tokens":124},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001076,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_237","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem compares one of these animals to a person who is \"part woman, three parts a child,\" because she scans to ensure \"that nobody looks\" when her \"feet / Practice a tinker shuffle / Picked up on a street.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are from Paul Muldoon's poem \"Hedgehog,\" which explicitly likens the animal's furtive gait and wariness to \"a person / Who is part woman, three parts a child.\" The reference to a \"tinker shuffle\" further evokes the traditional association between tinkers/gypsies and hedgehogs (which they were known to hunt and cook). No other common animal fits the quoted description.\n\n\\boxed{hedgehogs}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":229,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.23e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_300","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A speech in this novel claims that although \"no code\" mattered to a poor woman \"before she broke it,\" it \"came crashing down on her afterwards.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted speech concerns the harsh social/sexual double standard applied to a poor unmarried woman (Lena Grove) after she becomes pregnant. This reflection occurs in William Faulkner’s 1932 novel *Light in August*, whose narrator explicitly uses those terms while describing how “no code mattered” to Lena until after she had broken it. No other Faulkner novel (or any other canonical work) contains this precise formulation.\n\n\\boxed{Light in August (Faulkner)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.89e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_321","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The protagonist of this novel kills a man and then pretends to be him to have sex with his lover, this novel's antagonist.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel described is *The Talented Mr. Ripley* by Patricia Highsmith. The protagonist, Tom Ripley, murders Dickie Greenleaf and assumes his identity, eventually engaging with Dickie’s lover, Marge, though not directly having sex with her as part of the deception. The antagonist can be interpreted as Dickie himself or the truth that Tom tries to escape.\n\n\\boxed{The Talented Mr. Ripley}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":89}},"cost":6.458e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_337","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At the end of this novel, its protagonist finally begins finishing his dissertation on his ancestor, after he abandoned it during a long period he calls his \"Great Sleep.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches the plot of Robert Penn Warren's *All the King's Men*. Its narrator-protagonist Jack Burden explicitly labels his years-long withdrawal from life and from his historical research as the \"Great Sleep\"; that research is a never-finished dissertation on his ancestor Cass Mastern. The novel's closing pages show Burden returning to the project, now ready to complete it.\n\n\\boxed{All the King's Men}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":82},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.36e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_343","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem by a writer of this ethnicity asks \"Why are the senators sitting there without legislating?\" and ends by calling the title group of people \"a kind of solution.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are from C.P. Cavafy’s poem “Waiting for the Barbarians,” which ends by describing the barbarians as “a kind of solution.” Cavafy was born in Alexandria to Greek parents and is universally classified as a Greek poet writing in modern Greek. The ethnicity referenced by the clue is therefore Greek.\n\n\\boxed{Greek}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":72},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.9e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_403","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A main character of a play named for one of these events takes a garland of orange blossoms from her maid and throws them on the floor.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue references Lorca's *Blood Wedding* (*Bodas de sangre*), whose title names one of the events in question. In Act Two the Bride receives a crown of orange blossoms (a traditional wedding symbol) from her servant and angrily casts it to the floor, foreshadowing the violent disruption of the marriage. The same pattern of clues therefore identifies the events as weddings.\n\n\\boxed{weddings (Blood Wedding)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":84},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.38e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_408","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character in this novel physically skips from side to side whilst delivering convoluted explanations, and is said to be able to turn black into white.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific character trait (physical skipping during convoluted speeches, combined with a talent for rhetorical inversion or sophistry) that matches Koroviev/Fagott in Bulgakov’s The Master and Margarita. Koroviev is repeatedly shown skipping or dancing sideways while delivering deliberately tangled explanations, and the novel’s other characters explicitly note his ability to “turn black into white.” No other widely read novel contains a figure whose mannerisms and reputation align so precisely with the given details.\n\n\\boxed{The Master and Margarita}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.31e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_412","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one part of this poem, the protagonist has to climb aboard a creature with the wings of a dragon, the tail of a scorpion, and a face of an honest man in order to scale a cliff.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches the creature Geryon from Dante's *Inferno*, which has a human face, a dragon-like body, and a scorpion's tail. The protagonist, Dante, rides Geryon to descend from the upper to the lower circles of Hell. This creature symbolizes fraud, aligning with its deceptive human face and monstrous form.\n\n\\boxed{Geryon}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":6.066e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_431","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The main character of this novel persuades her husband to buy a skinny yellow mule by comparing the sale to the Emancipation Proclamation.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel described is *Their Eyes Were Watching God* by Zora Neale Hurston. The main character, Janie Crawford, persuades her husband, Joe Starks, to buy the mule as an act of compassion, likening its liberation to the Emancipation Proclamation. This moment symbolizes freedom and empathy in the story.\n\n\\boxed{Their Eyes Were Watching God}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":88,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.884e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_445","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an allusion to the Book of Isaiah, this author described a \"cup of trembling\" that glowed and shook as the title character plays the piano at the end of a short story.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The reference is to the closing scene of James Baldwin’s “Sonny’s Blues,” in which the narrator watches his brother perform and recognizes “the cup of trembling” (Isaiah 51:17–22) “glowing and shaking above my brother’s head.” The phrase is Baldwin’s explicit biblical allusion to the burden of suffering Sonny bears through his music. No other well-known short-story writer matches this precise combination of allusion, piano performance, and title character.\n\n\\boxed{James Baldwin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":99},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.290000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_451","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay by Edmund Wilson asks why people read stories that focus on these kinds of people.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to Edmund Wilson's well-known 1944 essay \"Why Do People Read Detective Stories?\", which analyzes the appeal of the mystery/detective genre. Such stories center on detectives (or, secondarily, the criminals they pursue) as the focal characters. This matches the phrasing about \"these kinds of people\" exactly, with no other Wilson essay fitting the description.\n\n\\boxed{detectives}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":198,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.01e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_479","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This story's protagonist pictures \"a young man under a dripping tree\" and realizes he is imagining shades of the afterlife.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The story described is \"The Dead\" by James Joyce, in which the protagonist, Gabriel Conroy, has a moment of epiphany where he envisions a young man under a tree, symbolizing a connection to the transient nature of life and the afterlife. This image reflects themes of memory, death, and spiritual isolation. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{The Dead}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.832e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_491","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The appendix of a book in this genre includes a poem satirizing \"pious priests\" who attack \"Jack and Nell\" in the name of \"heavenly union.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query refers to Frederick Douglass's 1845 autobiography (full title: Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave). Its appendix reprints a Whittier parody that mocks pro-slavery clergymen for invoking “heavenly union” while abusing enslaved people named Jack and Nell. Works of this type constitute the genre known as the slave narrative.\n\n\\boxed{slave narrative}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":76},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.08e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_542","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a play, one of these objects gradually loses its resemblance to Pan and becomes more Mephistophelean over the span of 14 years.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query is a quizbowl-style clue whose referent is established by two linked details: a dramatic work in which a single artwork ages and moralizes across a fixed interval, and the specific iconographic shift “Pan → Mephistopheles.” Both map directly onto the portrait in theatrical adaptations of Wilde’s *The Picture of Dorian Gray*; the novel’s 18-year span is routinely compressed to 14 years on stage, and the text’s description of the sitter’s hair “los[ing] its resemblance to Pan” and becoming “more like Mephistopheles” is retained verbatim. Consequently the class of objects named by the clue is portraits (or pictures).\n\n\\boxed{portraits (of Dorian Gray)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":145},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00011449999999999999,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_553","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character in this story describes how he always signs everything so he can hold up the crime to the punishment, see if they match, and determine whether he's been treated fairly.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted description matches a specific passage of dialogue in Alexandre Dumas's novel, in which the character Bertuccio explains his habit of signing his crimes so he can later compare them with any punishment received. This detail is unique to that work and does not appear in other well-known stories that treat similar themes of justice or imprisonment. Consequently, the story referenced by the query is The Count of Monte Cristo.\n\n\\boxed{The Count of Monte Cristo}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":87},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.65e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_559","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character is subjected to an older woman's interminable conversation about the contents of a new letter from her niece, which this character escapes having read aloud to her.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a scene of social tedium in a classic English novel: an older, garrulous woman (Miss Bates) has just received a letter from her absent niece (Jane Fairfax) and launches into a rambling oral summary of its contents. The protagonist repeatedly tries to cut the visit short in order to avoid the even longer ordeal of having the letter read aloud verbatim.  \n\nThat scene occurs in Jane Austen’s Emma (chapter 27 and several subsequent visits to the Bates apartment); the only character who fits every detail is therefore the novel’s titular heroine.  \n\nNo other Austen novel, nor any other well-known work of the period, contains a matching incident.\n\n\\boxed{Emma Woodhouse}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":140},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00011219999999999999,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_567","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one of these works, the title character unwittingly convinces thieving servants of his clairvoyance after he follows sarcastic instructions to buy an alphabet book with a picture of a rooster in it.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene occurs in *The Confusions of Young Törless* by Robert Musil, where the protagonist's ambiguous behavior leads others to misinterpret his actions. However, the specific detail about the rooster and the alphabet book is not consistent with that work. The correct answer is *The Good Soldier Švejk* by Jaroslav Hašek, where the title character's apparent naivety and literal interpretation of sarcasm lead to such comedic misunderstandings.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{The Good Soldier Švejk}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":111}},"cost":7.986e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_590","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An author from this movement contrasted phrases like \"white napkin\" and \"daylight\" or \"the world\" and \"a purse\" in a text that praises the novels of Matthew Lewis.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described textual analysis and praise of Matthew Lewis's novels point to the Romantic movement, as Lewis was a prominent Gothic novelist admired by Romantic authors. The contrast of imagery like \"white napkin\" and \"daylight\" aligns with Romantic emphasis on emotion, imagination, and symbolic language. The author in question is likely William Hazlitt, a key Romantic essayist, who wrote appreciatively of Lewis in his literary critiques.\n\n\\boxed{Romantic}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":94}},"cost":6.926999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_605","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After a writer was banned in this country, a fellow poet wrote the lines \"only the trees know the pain as they stand still erect… their branches dry like wire.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The lines refer to the exile of Russian poet Joseph Brodsky, who was banned and expelled from the Soviet Union in 1972. The quoted verse reflects the sorrow and silence surrounding his forced departure, symbolizing the repressive atmosphere under the Soviet regime. The country in question is the Soviet Union.\n\n\\boxed{Soviet Union}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.469e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_628","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem about one of these animals describes how it \"drops the silver chain of sound\" after it \"rises and begins to round.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are taken directly from George Meredith’s 1881 poem “The Lark Ascending,” which opens with the skylark’s characteristic spiraling flight and continuous song. Because the poem is explicitly about this bird, the animals referenced by the clue are larks (most commonly skylarks).  \n\n\\boxed{skylarks (larks)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":70},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.66e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_659","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Insisting on the centrality of \"intellectual freedom,\" this essay cites at length Arthur Quiller-Couch's argument that \"the poor poet has not in these days, nor has had for two hundred years, a dog's chance.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted passage matches Virginia Woolf's extended discussion of the material and social conditions needed for literary creation. In the relevant section of the text, Woolf explicitly invokes \"intellectual freedom\" as a prerequisite for poetry and then reproduces Quiller-Couch's remark about the \"poor poet\" at length. No other well-known essay of the period centers this precise citation in the service of that argument.\n\nThus the work in question is Woolf's *A Room of One's Own*.\n\n\\boxed{A Room of One's Own (Woolf)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":226,"output_tokens":107},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.87e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_681","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Virginia Woolf highlighted a symbolic passage in this novel in which a plain earthenware pot allows the reader to see into the human soul.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question points to a specific observation by Virginia Woolf about the power of an everyday object in fiction. In her 1919 essay on Daniel Defoe, Woolf singles out the famous scene in which Robinson Crusoe painstakingly fashions a simple earthenware pot; she treats the pot as a transparent window onto the castaway’s inner life, revealing the elemental human need for order, security, and self-sufficiency. No other novel matches this precise conjunction of Woolf’s commentary and the symbolic vessel she describes.\n\nThus the novel in question is Robinson Crusoe.\n\n\\boxed{Robinson Crusoe}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":117},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.970000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_728","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem dedicated to his mother, this author described a felled chestnut tree as \"a soul ramifying and forever / silent, beyond silence listened for.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines come from the sonnet sequence “Clearances,” which Heaney explicitly dedicated to his mother; the felled chestnut is the central image of the fifth sonnet. No other modern poet matches both the biographical dedication and the precise wording.  \n\nThus the author in question is Seamus Heaney.\n\n\\boxed{Seamus Heaney}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":69},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.69e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_763","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this poem, one of the speakers recounts how she was beaten and stripped of her mantle by the \"sentinels of the walls\" making their rounds in the city.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches a specific passage in the Song of Songs (also called the Song of Solomon or Canticles), in which the female speaker recounts being assaulted by the city watchmen after searching for her lover at night (5:7). This erotic poem, traditionally attributed to Solomon and forming part of the Hebrew Bible/Old Testament, is the only text that contains these precise narrative details. No other well-known poem reproduces the same episode with the sentinels “of the walls.”\n\n\\boxed{Song of Songs (5:7)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":107},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.65e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_771","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character fondly recalls \"the triumphant conviction of strength, the heat of life in the handful of dust\" associated with the title attribute in a short story that concludes with his first vision of the East.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a narrator who reflects on the power of youth (the \"title attribute\") while aboard the doomed ship Judea. This matches the plot and closing image of Joseph Conrad’s short story “Youth,” whose first-person narrator is the recurring character Marlow. The quoted phrase appears verbatim in Marlow’s reminiscence, confirming the identification.\n\n\\boxed{Marlow}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":74},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.099999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_780","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One of these people becomes a hero by saving a boy from drowning in a novel in which these people are impressed by a man's ability to shoot pheasants on the wing.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The people referred to are the Russian aristocracy in Leo Tolstoy's novel *Anna Karenina*. In the novel, Count Vronsky impresses them with his skill in shooting pheasants on the wing, and later, Konstantin Levin becomes a hero by saving a peasant boy from drowning. The correct answer is the Russian aristocracy.\n\n\\boxed{Russian aristocracy}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.988e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_811","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an essay, this author wondered at the pre-modern \"dry truthfulness\" with which Homer recorded the unequal value of the arms exchanged by Diomedes and Glaucus.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific literary-critical reference to a scene from the Iliad (book 6) in which Homer matter-of-factly narrates Glaucus’s exchange of gold armor for Diomedes’s bronze. That reference occurs in the opening chapter of Erich Auerbach’s Mimesis, which explicitly praises Homer’s “dry truthfulness” (or “trockene Sachlichkeit”) as an emblem of the ancient style’s externalized, non-hierarchical realism. No other major essayist employs this precise phrasing or focuses on this detail in the same way.\n\nThus the author in question is Erich Auerbach.\n\n\\boxed{Erich Auerbach}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":131},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010850000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_829","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem addressed to this type of person notes that \"A honey tongue, a heart of gall, Is fancy's spring, but sorrow's fall.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines come from Sir Walter Raleigh’s “The Nymph’s Reply to the Shepherd,” a direct rejoinder to Marlowe’s “The Passionate Shepherd to His Love.” In that poem the nymph systematically dismantles the shepherd’s idyllic promises, declaring that “A honey tongue, a heart of gall / Is fancy’s spring, but sorrow’s fall.” The addressee is therefore explicitly a shepherd.\n\n\\boxed{shepherd}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.6e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_833","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character in this novel is beaten to demonstrate that \"definitions belonged to the definers—not the defined.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel being referenced is *Beloved* by Toni Morrison, in which Sethe is brutally beaten to assert control and power over her identity. This act symbolizes how those in power (the \"definers\") impose definitions and ownership on others (the \"defined\"). The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{Beloved}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":80,"output_tokens":66}},"cost":5e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_881","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After browsing a library containing Paracelsus and Roger Bacon, a woman in this story weeps against the cover of a journal recording how all the projects of a man's life have fallen short of their aims.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene matches a pivotal moment in Nathaniel Hawthorne’s short story “The Birth-Mark.” Georgiana, while alone in Aylmer’s laboratory, examines his folio of alchemical writings (explicitly naming Paracelsus, Cornelius Agrippa, and Roger Bacon) and then discovers his private journal, whose entries record a lifetime of failed experiments; she weeps over the volume’s cover in recognition of her husband’s thwarted ambitions. No other Hawthorne tale or contemporaneous work reproduces this precise sequence of library, journal, and weeping.\n\n\\boxed{The Birth-Mark (Hawthorne)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":222,"output_tokens":115},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010190000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_885","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The protagonist of a novel titled for this entity is forced to pose for visitors at the Museum of Natural Wonders and is pursued by Ridgeway.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes key plot elements from Colson Whitehead's 2016 novel *The Underground Railroad*, whose protagonist Cora is exhibited in dioramas at a South Carolina museum (called the Museum of Natural Wonders in the text) and relentlessly hunted by the slave-catcher Ridgeway. The novel's title is therefore the entity referenced by the question. No other well-known work matches both the museum scene and the pursuer's name.\n\n\\boxed{Underground Railroad}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.81e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_891","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The brother of the title character of Roddy Doyle's novel  Paddy Clarke Ha Ha Ha  is nicknamed for this character, whose daughter Yasmin falls in love with an American called \"Somebody\" in a John Barth novel.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The nickname refers to \"Sinbad,\" the brother of Paddy Clarke in Roddy Doyle's novel. Sinbad is also the name of the sailor from the Arabian Nights, whose daughter Yasmin appears in John Barth's *The Last Voyage of Somebody the Sailor*, where she falls in love with the protagonist, \"Somebody.\" The correct and most common answer is:\n\n\\boxed{Sinbad}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":81}},"cost":6.238000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_898","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character suggests that every man \"see to the restoration of his own land\" in a refusal to join men with \"sheets and hoods and night-galloping horses.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The character described is Atticus Finch from Harper Lee's *To Kill a Mockingbird*. He refuses to join the Ku Klux Klan and advocates for individual moral responsibility, urging each man to \"see to the restoration of his own land\" rather than engage in secretive, hateful acts. This moment reflects his commitment to justice and personal integrity.\n\n\\boxed{Atticus Finch}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":5.842e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_906","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The opening two words \"Smart lad\" in this poem's third quatrain are both stressed, breaking the otherwise iambic tetrameter of the poem.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement is a specific identifying clue about a well-known lyric poem written in quatrains. The only poem that matches the metrical detail (regular iambic tetrameter interrupted by the spondaic “Smart lad” at the start of the third stanza) is A. E. Housman’s “To an Athlete Dying Young.”\n\nThus the answer required by the query is the title (or author + title) of that poem.\n\n\\boxed{To an Athlete Dying Young (Housman)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.41e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_920","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character makes a \"great and edifying entertainment\" out of the adventures of his demon in a Kafka parable revealing the \"Truth about\" this character.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to a Kafka parable titled \"The Truth about Sancho Panza,\" in which Kafka reimagines Don Quixote's squire, Sancho Panza, as the wise figure who deliberately employs Don Quixote (the demon) as a form of \"great and edifying entertainment.\" In this reinterpretation, Sancho Panza is not the fool but the enlightened one who uses the adventures to maintain spiritual balance. The character in question is thus Sancho Panza.\n\n\\boxed{Sancho Panza}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":108}},"cost":7.663e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1042","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: his character describes how Maurilians prefer visitors to praise postcards of their former scenery over their current, and how Eusapians pose their dead in an identical underground metropolis.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given description matches two specific fictional cities (Maurilia and Eusapia) from Italo Calvino's 1972 novel *Invisible Cities*, in which the character Marco Polo catalogs dozens of imagined locales to Kublai Khan. No other well-known literary work contains these precise vignettes about postcards of a former cityscape or an identical subterranean necropolis.  \n\nThus the question is answered by naming the novel (or its author).  \n\n\\boxed{Invisible Cities (Calvino)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":96},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.080000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1055","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In Jamaica Kincaid's  Lucy , the title character dreams of being trampled by this poem's subjects after being forced to recite it.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel *Lucy* (1990) draws repeatedly on postcolonial themes of imposed British cultural norms. Early in the narrative, the protagonist is made to memorize and recite Wordsworth’s “I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud,” whose central image is a field of daffodils. She later recounts a nightmare in which those same daffodils trample her—an oneiric reversal of the colonial authority the poem represents.\n\nThus the poem in question is Wordsworth’s “Daffodils.”\n\n\\boxed{Daffodils (Wordsworth)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":110},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.66e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1074","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Herbie Hancock played the piano on a \"rhapsody\" named after this character, which appears on an album named for him by Chick Corea.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a 1978 Chick Corea jazz fusion album whose title and thematic inspiration derive from a well-known fictional character; that album contains the track “Rhapsody for ___.” Corea’s sideman list for the project includes Herbie Hancock on acoustic piano, confirming the personnel detail. The only character that satisfies every element of the clue is therefore the Mad Hatter.\n\n\\boxed{Mad Hatter}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":83},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.35e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1098","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The italicized ending of a poem about one of these places describes a youth who \"gain'd from Heav'n\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue is an excerpt from a quizbowl-style tossup on Thomas Gray's \"Elegy Written in a Country Church-Yard.\" Its final section (\"The Epitaph\") is conventionally printed in italics and contains the exact quoted line about the \"youth\" who \"gain'd from Heav'n … a friend.\" The poem's setting is therefore the category being defined.\n\n\\boxed{churchyards (graveyards)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":85},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.33e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1122","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Eliot Weinberger and Octavio Paz collaborated on a book titled for  19 Ways of Looking at  a poet from this country, analyzing translations of a poem that describes sunlight playing over green moss on a solitary mountain.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The poem described is \"A View of the Mountain Pass\" by Chinese poet Wang Wei, which has been translated and analyzed in various forms. Eliot Weinberger's book *19 Ways of Looking at Wang Wei* examines multiple translations of this poem, originally written in Chinese. The country referred to is China.\n\n\\boxed{China}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.518e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1170","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this novel, the protagonist's love interest and his boyhood crush Pribislav are linked by their \"Kirghiz eyes\" and by scenes in which they lend him pencils.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The novel being referenced is *The Magic Mountain* by Thomas Mann, in which the protagonist, Hans Castorp, associates his love interest Clawdia Chauchat and his boyhood crush Pribislav Hippe with \"Kirghiz eyes\" and pencil-lending scenes, symbolizing exotic allure and personal transformation. These recurring motifs emphasize thematic connections between memory, desire, and identity.  \n\n\\boxed{The Magic Mountain}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.481e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1194","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character's home is suggested to be the \"Great Good Place\" and has \"fairy-tale\" like qualities according to W. H. Auden's essay \"Brothers and Others,\" which argues that this woman appears in an \"Unpleasant Play.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The character being referred to is Hermione from William Shakespeare's *The Winter's Tale*. W. H. Auden describes her symbolic return in the \"Great Good Place,\" a fairy-tale realm, contrasting the play's earlier tragic tone. The \"Unpleasant Play\" Auden references is *The Winter's Tale*, where Hermione's miraculous reappearance gives the play its redemptive, fairy-tale quality.\n\n\\boxed{Hermione}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":112,"output_tokens":90}},"cost":6.856e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1205","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A scene involving one of these objects concludes with the image of a candle used to read a book filled with \"grief and evil\" flaring up then going out forever.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a literary/filmic scene whose central prop is a sealed, transparent sphere containing a miniature wintry scene; when the sphere is dropped or overturned at the moment of a character's death, the swirling flakes visually echo the extinguishing of the candle that had illuminated a volume explicitly labeled as containing “grief and evil.”  \n\nThis combination of imagery (miniature weather, sudden darkness, mortality) is classically realized by the snow-globe that falls from Charles Foster Kane’s hand in the opening of Citizen Kane, a motif later echoed in other works that likewise pair the object with a dying reader.  \n\nHence the referent of “these objects” is snow globes.\n\n\\boxed{snow globes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":142},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.000114,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1210","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem by this author that opposes the palindromic terms amor and Roma, Tarpeia begs for a retributive \"Rape of the Roman Women.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific elegy (Propertius 4.4) whose central conceit is the anagrammatic opposition of *amor* and *Roma*. In that poem Tarpeia, having betrayed the city for love of the Sabine king Tatius, explicitly calls for a retaliatory “rape of the Roman women” that would invert the earlier Sabine episode. No other ancient poet treats the Tarpeia myth in precisely these terms.\n\nThus the author in question is Propertius.\n\n\\boxed{Propertius}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":104},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.46e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1215","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Margaret Ezell challenged the historical assumptions perpetuated by this character's story in her essay on \"The Myth of [this character].\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Margaret Ezell is a historian of early modern women's writing whose scholarship frequently critiques anachronistic or overly schematic narratives of literary history. One of her best-known interventions is the 1990 essay \"The Myth of Judith Shakespeare,\" which directly attacks the influential parable Virginia Woolf invented in *A Room of One's Own*. Ezell shows how Woolf's tragic fiction of Shakespeare's gifted but doomed sister had been taken as empirical fact by later feminist critics, thereby distorting the actual archival record of women's literary production.\n\nThe character in question is therefore Judith Shakespeare.\n\n\\boxed{Judith Shakespeare}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":116},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.9e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1225","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem set at this location, the speaker hears \"peals of distant ironical laughter at every word I have written\" and describes himself as \"ba  led, balk'd, bent to the very earth\" in his awareness that \"before all my arrogant poems the real Me stands yet untouch'd, untold, altogether unreach'd.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are taken verbatim from Walt Whitman’s “As I Ebb’d with the Ocean of Life” (1860). That poem is explicitly situated “at the shores of Paumanok,” Whitman’s recurrent name for Long Island; the speaker addresses the surf, the wind, and the “fish-shaped” island itself while confronting the gap between his written poems and the “real Me.” Consequently the location demanded by the question is Paumanok.\n\n\\boxed{Paumanok (LI shore)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":250,"output_tokens":100},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1235","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character addresses the \"dark Hindoo half of nature\" as he watches an animal turn to \"seek intercedings with yon all-quickening sun\" before it dies.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted language is taken directly from Herman Melville’s *Moby-Dick* (chapter 118, “The Quadrant”). In that scene Captain Ahab, standing on the deck, watches a harpooned whale roll toward the sun in its death throes and apostrophizes the “dark Hindoo half of nature,” thereby revealing both his Promethean defiance and his awareness of cosmic duality. No other character in the novel (or in any other well-known work containing similar imagery) speaks these lines.\n\n\\boxed{Capt. Ahab (Moby-Dick)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":216,"output_tokens":116},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001012,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1253","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This poem's author punned on the word for \"meaning\" by adding an extra n to the last word of the phrase \"unser dunkeles Sinngrün\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue describes a distinctive German neologism that puns on “Sinn” (meaning) by fusing an extra “n” into the compound “Sinngrün,” thereby evoking both semantic depth and the evergreen plant name. That exact formation appears at the close of Paul Celan’s 1958 poem “Engführung,” whose final lines read “unser / dunkeles / Sinngrün.” No other well-known German-language poem employs this orthographic pun in precisely the cited phrase.\n\nThus the poem whose author is alluded to is “Engführung.”\n\n\\boxed{Engführung (Celan)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":216,"output_tokens":127},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010669999999999999,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1263","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem, one of these animals \"believes we may long for one more dream...before we come home to our strange bodies and rise from the dead.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given quote is a clue pointing to a specific animal (or category of animals) featured in a poem. The phrasing around \"strange bodies\" and \"rise from the dead\" is a clear allusion to metamorphosis after a long dormancy, which aligns with cicadas (nymphs emerging as winged adults after 13–17 years underground). This fits far better than alternatives such as dogs, butterflies, or mythical creatures.\n\n\\boxed{cicadas}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":92},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.86e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1283","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This poem's speaker calls on an \"old lecher with a love on every wind\" who has \"reckoned up every unforeknown, unseeing plunge...into the labyrinth of another's being.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrases are spoken by the aging poet-persona of W. B. Yeats’s “The Tower” (1928). In the poem’s second section the speaker explicitly names himself “an old lecher with a love on every wind” and then describes the erotic and imaginative risks he has taken as “every unforeknown, unseeing plunge … into the labyrinth of another’s being.”  \n\nThus the only poem that matches both the diction and the dramatic situation is Yeats’s “The Tower.”\n\n\\boxed{The Tower (Yeats)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":219,"output_tokens":107},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.73e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1326","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel by a later author, this writer is nearly crushed by a falling bed he had rigged to his ceiling, and is ridiculed by the protagonist for the mixed metaphors in a speech he delivers so quietly the audience can't hear him.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clues describe a cameo appearance by an earlier writer (as a character) in a later satirical novel. The inaudible speech full of mixed metaphors is a direct caricature of Colley Cibber’s notoriously inept birthday odes as Poet Laureate; the rigged collapsing bed is an additional comic humiliation invented for the same scene. Both incidents occur in Henry Fielding’s 1741 novel Shamela (a work that repeatedly lampoons Cibber’s Apology and his public persona).\n\nThus the writer in question is Colley Cibber.\n\n\\boxed{Colley Cibber}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":227,"output_tokens":114},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010240000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1349","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This text's \"ethnographic imagination\" is the subject of a book by Carol Dougherty that argues that the description of the creation of an object in this text is a metaphor for the creation of the text itself.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The text referred to is Homer's *Odyssey*, and Carol Dougherty's book explores how the weaving of Penelope is both a literal and metaphorical act tied to the composition of the epic itself. The \"ethnographic imagination\" connects the domestic craft of weaving to the poetic craft of storytelling. \n\n\\boxed{Odyssey}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.4789999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1398","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Frederick de Armas argues that a fanciful description of this character with two maids is an ekphrasis of Botticelli's Primavera.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query concerns a specific scholarly claim by Frederick de Armas (a specialist in Golden-Age ekphrasis) linking a literary scene to Botticelli’s Primavera. In that painting Venus stands attended by the three Graces; a parallel “one figure + two maids” grouping therefore points to the heroine of Cervantes’ Persiles, whose fanciful portrait is shown to be a deliberate verbal rendering of the same composition. Hence the character in question is Auristela.\n\n\\boxed{Auristela (Persiles)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":105},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.43e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1430","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: James and Jorndorf dispute Jansenist readings of this play, citing the lines \"You are in love.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue is a partial quizbowl-style tossup (common in academic competitions) whose lead-in references scholarly debates over religious interpretations of a French play. Jansenist readings most commonly attach to works by Molière (especially Tartuffe) or Racine; the quoted dialogue fragment and the scholars named align with the former. Hence the play being asked for is Tartuffe.\n\n\\boxed{Tartuffe (Molière)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":202,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.44e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1461","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the introduction to the story this character appears in, the narrator discusses confusions about this character's name and origins, as well as what to title his story, as the four difficulties he faced in writing it.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a narrator who opens by enumerating four compositional obstacles—uncertainty over the protagonist’s baptismal name, uncertainty over his parentage and birth, uncertainty over an appropriate title, and one further meta-difficulty—before proceeding to tell the life story. These exact preoccupations appear at the outset of Laurence Sterne’s novel, whose narrator-protagonist is named Tristram Shandy. No other eighteenth-century fictional autobiography matches the enumerated difficulties so precisely.\n\nThus the character in question is Tristram Shandy.\n\n\\boxed{Tristram Shandy}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":222,"output_tokens":114},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001014,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1479","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay by this author contrasts Titian's skin tones with Van Dyck's, arguing that the latter lack the title property, which is the \"power or passion de ning any object.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The essay in question is by John Ruskin, who contrasts Titian's rich, passionate use of color with Van Dyck's more restrained approach, arguing that Van Dyck's work lacks \"vitality\" or \"power,\" which Ruskin considers essential. This aligns with Ruskin's broader aesthetic philosophy emphasizing emotional and spiritual depth in art. The correct answer is John Ruskin.\n\n\\boxed{John Ruskin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.481e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1485","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This writer criticized the settings of another writer's poems as the products of \"a cook's boy helping the gloomier dwarfs boil toads and snails\" in a series of lectures that begins by asking listeners to \"imagine a man on an island\" who 3nds a box containing all of W. H. Auden's works.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a critic/lecturer who opens a poetry series by positing a Robinson-Crusoe figure discovering a cache of Auden’s poems, then uses that framework to evaluate other poets. The distinctive “cook’s-boy … boil toads and snails” remark is a characteristic Jarrell witticism aimed at the Gothic settings of Poe (and, by extension, certain mid-century imitators). Jarrell’s published Oxford and Princeton lectures of the 1950s match both the opening scenario and the quoted criticism exactly; no other mid-century poet-critic (Berryman, Hecht, etc.) fits the details.\n\n\\boxed{Randall Jarrell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":245,"output_tokens":134},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.000116,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1489","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In Cultural Capital, John Guillory related this poem's \"refunctioning of the commonplace book\" to its subsequent linguistic capital as a means of inculcating literacy in schools.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question points to a specific argument in John Guillory’s *Cultural Capital* (1993), which analyzes how certain canonical texts acquired institutional value as instruments of linguistic pedagogy. Guillory identifies T. S. Eliot’s *The Waste Land* as a modernist “refunctioning of the commonplace book”: its collage of quotations, fragments, and annotations converts the older manuscript practice into a teachable archive of literary English. This formal feature, Guillory shows, supplied the poem with durable “linguistic capital,” allowing it to be installed in school and university syllabi as a privileged vehicle for the inculcation of literacy and cultural literacy alike.\n\nThus the poem in question is *The Waste Land*.\n\n\\boxed{The Waste Land (Eliot)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":216,"output_tokens":149},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001177,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1500","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When this character is told \"there is no such thing as abstract morality,\" he decides to live his life \"with due regard to the policeman round the corner.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted dialogue and resulting life philosophy match a key scene late in Samuel Butler’s semi-autobiographical novel *The Way of All Flesh*. After Ernest Pontifex is released from prison and begins to shed his earlier religious scruples, a worldly mentor tells him there is “no such thing as abstract morality”; Ernest immediately adopts the pragmatic rule of simply staying one step ahead of “the policeman round the corner.” No other major literary character is associated with this precise exchange.\n\n\\boxed{Ernest Pontifex (Way of All Flesh)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":109},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.69e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1518","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Franz Kafka wrote in a short parable that the \"truth about\" this man was that he exorcised his demons by telling his stories to another man, whom he joined on \"crusades\" that provided a \"great and edifying entertainment to the end of his days.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted parable is Kafka's \"The Truth About Sancho Panza\" (1915/1917). Its text states that Sancho Panza \"succeeded... by feeding him a great number of romances... in so diverting from him his demon, whom he later called Don Quixote,\" then \"philosophically followed Don Quixote on his crusades\" for \"great and edifying entertainment to the end of his days.\" The phrasing matches the query exactly, identifying the referent as Sancho Panza (not Don Quixote).\n\n\\boxed{Sancho Panza}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":235,"output_tokens":117},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001055,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1542","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay about this country anecdotally de nes its archetype of the \"Ultimate Peasant\" before describing how changes in this country's culture affected the essayist Richard Cobb.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The essay in question is Richard Cobb's reflection on France, where he describes the \"Ultimate Peasant\" as a cultural archetype. Cobb, a British historian, was deeply influenced by French provincial life and how its evolving culture impacted his personal and academic perspectives. The country central to this discussion is France.\n\n\\boxed{France}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":67}},"cost":5.281e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1546","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Lisel Mueller wrote that, among postwar Germans who did this activity, she was \"a Briar Rose, a Rip Van Winkle, / a stranger to the beginnings / they make from the stench of evil.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote is drawn from Lisel Mueller’s poem “Curriculum Vitae,” in which the poet (who emigrated from Germany in 1939) describes her own belatedness and detachment. The lines contrast her Rip-Van-Winkle-like slumber with the work of the postwar German poets who were actively confronting and rebuilding language after the moral catastrophe of the Third Reich. Consequently the activity referenced in the question is writing poetry.\n\n\\boxed{writing poetry (postwar Ger. poets)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":223,"output_tokens":95},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.21e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1575","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In one of this author's poems, the speaker compares a \"catafalqued (KAT-ah-falk'd) king\" and a \"solitary man of war / among these frail vegetables\" to the title \"torpedo from the ocean\" that is \"navigating now / the waters of death.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrases match the imagery and diction of Pablo Neruda’s “Ode to a Large Tuna in the Market,” in which the fish is apostrophized as both a “catafalqued king” and a “solitary man of war / among these frail vegetables,” then likened to a “torpedo from the ocean” now “navigating now / the waters of death.” Neruda’s characteristic odes to humble market objects therefore identify the author in question.\n\n\\boxed{Pablo Neruda}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":243,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1581","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a reimagining of this poem, the speaker warns that \"hunger shall make thy modest zone / and cheat fond death of all but bone\" and offers the addressee \"peace and plenty, bed and board, / that chance employment may afford.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are a clear modern reworking of Marlowe’s pastoral invitation, replacing the original’s beds of roses and singing shepherds with a sober offer of “bed and board” while retaining the same structure of enticement-plus-warning. The distinctive phrase “modest zone” echoes the Elizabethan imagery of the coy mistress’s girdle, now threatened by hunger rather than time, confirming the source poem.  \n\nThus the poem being reimagined is Marlowe’s “The Passionate Shepherd to His Love.”\n\n\\boxed{The Passionate Shepherd to His Love (Marlowe)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":233,"output_tokens":115},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010410000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1592","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This man idolizes the \"intellectual and virginal manliness\" exempli⢄ed by St. Sebastian and lives by the motto \"Durchhalten,\" or \"persevere,\" which he borrowed from Frederick the Great.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches the protagonist of Thomas Mann's novella *Death in Venice* (1912). Mann explicitly characterizes Gustav Aschenbach as an ascetic writer who models himself on the \"intellectual and virginal manliness\" of St. Sebastian and who adopts Frederick the Great's motto \"Durchhalten\" as his personal ethic of endurance. No other figure in Mann's oeuvre or in the surrounding literary-historical context fits both the iconographic and the biographical details simultaneously.\n\n\\boxed{Gustav Aschenbach (Death in Venice)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":227,"output_tokens":107},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.89e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1596","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Because actions involving this substance and \"the Lip you press\" might \"end in the Nothing all Things end in,\" a poem suggests thinking that \"you are Today what Yesterday / you were\" and that \"tomorrow you shall not be less.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrases are taken verbatim from Edward FitzGerald’s translation of The Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám (stanza 43 in the first edition, stanza 47 in later versions). In context, the “substance” whose contact with “the Lip you press” is said to “end in the Nothing” is therefore the wine that the poem repeatedly urges the reader to enjoy.  \n\nThus the answer to the query is simply the substance named in the poem.\n\n\\boxed{wine (Rubaiyat)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":229,"output_tokens":106},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.88e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1610","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a play, a man with this job encourages people to memorize an anecdote about how 14 foreskins of Christ existed at the time of the Reformation; in that play, Dakin (DAY-kin) blackmails Felix Armstrong to help a man keep his job in this profession, but that man dies in an accident while riding his motorcycle with Irwin.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes key plot points from Alan Bennett's play *The History Boys*. Hector (a teacher of general studies) recounts the \"14 foreskins of Christ\" anecdote during one of his lessons, Dakin blackmails the headmaster to retain Hector's position, and Hector dies in the motorcycle crash with Irwin. Thus the profession referenced throughout is simply that of a teacher.\n\n\\boxed{teacher}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":252,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.090000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1627","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a novel from this country, a character suffers from \"delirium\" and imagines himself transforming into Thomas Aquinas's Summa Theologica; that novel by an author from this country is dedicated to the \" rst worm\" that gnawed on the narrator's corpse.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The literary clues match the novel *The Posthumous Memoirs of Brás Cubas* (1881). Its famous tongue-in-cheek dedication is addressed to “the worm who first gnawed on the cold flesh of my corpse,” and its celebrated “Delirium” chapter (ch. 7) depicts the dying narrator hallucinating that he has metamorphosed into a cosmic embodiment of scholastic knowledge, explicitly invoking Aquinas’s *Summa Theologica*. Both the author (Machado de Assis) and the setting therefore identify the country as Brazil.\n\n\\boxed{Brazil}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":234,"output_tokens":116},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001048,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1645","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One character created by this author listens to a broadcast about Soviet nuclear testing while agonizing over his decision to dilute the milk being fed to his infant son.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described scene is a specific flashback in Jonathan Franzen’s 2001 novel *The Corrections*. It occurs early in the book when a young Alfred Lambert, struggling financially, waters down the milk/formula he is feeding his infant son while a radio broadcast reports on Soviet nuclear tests. No other prominent author’s work contains this precise combination of details, confirming Franzen as the referent.\n\n\\boxed{Jonathan Franzen}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":85},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.45e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1653","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A four-line poem set during this decade discusses a group of men who should be with their sweethearts gathering the \"᥸owers le᥸ thick at nightfall in the wood.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a poem whose setting and subject matter (soldiers instead of lovers, collecting flowers in a wood) point to the First World War. The only decade that fits both the historical context and the quoted imagery is therefore the 1910s.  \n\n\\boxed{1910s}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":219,"output_tokens":59},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.33e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1734","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this play, a student exits with the sarcastic line \"goodnight, Prometheus,\" prompting another character to speculate that there's \"no such thing\" as \"Prometheus.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted exchange occurs near the end of Act 1 of Willy Russell’s two-hander Educating Rita. A departing pupil sarcastically addresses the alcoholic tutor Frank as “Prometheus,” after which the newly arrived, working-class Rita—still innocent of classical mythology—replies that she has never heard of any such person and wonders whether “there’s no such thing.” The moment neatly dramatizes the cultural gulf the play explores and is therefore the only work that fits the given dialogue.\n\n\\boxed{Educating Rita}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.43e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1735","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a poem addressed to this man, the speaker sees himself \"as one who sits ashore and longs . . . to visit dolphin-coral in deep seas\"; that sonnet to this man ends by invoking \"Dian (dy-ANN), Queen of Earth, and Heaven, and Hell\" and opens with the speaker \"standing aloof in giant ignorance.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted sonnet is Keats’s “To Homer,” which opens with the line “Standing aloof in giant ignorance,” compares the speaker to a shore-bound observer “long[ing] . . . to visit dolphin-coral in deep seas,” and closes by naming “Dian, Queen of Earth, and Heaven, and Hell.” The poem is therefore addressed directly to the blind Greek epic poet. No other figure satisfies all three textual markers simultaneously.\n\n\\boxed{Homer}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":251,"output_tokens":94},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.72e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1742","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem about this event asks \"can you then impute a sinful deed to babes who on their mothers' bosoms bleed?\" and states \"one day everything will be well, that is our hope.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The poem refers to the Massacre of the Innocents, in which King Herod ordered the killing of infant boys in Bethlehem. The quoted lines reflect on the innocence of the victims and the hope for divine justice. The event is commonly known as the **Massacre of the Innocents**.\n\n\\boxed{Massacre of the Innocents}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.4999999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1750","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A Herman Melville essay notes that this story's innocuous title is meant to deceive the \"super cial skimmer of pages,\" who fails to see that this story is \"deep as Dante.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query refers to a specific remark in Herman Melville’s 1850 review essay “Hawthorne and His Mosses.” In that essay Melville singles out Hawthorne’s tale “Young Goodman Brown,” observing that its bland, almost pastoral-sounding title is calculated to mislead “the superficial skimmer of pages,” while the story itself is in fact “as deep as Dante.”  \n\nNo other Hawthorne story is described by Melville in those exact terms, and the phrasing matches the query verbatim.  \n\nThus the story in question is “Young Goodman Brown.”\n\n\\boxed{Young Goodman Brown}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":218,"output_tokens":113},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1751","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This poem describes an entity that \"makes the willow shiver in the sun\" for maidens who \"taste / and stray impassioned in the littering leaves.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines point to a personified natural force acting upon trees and fallen foliage (with attendant imagery of maidens wandering outdoors). The only entity that literally produces the described motion—“making the willow shiver”—while the maidens move through “littering leaves” is the wind.  \n\nThus the poem’s subject is identified simply as the wind.\n\n\\boxed{the wind}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":76},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.04e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1780","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The speaker of a poem by this author asks \"whom dost thou worship in this lonely dark corner of a temple with doors all shut?\" and says that God is \"where the tiller is tilling the hard ground and where the pathmaker is breaking stones.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are from a poem by Rabindranath Tagore, reflecting his spiritual and humanistic themes. The speaker emphasizes that God is present in everyday labor and human effort, not in secluded worship. This aligns with Tagore's philosophical views expressed in works like *Gitanjali*.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Rabindranath Tagore}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":112,"output_tokens":75}},"cost":5.9560000000000006e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1784","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In David Luke's rhyming translation of this play, one character says \"I'm not a lady and I'm not sweet / I can get home on my own two feet\" in response to the protagonist asking to escort her from confession.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted lines are a direct match for David Luke’s verse rendering of the exchange between Faust and Margarete (Gretchen) immediately after she leaves the confessional in Goethe’s Faust, Part One. No other well-known play exists in a David Luke rhyming translation that contains this scene or these lines.  \n\nThus the work in question is Goethe’s Faust (Part One).\n\n\\boxed{Goethe's Faust (Pt. 1)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":226,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.97e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1787","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A man who breaks plates without touching them opens a factory that produces this stuff, then builds a railroad to improve its business.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The riddle describes the origin of a specific product and its associated business. The phrase \"breaks plates without touching them\" is a reference to the delicate nature of fine china (porcelain dishware), which can shatter from minor impacts or vibrations. The entrepreneur in question therefore opens a factory to manufacture porcelain and later invests in rail infrastructure to ship the fragile finished goods efficiently.\n\nThis yields the most common solution to the riddle.\n\n\\boxed{porcelain}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.549999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1795","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay argues that the \"artificial comedy\" of this century occurs in a \"utopia of gallantry\" unrelated to morality.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given clue matches the central argument of Charles Lamb's 1822 essay \"On the Artificial Comedy of the Last Century,\" in which he defends Restoration playwrights by claiming their work inhabits a self-contained \"Utopia of gallantry\" detached from everyday ethical judgment.  \n\nThe phrasing (\"artificial comedy,\" \"utopia of gallantry\") is taken verbatim from Lamb, making him the unique referent of the question.  \n\nThus the expected answer is the essay's author.\n\n\\boxed{Charles Lamb (On Artif. Comedy)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":107},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.47e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1803","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The protagonist of this novel mocks book-reading while arguing against predestination in a speech that repeats his claim to be a racially-pure \"man without a cross.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches a key scene from James Fenimore Cooper’s *The Last of the Mohicans* (1826). In Chapters 3–4, Natty Bumppo (Hawkeye) repeatedly calls himself “a man without a cross” while debating David Gamut about Calvinist predestination and dismissing the value of book-learning and psalmody. No other Leatherstocking Tale contains this precise combination of motifs.\n\nThus the novel in question is *The Last of the Mohicans*.\n\n\\boxed{The Last of the Mohicans}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":106},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.54e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1820","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character says that one of these objects \"irked me... it itched me\" after he and another man find one of them on the ground and silently swap three of them back and forth.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific scene from Samuel Beckett's play *Waiting for Godot*. Vladimir and Estragon discover Lucky's hat (along with their own), then silently pass the three hats back and forth while trying them on; one of them subsequently complains that a particular hat \"irked me... it itched me.\"  \n\nThis matches the given details exactly (three objects, silent swapping, explicit quotation) and is the most common referent for the phrasing in literary quiz contexts.  \n\n\\boxed{hats (Godot)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":104},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.6e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1833","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This character conceives of conversation as a \"served dessert\" of ripe fruits to be piled atop a silver plate signifying his wife's mind.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The description matches a memorable image from Henry James’s *The Portrait of a Lady*. In conversation with Isabel Archer, Gilbert Osmond explicitly figures her mind as a polished silver salver on which he will heap “ripe fruits” of cultivated talk, thereby reducing her intellect to a decorative receptacle for his own aesthetic pleasure. No other major literary character employs this precise domestic-metaphorical conceit.\n\n\\boxed{Gilbert Osmond (Portrait of a Lady)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":90},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.66e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1843","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a story titled for a man with this name, the narrator gives a brief outline of the plot, then gives the reader the option of meditating on it individually or reading the fuller account, trusting that there will be a moral \"done up neatly, and condensed into the 炤nal sentence.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific passage from Nathaniel Hawthorne's 1835 short story \"Wakefield,\" in which the narrator summarizes a newspaper anecdote about a man who abandons his wife for twenty years, then explicitly offers the reader the choice of meditating on the outline or continuing to the full narrative whose moral will be \"condensed into the final sentence.\"  \nThe story is therefore titled for its protagonist, making \"Wakefield\" the name in question.  \nNo other well-known tale matches the quoted phrasing or narrative device.\n\n\\boxed{Wakefield}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":240,"output_tokens":109},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001025,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1856","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this poem, a transition between two radically di鴁ferent refrains occurs with the line \"The ant's a centaur in his dragon world.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted line is the pivot in Ezra Pound’s Canto LXXXI (from The Pisan Cantos). Immediately before it the poem repeats the refrain “Tudor indeed is gone,” while immediately after it the new, antithetical refrain “Pull down thy vanity” begins and is reiterated for the remainder of the canto.  \n\nThus the poem that exhibits the described transition between refrains is Canto LXXXI.\n\n\\boxed{Canto LXXXI (Pound)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":91},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.79e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1879","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a short story, a young man tasked with picking out one of these beings among a group of maidens playing \"hawk and chickens\" is rewarded with a mysterious letter for his father the mayor.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific folkloric motif in which a youth must identify one supernatural maiden among many (here disguised as ordinary girls playing a children's game) and is then entrusted with a sealed letter. This matches the plot of multiple Slavic tales featuring rusalki (also spelled rusalky), water or forest spirits who appear as beautiful young women, lure or test mortal men, and sometimes deliver fateful messages to authority figures such as a town mayor. No other class of beings (swan-maidens, vilas, nixies, etc.) consistently combines the exact game, the selection test, and the letter-for-the-mayor reward in the same short-story tradition.\n\n\\boxed{rusalki (rusalky)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":146},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.000117,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Literature_1893","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this novel, a professor tells his students to \"let your thought be the wall,\" then \"let your thought be that that thought the wall.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted exercise in recursive, object-directed thinking is a phenomenological drill drawn from a minor character's backstory in David Foster Wallace's unfinished novel *The Pale King*. In that work a Jesuit instructor directs students first to make their awareness identical with a wall and then to make their awareness identical with the prior act of awareness itself. No other well-known novel contains this precise two-step formulation.\n\n\\boxed{The Pale King (DFW)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":84},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.4e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_0","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the capital of France?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The capital of France is Paris, a major European city known for its art, fashion, and culture. It is the largest city in France and serves as the country's political, economic, and cultural center. \n\n\\boxed{Paris}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":66,"output_tokens":48}},"cost":3.738e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_3","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which country has the largest population?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"India is currently the country with the largest population, having surpassed China in 2023 according to UN population estimates. While China held this distinction for decades, recent demographic data (including lower birth rates in China and sustained growth in India) confirm the shift. The most common correct answer today is therefore India.\n\n\\boxed{India}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":187,"output_tokens":66},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.04e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_4","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the largest island in the world?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The largest island in the world is Greenland. While Australia is larger, it is classified as a continental landmass rather than an island. Therefore, Greenland holds the title of the largest island.\n\n\\boxed{Greenland}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":68,"output_tokens":45}},"cost":3.5839999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_5","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which country is known for its pyramids?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The country most commonly known for its pyramids is Egypt, home to the famous Giza Pyramids, including the Great Pyramid of Khufu. These ancient structures were built as tombs for pharaohs and are among the most iconic landmarks in the world. While other countries like Mexico and Sudan also have pyramids, Egypt is the most widely recognized.\n\n\\boxed{Egypt}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":68,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":5.504e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_6","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which ocean is the largest in the world?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Pacific Ocean is the largest ocean in the world, covering more than 60 million square miles. It spans from the Arctic in the north to the Southern Ocean in the south and is bounded by Asia and the Americas. Its vast size makes it the largest and deepest of Earth's oceanic divisions.\n\n\\boxed{Pacific Ocean}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":68,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":4.963999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_7","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the capital of Brazil?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The capital of Brazil is Brasília. It was designated as the capital in 1960, replacing Rio de Janeiro, to promote development in the country's interior. The city was specifically designed and built for this purpose.\n\n\\boxed{Brasília}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":66,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.0980000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_8","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Where is Mount Kilimanjaro located?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Mount Kilimanjaro is located in northeastern Tanzania, near the border with Kenya. It is the highest mountain in Africa and a prominent free-standing volcanic peak. \n\n\\boxed{Tanzania}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":68,"output_tokens":40}},"cost":3.2840000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"GeoGraphyData_100k_9","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the largest country in Africa by area?\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The largest country in Africa by area is Algeria. It covers approximately 2.38 million square kilometers, making it slightly larger than the Democratic Republic of the Congo. \n\n\\boxed{Algeria}","success":t