t_tokens":86},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.72e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_3788","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these people is NOT a part of the Internet comedy group Mega64?\n\nOptions: \nA. Rocco Botte\nB. Shawn Chatfield\nC. Jon Jafari\nD. Derrick Acosta\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Jon Jafari, also known as \"JonTron,\" was a member of Mega64 but left the group years ago, though he was notably involved in the early days. Rocco Botte, Shawn Chatfield, and Derrick Acosta are core founding members who have remained central to the group. However, the question asks who is *not* a part of Mega64, and since Jon Jafari is no longer actively involved, the answer reflects current membership.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":108,"output_tokens":100}},"cost":7.404e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_3793","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is generally considered to be William Shakespeare's birth date?\n\nOptions: \nA. July 4th, 1409\nB. April 23rd, 1564\nC. December 1st, 1750\nD. September 29th, 1699\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"William Shakespeare was baptized on April 26, 1564, in Stratford-upon-Avon, and the conventional date assigned to his birth (based on the common interval of three days between birth and baptism, plus the fact that he died on April 23, 1616) is therefore April 23 of the same year. All other listed options fall well outside the documented 16th-century timeframe of his life.  \n\nThus the only historically plausible choice is B.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":242,"output_tokens":98},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.740000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_3896","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which actress's real name was Frances Ethel Gumm?\n\nOptions: \nA. Marilyn Monroe\nB. Julie Andrews\nC. Judy Garland\nD. Doris Day\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Frances Ethel Gumm was the birth name of Judy Garland, who later became famous for her role as Dorothy in *The Wizard of Oz*. The other actresses listed had different birth names. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":52}},"cost":4.407e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_3968","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Gwyneth Paltrow has a daughter named...?\n\nOptions: \nA. Lily\nB. Dakota\nC. Apple\nD. French\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Gwyneth Paltrow named her daughter Apple. This choice attracted media attention due to its uniqueness. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":28}},"cost":2.9020000000000003e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_4035","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Named after a character he played in a 1969 film, what is the name of the ski resort in Utah that Robert Redford bought in 1968?\n\nOptions: \nA. Booker\nB. Turner\nC. Woodward\nD. Sundance\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Robert Redford bought a ski resort in Utah in 1968 and later named it after the character he played in the 1969 film *Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid*. The character's name was the Sundance Kid, which inspired the resort's name. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":120,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.879999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_4085","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What year did radio icon Howard Stern start a job at radio station WNBC?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1984\nB. 1985\nC. 1982\nD. 1995\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Howard Stern joined WNBC-AM in New York in September 1982 after a brief stint in Detroit, marking the start of his major-market breakthrough. He remained there until his firing in September 1985, confirming the start date among the given options. Later years such as 1995 refer to his move to K-Rock, not WNBC.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":227,"output_tokens":75},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.290000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_4096","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Neil Hamburger is played by which comedian?\n\nOptions: \nA. Gregg Turkington\nB. Nathan Fielder\nC. Tim Heidecker\nD. Todd Glass\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Neil Hamburger is a fictional comedic character created and portrayed by Gregg Turkington. The character is known for his awkward stand-up style and distinctive appearance, often featured in Turkington's performances and projects like \"Tom Goes to the Mayor\" and \"On Cinema.\" \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":59}},"cost":4.827e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_3577","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If he was still alive, in what year would Elvis Presley celebrate his 100th birthday?\n\nOptions: \nA. 2030\nB. 2045\nC. 2040\nD. 2035\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Elvis Presley was born on January 8, 1935. His 100th birthday would occur in 1935 + 100 = 2035. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":120,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.9800000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_162","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who out of these actresses is the youngest?\n\nOptions: \nA. Bonnie Wright\nB. Emma Watson\nC. Kiernan Shipka\nD. Ariel Winter\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Ariel Winter is the youngest among the listed actresses, born on January 28, 1998. Kiernan Shipka was born on November 10, 1999, making her younger than Winter. Therefore, the correct answer is C, Kiernan Shipka.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.413999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Celebrities_1078","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What caused Jake Lloyd who played Anakin Skywalker in The Phantom Menace to quit acting?\n\nOptions: \nA. Bullying\nB. Racism\nC. Criminal Record\nD. Nomination for Worst Actor\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Jake Lloyd quit acting primarily due to the intense bullying he experienced after playing Anakin Skywalker in *The Phantom Menace*. The negative reception and public scrutiny, including being nominated for a Razzie Award for Worst Actor, contributed to his decision. The most direct cause was the persistent bullying he faced from peers.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":107,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":5.4709999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3452","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The body of the Egyptian Sphinx was based on which animal?\n\nOptions: \nA. Bull\nB. Lion\nC. Horse\nD. Dog\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The body of the Egyptian Sphinx is traditionally depicted with the body of a lion and the head of a human, symbolizing strength and wisdom. This iconic structure, located at Giza, reflects the lion's association with power in ancient Egyptian culture. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":56}},"cost":4.5820000000000005e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2604","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In which English county is the city of Portsmouth?\n\nOptions: \nA. Hampshire\nB. Buckinghamshire\nC. Surrey\nD. Oxfordshire\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city of Portsmouth is located on the south coast of England and is part of the ceremonial and historic county of Hampshire. Although it is a unitary authority, it remains within the traditional boundaries of Hampshire. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":48}},"cost":4.102e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3843","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Colchester Overpass, otherwise known as \"Bunny Man Bridge\", is located where?\n\nOptions: \nA. Fairfax County, Virginia\nB. Lemon Grove, California\nC. Braxton County, Virgina\nD. Medford, Oregon\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Colchester Overpass, famously known as the \"Bunny Man Bridge,\" is located in Fairfax County, Virginia. It is associated with a local urban legend about a man wearing a bunny costume who supposedly committed murders in the area. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":116,"output_tokens":53}},"cost":4.688e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3353","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are no roads in/out of Juneau, Alaska.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Juneau, Alaska, is not connected to the rest of the road system in the United States or Canada by car-accessible roads, meaning there are no roads leading in or out of the city. Travel to and from Juneau is primarily by air or sea. Therefore, the statement is essentially true in practical terms.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.3179999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1545","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: St. Louis is the capital of the US State Missouri.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"St. Louis is a major city in Missouri, but it is not the capital. The capital of Missouri is Jefferson City. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":37}},"cost":3.338e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_4025","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Where would you find the \"Spanish Steps\"?\n\nOptions: \nA. London, England\nB. Barcelona, Spain\nC. Rome, Italy\nD. Berlin, Germany\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Spanish Steps\" are a famous landmark located in Rome, Italy, connecting the Piazza di Spagna with the Trinità dei Monti church. Despite the name, they are not in Spain or any other country listed. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.5270000000000005e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_429","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What North American tourist attraction is served by the \"Maid of the Mist\" tour company?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yosemite National Park\nB. Niagara Falls\nC. Disney World\nD. Whistler, British Columbia\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Maid of the Mist\" is a famous boat tour that takes visitors to the base of Niagara Falls. It has been operating for over a century and is specifically associated with Niagara Falls on the U.S.-Canada border. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":53}},"cost":4.61e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3559","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Space Needle is located in which city?\n\nOptions: \nA. Los Angles\nB. Vancouver\nC. Toronto\nD. Seattle\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Space Needle is a famous landmark located in Seattle, Washington. It was built for the 1962 World's Fair and remains one of the city's most iconic structures. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":44}},"cost":3.849e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1770","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Harvard University is located in which city?\n\nOptions: \nA. Providence\nB. Cambridge\nC. New York\nD. Washington D.C.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Harvard University is located in Cambridge, Massachusetts. This city is directly across the Charles River from Boston and is home to several prestigious academic institutions. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":36}},"cost":3.369e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3028","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Llanfair­pwllgwyngyll­gogery­chwyrn­drobwll­llan­tysilio­gogo­goch is located on which Welsh island?\n\nOptions: \nA. Anglesey\nB. Bardsey\nC. Barry\nD. Caldey\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Llanfairpwllgwyngyllgogerychwyrndrobwllllantysiliogogogoch is a village located on the island of Anglesey in Wales. It is famous for having the longest place name in Europe. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":127,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":5.131e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3407","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What island in the Canary Islands was the scene of one of the worst air disasters in history with the collision of two jumbo jets?\n\nOptions: \nA. Gran Canaria\nB. Maui\nC. Tenerife\nD. Fuerteventura\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The correct answer is C. Tenerife. The Tenerife airport disaster occurred on March 27, 1977, when two Boeing 747 jumbo jets collided on the runway at Los Rodeos Airport (now Tenerife North Airport), resulting in 583 fatalities, the deadliest accident in aviation history. Gran Canaria was the original destination, but flights were diverted to Tenerife due to a bomb threat.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":116,"output_tokens":100}},"cost":7.508e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1317","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: New Haven is the capital city of the state of Connecticut in the United States.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The statement is false because the capital city of Connecticut is Hartford, not New Haven. New Haven is a major city in Connecticut but does not serve as the state capital.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":40}},"cost":3.57e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2088","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Broome is a town in which state of Australia?\n\nOptions: \nA. Northern Territory\nB. Tasmania\nC. South Australia\nD. Western Australia\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Broome is a town located in the northern part of Western Australia, known for its pearling history and proximity to the Kimberley region. It is situated along the Indian Ocean coast, which aligns with Western Australia's geography. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":59}},"cost":4.788e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1608","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: All of the following are classified as Finno-Ugric languages EXCEPT:\n\nOptions: \nA. Hungarian\nB. Finnish\nC. Estonian\nD. Samoyedic\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Finno-Ugric language family includes Hungarian, Finnish, and Estonian. Samoyedic is not part of the Finno-Ugric group but is instead a separate branch of the larger Uralic language family. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.793e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_504","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In which English county is Stonehenge?\n\nOptions: \nA. Wiltshire\nB. Somerset\nC. Cumbria\nD. Herefordshire\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Stonehenge is located in the county of Wiltshire, in South West England. It is one of the most famous prehistoric monuments in the world and has been a protected site for many years. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":4.081e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3211","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What event led to Liechenstein adding a crown to its flag?\n\nOptions: \nA. The 1936 Olympics\nB. Signing of the 1862 Constitution of Liechtenstein\nC. Charles VI's decree in 1719\nD. Coronation of Prince Johann I Joseph in 1805\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The addition of the crown to Liechtenstein's flag was inspired by the country's participation in the 1936 Summer Olympics in Berlin, where Liechtenstein realized its flag was identical to Haiti's. To distinguish itself, Liechtenstein added a crown to its flag shortly after. This change was officially adopted in 1937.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":135,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":6.375e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1221","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: On a London Underground map, what colour is the Circle Line?\n\nOptions: \nA. Blue\nB. Red\nC. Green\nD. Yellow\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Circle Line on the London Underground map is represented by the color yellow. This distinguishes it from other lines such as the Central Line (red) or the Jubilee Line (silver). \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":95,"output_tokens":45}},"cost":3.935e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2468","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many stations does the Central Line have on the London Underground?\n\nOptions: \nA. 43\nB. 49\nC. 47\nD. 51\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Central Line on the London Underground has 49 stations. This includes stations from West Ruislip and Ealing Broadway in the west to Epping and Hainault in the east. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":109,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":4.2369999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1120","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The flag of South Africa features 7 colours.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The flag of South Africa features six colors, not seven: red, blue, green, yellow, black, and white. The design combines these colors in a unique Y-shaped pattern. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":50}},"cost":4.092e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3880","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following Inuit languages was the FIRST to use a unique writing system not based on the Latin alphabet?\n\nOptions: \nA. Inuktitut\nB. Inupiat\nC. Greenlandic\nD. Inuinnaqtun\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Inuit language that first used a unique writing system not based on the Latin alphabet is Inuktitut, which adopted Canadian Aboriginal syllabics in the 19th century. This writing system was originally developed for Cree but was adapted for Inuktitut and is distinct from the Latin alphabet. The other options either use Latin-based scripts or later adopted writing systems.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":115,"output_tokens":81}},"cost":6.355e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1953","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Where is the fast food chain \"Panda Express\" headquartered?\n\nOptions: \nA. Fresno, California\nB. Rosemead, California\nC. San Diego, California\nD. Sacramento, California\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Panda Express is headquartered in Rosemead, California. This is where the company was founded and continues to operate its main offices. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":33}},"cost":3.358e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2812","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Liechtenstein does not have an airport.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Liechtenstein is a small landlocked country without an airport due to its size and mountainous terrain. It relies on nearby airports in Switzerland and Austria for air travel. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":83,"output_tokens":46}},"cost":3.839e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2364","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What national museum will you find in Cooperstown, New York?\n\nOptions: \nA. National Toy Hall of Fame\nB. National Baseball Hall of Fame\nC. Metropolitan Museum of Art\nD. Museum of Modern Art\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The National Baseball Hall of Fame is located in Cooperstown, New York, and is dedicated to preserving the history of baseball in the United States. The other options listed are not located in Cooperstown. The Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Museum of Modern Art are in New York City, and while the National Toy Hall of Fame is a real institution, it is located in Rochester, New York.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":109,"output_tokens":85}},"cost":6.517e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_878","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following European languages is classified as a \"language isolate?\"\n\nOptions: \nA. Maltese\nB. Galician\nC. Hungarian\nD. Basque\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The correct answer is D. Basque is considered a language isolate because it has no known genetic relationship to any other language, especially not to the Indo-European family. Unlike Maltese, Galician, and Hungarian—each of which belongs to a larger language family—Basque stands alone in its linguistic lineage.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":5.379999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1057","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Japanese district Akihabara is also known by what nickname?\n\nOptions: \nA. Otaku Central \nB. Electric Town\nC. Big Eyes\nD. Moon Walk River\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Akihabara is widely known as \"Electric Town\" due to its numerous electronics shops and its historical role as a hub for electronic commerce in Tokyo. Over time, it also became a center for anime, manga, and otaku culture, but its official nickname remains tied to its electronic roots. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":67}},"cost":5.345999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3368","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What name was historically used for the Turkish city currently known as Istanbul?\n\nOptions: \nA. Adrianople\nB. Constantinople\nC. Hüdavendigar\nD. Söğüt\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city now known as Istanbul was historically called Constantinople, serving as the capital of the Byzantine Empire. After the Ottoman conquest in 1453, it remained widely known by that name until the official name change to Istanbul in the 20th century. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":104,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":5.0719999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3087","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The title of the 1969 film \"Krakatoa, East_of Java\" is incorrect, as Krakatoa is in fact west of Java.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The 1969 film \"Krakatoa, East of Java\" is indeed titled incorrectly because Krakatoa is located west of Java, not east. The volcano is situated in the Sunda Strait between Java and Sumatra, placing it to the west of Java. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":108,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.544e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2480","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which English county will you find the University of East Anglia?\n\nOptions: \nA. Norfolk\nB. Suffolk\nC. Essex\nD. Cambridgeshire\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The University of East Anglia is located in Norwich, which is in the county of Norfolk. Therefore, the correct answer is A. Norfolk.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":35}},"cost":3.374e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_724","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Into which basin does the Jordan River flow into?\n\nOptions: \nA. Aral Sea\nB. Salton Sea\nC. Dead Sea\nD. Caspian Sea\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Jordan River flows into the Dead Sea, which is a saltwater lake bordered by Jordan to the east and Israel and the Palestinian territories to the west. It is one of the saltiest bodies of water in the world and lies at the lowest elevation on Earth's surface. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":5.0199999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2466","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Gothenburg is the capital of Sweden.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Gothenburg is not the capital of Sweden; the capital is Stockholm. Gothenburg is Sweden's second-largest city.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":83,"output_tokens":31}},"cost":2.939e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1511","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The formerly East-Prussian city of Königsberg is known as which Russian City today?\n\nOptions: \nA. Kazan\nB. Krasnodar\nC. Kaliningrad\nD. Kursk\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city of Königsberg, formerly in East Prussia, became part of Russia after World War II and was renamed Kaliningrad in 1946. It is now a Russian exclave situated between Poland and Lithuania. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":109,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.657e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2056","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The two largest ethnic groups of Belgium are Flemish and Walloon. \n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The two largest ethnic groups in Belgium are indeed the Flemish, who are Dutch-speaking and live in the northern region of Flanders, and the Walloons, who are French-speaking and reside in the southern region of Wallonia. This linguistic and cultural division is a key aspect of Belgium's demographic and political landscape. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":89,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.417e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1954","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Southeast Asian island of Borneo is politically divided among 3 countries.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The island of Borneo is indeed divided among three countries: Indonesia, Malaysia, and Brunei. Indonesia controls the largest portion (Kalimantan), while the Malaysian states of Sabah and Sarawak occupy the northern part, and Brunei is a small sovereign nation on the island. Therefore, the statement is correct.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.43e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_776","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If soccer is called football in England, what is American football called in England?\n\nOptions: \nA. Touchdown\nB. Combball\nC. American football\nD. Handball\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"In England, American football is commonly referred to as \"American football\" to distinguish it from the sport known as football (soccer) in the U.S. The other options—Touchdown, Combball, and Handball—are not correct names for the sport.  \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":60}},"cost":4.926e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1149","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The World Health Organization headquarters is located in which European country?\n\nOptions: \nA. France\nB. Switzerland\nC. Belgium\nD. United Kingdom\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The World Health Organization (WHO) headquarters is located in Geneva, Switzerland. Switzerland is known for hosting numerous international organizations and agencies. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":95,"output_tokens":33}},"cost":3.215e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1548","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Israel is 7 hours ahead of New York.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Israel is typically 7 hours ahead of New York during certain times of the year, depending on daylight saving time adjustments in both regions. However, due to differing daylight saving schedules, the time difference can vary between 6 and 7 hours. Generally, when both are not observing daylight saving, the difference is 7 hours.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.411999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2150","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are no deserts in Europe.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The statement is false, as Europe contains multiple arid regions that qualify as deserts (or semi-deserts) under standard geographic definitions. Examples include Spain's Tabernas Desert and smaller sites such as Italy's Accona Desert.  \n\nThus the correct choice is B.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":202,"output_tokens":56},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":6.840000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1942","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What was the African nation of Zimbabwe formerly known as?\n\nOptions: \nA. Mozambique\nB. Zambia\nC. Rhodesia\nD.  Bulawayo\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The African nation of Zimbabwe was formerly known as Rhodesia, named after British colonialist Cecil Rhodes. It was called Southern Rhodesia during the colonial period before gaining independence in 1980. The name was changed to Zimbabwe upon independence.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.5740000000000006e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_171","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the name of one of the Neo-Aramaic languages spoken by the Jewish population from Northwestern Iraq?\n\nOptions: \nA. Lishan Didan\nB. Hulaulá\nC. Lishana Deni\nD. Chaldean Neo-Aramaic\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"One of the Neo-Aramaic languages spoken by the Jewish population from northwestern Iraq is Hulaulá, which is specifically associated with Jewish communities in that region. The other options, such as Lishan Didan and Lishana Deni, are also Jewish Neo-Aramaic dialects but are linked to different geographic areas. Chaldean Neo-Aramaic is traditionally spoken by Christian communities, not Jewish ones.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":122,"output_tokens":93}},"cost":7.166e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2220","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is an island in Japan called Ōkunoshima, A.K.A. \"Rabbit Island\", so named because of it's huge population of rabbits.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The island of Ōkunoshima in Japan is indeed commonly known as \"Rabbit Island\" due to the large number of wild rabbits that inhabit it. Tourists often visit the island to interact with the rabbits, which roam freely. This makes the statement accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":108,"output_tokens":61}},"cost":5.064e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3638","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The prefix Sino- (As in Sino-American) is used to refer to what nationality?\n\nOptions: \nA. Japanese\nB. Russian\nC. Chinese\nD. Indian\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The prefix \"Sino-\" is derived from the Latin word \"Sinae,\" which refers to China. It is commonly used in terms like \"Sino-American\" to denote relations between China and another country. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.626e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3837","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following languages does NOT use the Latin alphabet?\n\nOptions: \nA. Vietnamese\nB. Turkish\nC. Swahili\nD. Georgian\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Georgian language uses the unique Georgian script, not the Latin alphabet. Vietnamese, Turkish, and Swahili all use variations of the Latin alphabet. Therefore, the correct answer is D.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":44}},"cost":3.888e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3780","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You could walk from Norway to North Korea while only passing through Russia.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Norway shares a short land border with Russia in the far north, and Russia in turn shares a short land border with North Korea near Vladivostok. A continuous overland route can therefore begin in Norway, cross only into Russia, traverse Russian territory, and enter North Korea without touching any third country. The statement is consequently true.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":71},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.730000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_870","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following countries banned the use of personal genetic ancestry tests?\n\nOptions: \nA. Austria\nB. Germany\nC. Canada\nD. Sweden\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Germany has strict regulations on genetic testing, and personal genetic ancestry tests are effectively banned due to laws protecting genetic privacy under the Genetic Diagnostics Act. These laws restrict direct-to-consumer genetic testing without medical oversight. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":49}},"cost":4.188e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3207","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Frankenmuth, a US city nicknamed \"Little Bavaria\", is located in what state?\n\nOptions: \nA. Kentucky\nB. Virginia\nC. Michigan\nD. Pennsylvania\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Frankenmuth, known as \"Little Bavaria\" for its strong German heritage and architecture, is located in Michigan. It is a popular tourist destination famous for its Bavarian-themed festivals, food, and the world's largest Christmas store. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":56}},"cost":4.673000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2880","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the official German name of the Swiss Federal Railways?\n\nOptions: \nA. Schweizerische Staatsbahnen\nB. Schweizerische Nationalbahnen\nC. Bundesbahnen der Schweiz\nD. Schweizerische Bundesbahnen\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The official German name of the Swiss Federal Railways is \"Schweizerische Bundesbahnen,\" which directly translates to \"Swiss Federal Railways.\" This name reflects its status as a federal enterprise in Switzerland. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":116,"output_tokens":50}},"cost":4.508e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_113","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Where is Hadrian's Wall located?\n\nOptions: \nA. Alexandria, Egypt\nB. Rome, Italy\nC. Carlisle, England\nD. Dublin, Ireland\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Hadrian's Wall is located in northern England, stretching from the River Tyne near Newcastle to the Solway Firth. It was built by the Romans under Emperor Hadrian to mark the northern boundary of the Roman Empire. The correct answer is Carlisle, England, as it lies along the wall's route.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.4269999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1131","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Tokyo is the capital of Japan.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Tokyo is indeed the capital city of Japan. It is the political, economic, and cultural center of the country. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":31}},"cost":2.913e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1664","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Nova Scotia is on the east coast of Canada.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Nova Scotia is indeed located on the east coast of Canada, bordered by the Atlantic Ocean. It is one of the Maritime provinces and is situated on the southeastern coast of the country. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":43}},"cost":3.672e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2346","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the county seat of King County, Washington?\n\nOptions: \nA. Seattle\nB. Skykomish\nC. Bellevue\nD. Enumclaw\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The county seat of King County, Washington, is Seattle, which is the largest city in the state and the administrative center of the county. Although other cities like Bellevue and Enumclaw are located within King County, they are not the county seat. Skykomish is a smaller town in the county and not a governmental center.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.5939999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_473","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Hunua Ranges is located in...\n\nOptions: \nA. China\nB. Nepal\nC. New Zealand\nD. Mexico\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Hunua Ranges is a geographical feature located in the North Island of New Zealand, southeast of Auckland. It is known for its native bush, walking trails, and reservoirs supplying water to Auckland. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":92,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.436e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1997","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Montreal is in which Canadian province?\n\nOptions: \nA. Quebec\nB. Nova Scotia\nC. Alberta\nD. Ontario\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Montreal is the largest city in the province of Quebec and is located in the southern part of the province along the Saint Lawrence River. It is known for its French-speaking population and rich cultural heritage. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":3.99e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3155","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The capital of the US State Ohio is the city of Chillicothe.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The statement is false because while Chillicothe was the original capital of Ohio, the current capital is Columbus. Columbus has been the capital since 1816. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":3.99e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_3698","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Sonoran Desert is located in eastern Africa.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Sonoran Desert is not located in eastern Africa; it is primarily found in the southwestern United States and northern Mexico. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.  \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":37}},"cost":3.312e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_514","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these countries is located the FURTHEST away from the South China Sea?\n\nOptions: \nA. Malaysia\nB. Vietnam\nC. Bangladesh\nD. Philippines\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Bangladesh is located in South Asia, far from the South China Sea, while Malaysia, Vietnam, and the Philippines all have coastlines directly on the South China Sea. Therefore, Bangladesh is the furthest from the South China Sea among the listed countries.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.7199999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_792","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these countries is \"doubly landlocked\" (surrounded entirely by one or more landlocked countries)?\n\nOptions: \nA. Uzbekistan\nB. Switzerland\nC. Bolivia\nD. Ethiopia\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"A doubly landlocked country is one that is landlocked and also surrounded only by other landlocked countries. Among the options, only Uzbekistan meets this criterion, as it is surrounded by other landlocked countries in Central Asia. The other countries either have access to the sea or are bordered by non-landlocked nations.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":107,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.591e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_1143","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is not a megadiverse country - one that harbors a high number of the earth's endemic species?\n\nOptions: \nA. Peru\nB. Thailand\nC. South Africa\nD. Mexico\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"All the listed countries are megadiverse, but Thailand is not officially recognized as one of the 17 megadiverse countries by Conservation International, unlike Peru, South Africa, and Mexico. Megadiverse countries are selected based on having high levels of biodiversity, especially endemism, and Thailand, while biodiverse, does not meet the full criteria. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":109,"output_tokens":78}},"cost":6.097e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2393","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following Arab countries does NOT have a flag containing only Pan-Arab colours?\n\nOptions: \nA. United Arab Emirates\nB. Jordan\nC. Qatar\nD. Kuwait\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Pan-Arab colors are black, white, green, and red. Qatar's flag contains maroon and white, with maroon not being one of the traditional Pan-Arab colors. Therefore, Qatar does not have a flag with only Pan-Arab colors.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.806e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_Geography_2099","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following language families is the most controversial amongst modern linguists?\n\nOptions: \nA. Indo-European\nB. Dravidian\nC. Altaic\nD. Sino-Tibetan\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Altaic language family is considered the most controversial among linguists due to ongoing debates about whether the similarities among its proposed members (such as Turkish, Mongolian, and Korean) are due to genetic relationship or are the result of language contact and borrowing. While Indo-European, Dravidian, and Sino-Tibetan are widely accepted as valid language families, Altaic lacks consensus. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":86}},"cost":6.538e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_662","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 1967, a magazine published a story about extracting hallucinogenic chemicals from bananas to raise moral questions about banning drugs.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The story about extracting hallucinogenic chemicals from bananas was published in 1967 by the *Berkeley Barb*, a counterculture magazine, as a satirical piece to challenge drug prohibition policies. It claimed that banana peels contained psychoactive substances, prompting debates and even some attempts to replicate the results. The story was a hoax, but it made a serious point about the logic of drug laws.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":89}},"cost":6.666e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_253","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Fallingwater House, located in Pennsylvania, was designed by which architect?\n\nOptions: \nA. Antoni Gaudi\nB. Frank Lloyd Wright\nC. Frank Gehry\nD. Le Corbusier\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Fallingwater House in Pennsylvania is one of the most famous works of Frank Lloyd Wright, designed in 1935 as a private residence. It exemplifies his philosophy of organic architecture, integrating buildings with their natural surroundings. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":123,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.859e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3563","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If you grab the bladed end of a longsword in a specific way, you will not cut yourself.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question specifies grabbing the blade \"in a specific way,\" which directly alludes to historical half-swording techniques (one hand on the hilt, the other gripping the blade flats or ricasso). In such grips, pressure is applied perpendicular to the edges so the blade does not slice flesh; the motion of cutting, not static contact, produces injury. Therefore the statement is true.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.39e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2093","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: \"I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it\" is a quote from French philosopher Voltaire.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote \"I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it\" is often attributed to Voltaire, but he did not actually say it. It was written by Evelyn Beatrice Hall, an English writer, to summarize Voltaire's views on free speech. Therefore, the statement is not literally true.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":5.985e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_236","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The fourth funnel of the RMS Titanic was fake designed to make the ship look more powerful and symmetrical.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The statement is accurate based on historical records of Titanic's design and construction. The ship's fourth funnel was a dummy (nonfunctional) stack added purely for visual symmetry and to convey an impression of greater power/speed, matching similar choices on other contemporary liners like the Olympic. While it provided minor secondary ventilation, this did not change its primarily decorative purpose.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":74},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.999999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3511","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which Las Vegas casino was originally constructed and operated by mobster Bugsy Siegel?\n\nOptions: \nA. The Flamingo\nB. The MGM Grand\nC. The Sahara\nD. The Sands\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Bugsy Siegel was instrumental in the development of the Flamingo Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas, which opened in 1946. It was one of the first luxury casinos on the Strip and was partially funded with mob money. The other options were developed later by different individuals.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":65}},"cost":5.278e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2033","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is NOT classified as a Semetic language?\n\nOptions: \nA. Sumerian\nB. Mandaic\nC. Akkadian\nD. Maltese\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Sumerian is not a Semitic language; it is a language isolate with no known linguistic relatives. Mandaic, Akkadian, and Maltese are all classified as Semitic languages. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":54}},"cost":4.579e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2185","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The coat of arms of the King of Spain contains the arms from the monarchs of Castille, Leon, Aragon and which other former Iberian kingdom?\n\nOptions: \nA. Catalonia\nB. Granada\nC. Galicia\nD. Navarre\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Spanish royal coat of arms is partitioned into four primary quarters that display the historic arms of Castile, León, Aragon, and Navarre; Granada appears only as a small escutcheon “enté en point” at the base. Because the question explicitly names the first three kingdoms and asks for the remaining one represented among those main quarters, the answer must be Navarre. Catalonia was never an independent kingdom, while Galicia’s arms are not included.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":234,"output_tokens":95},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.430000000000002e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1532","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Pianist Frédéric Chopin was a composer of which musical era?\n\nOptions: \nA. Baroque\nB. Romantic\nC. Renaissance\nD. Classic\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Frédéric Chopin was a composer of the Romantic era, known for his expressive piano works and emphasis on emotion and individualism. The Romantic period spanned much of the 19th century, which aligns with Chopin's lifetime (1810–1849). His music exemplifies the stylistic traits of Romanticism, such as lyrical melodies and rich harmonies.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.507e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_950","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The M41 Walker Bulldog remains in service in some countries to this day.\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The M41 Walker Bulldog, a light tank developed by the United States in the 1950s, was widely exported and saw service in numerous countries. Although it has been retired by most modern militaries, some nations, particularly in South America and Asia, have kept upgraded versions in limited service. Therefore, the statement is accurate.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":76}},"cost":5.7430000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_453","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What was the aim of the \"Umbrella Revolution\" in Hong Kong in 2014?\n\nOptions: \nA. Lower taxes\nB. Go back under British Rule\nC. Genuine universal suffrage\nD. Gaining Independence\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Umbrella Revolution\" in 2014 was a pro-democracy movement in Hong Kong that called for greater political freedom and a more democratic electoral system. Protesters demanded genuine universal suffrage for the selection of Hong Kong's Chief Executive, opposing Beijing's restrictions on candidate nominations. The movement did not seek independence or a return to British rule, but rather democratic reforms within the framework of \"One Country, Two Systems.\"\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":114,"output_tokens":94}},"cost":7.122e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3923","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The original Roman alphabet lacked the following letters EXCEPT:\n\nOptions: \nA. J\nB. X\nC. W\nD. U\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The original Roman alphabet did not include the letters J, W, or U, as these were later developments. The letter X, however, was part of the early Roman alphabet and used to represent the /ks/ sound. Therefore, the only letter among the options that was present originally is X.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":66}},"cost":5.169e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1092","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When did L. L. Zamenhof first publish \"Unua Libro\", the first publication describing his international language Esperanto?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1887\nB. 1915\nC. 1897\nD. 1905\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"L. L. Zamenhof first published \"Unua Libro\" in 1887, introducing the constructed language Esperanto to the public. This book was published under the pseudonym \"Doktoro Esperanto\" and marked the official beginning of the Esperanto movement. The correct answer is therefore:\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":125,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.8849999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3348","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The ancient city of Chichén Itzá was built by which civilization?\n\nOptions: \nA. Toltecs\nB. Mayans\nC. Incas\nD. Aztecs\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The ancient city of Chichén Itzá was primarily built by the Maya civilization, although it shows influence from the Toltecs due to later cultural interactions. It is located in the Yucatán Peninsula of Mexico, a region historically inhabited by the Maya. While the Toltecs had some influence on the site, the original builders and dominant culture were the Maya.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":80}},"cost":6.139000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1452","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Under which name was Rodrigo Borgia made Pope?\n\nOptions: \nA. John Paul II\nB. Rodrigo I\nC. Pius VII\nD. Alexander VI\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Rodrigo Borgia was elected Pope in 1492 and took the name Alexander VI. He is one of the most controversial popes in history, known for his involvement in political intrigue and nepotism. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":52}},"cost":4.394e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_46","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Tiananmen Square protests of 1989 were held in Hong Kong.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Tiananmen Square protests of 1989 took place in Beijing, not in Hong Kong. Therefore, the statement is factually incorrect. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":92,"output_tokens":38}},"cost":3.476e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_422","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The creator of the Enigma Cypher and Machine was of what nationality?\n\nOptions: \nA. German\nB. British\nC. American\nD. Polish\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Enigma machine was originally developed by a German engineer, Arthur Scherbius, in the early 1920s. Although the Poles later made significant breakthroughs in breaking the Enigma cipher, the machine itself was a German invention. Therefore, the creator's nationality was German.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":97,"output_tokens":67}},"cost":5.281e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2949","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: King Henry VIII was the second monarch of which European royal house?\n\nOptions: \nA. Stuart\nB. York\nC. Tudor\nD. Lancaster\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"King Henry VIII was the second monarch of the Tudor dynasty, which came to power after the Wars of the Roses. His father, Henry VII, was the first Tudor monarch, making Henry VIII the second. The other houses listed—Stuart, York, and Lancaster—preceded or succeeded the Tudors.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.448e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3603","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Sir Issac Newton served as a Member of Parliament, but the only recorded time he spoke was to complain about a draft in the chambers.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement is a well-known historical anecdote about Newton’s brief tenure as MP for Cambridge University (1689–1690 and 1701–1702). Contemporary and later accounts record only a single intervention by him—requesting that a window be closed because of a draft—making the claim accurate. Therefore the correct choice is B.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":223,"output_tokens":73},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.109999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3902","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is not in the Indo-European language family?\n\nOptions: \nA. Finnish\nB. Russian\nC. English\nD. Hindi\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Finnish is not part of the Indo-European language family; it belongs to the Uralic language family, specifically the Finno-Ugric group. Russian, English, and Hindi are all Indo-European languages, belonging to the Slavic, Germanic, and Indo-Iranian branches respectively. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":95,"output_tokens":67}},"cost":5.255e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1183","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After his loss at the Battle of Waterloo, Napoleon Bonaparte was exiled to which island?\n\nOptions: \nA. Elba\nB. St. Helena\nC. Canary\nD. Corsica\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"After his defeat at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815, Napoleon Bonaparte was exiled to the remote island of St. Helena in the South Atlantic Ocean. This was his second and final exile, as he had previously been exiled to Elba in 1814 before escaping and returning to power. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":73}},"cost":5.7579999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2888","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who is the creator of the soft drink, Dr. Pepper?\n\nOptions: \nA. James Wellington\nB. Charles Alderton\nC. Boris Heviltik\nD. Johnson Hinsin\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The creator of Dr. Pepper is Charles Alderton, a pharmacist who developed the drink in Waco, Texas, in 1885. He worked at Morrison's Old Corner Drug Store and formulated the unique flavor that became Dr. Pepper. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.785e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_940","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The minigun was designed in 1960 by which manufacturer.\n\nOptions: \nA. General Electric\nB. Colt Firearms\nC. Sig Sauer\nD. Heckler & Koch\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The minigun was designed in the 1960s by General Electric as part of a project to create a high-rate-of-fire rotary cannon for military aircraft and vehicles. It was developed from the earlier M61 Vulcan cannon and became widely known for its use in helicopters and ground vehicles during the Vietnam War. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.685e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1865","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In addition to his career as an astrologer and \"prophet\", Nostradamus published a 1555 treatise that included a section on what?\n\nOptions: \nA. Cheating at card games\nB. Making jams and jellies\nC. Teaching parrots to talk\nD. Digging graves\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Nostradamus was a 16th-century physician and author best known for his prophecies, but he also wrote on health and hygiene. His 1555 treatise, *Les Prophéties*, was accompanied by a section on practical health advice, including methods for preventing disease, such as making and using preserves to combat the plague. This aligns most closely with the option related to food preservation.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":132,"output_tokens":93}},"cost":7.295999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3884","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who is attributed credit for recording the epic poem The Odyssey?\n\nOptions: \nA. Homer\nB. Socrates\nC. Aristotle\nD. Odysseus\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The epic poem *The Odyssey* is traditionally attributed to the ancient Greek poet Homer. While the historical existence of Homer is debated, ancient and modern scholarship widely credits him as the author. Socrates, Aristotle, and Odysseus were not poets of the work—Socrates and Aristotle were philosophers, and Odysseus is the fictional hero of the poem.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":80}},"cost":6.0740000000000005e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1815","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During the Spanish Civil War (1936), Francisco Franco fought for which political faction?\n\nOptions: \nA. Popular Front\nB. Papal State\nC. Nationalist Spain\nD. Republican Spain\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Francisco Franco was a leading figure in the Nationalist faction during the Spanish Civil War, which opposed the Republican government. The Nationalists were composed of conservatives, monarchists, fascists, and the military, seeking to overthrow the left-leaning Popular Front government. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":107,"output_tokens":64}},"cost":5.231e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3970","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which English king was married to Eleanor of Aquitaine?\n\nOptions: \nA. Henry V\nB. Richard I\nC. Henry I\nD. Henry II\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"D. Henry II was married to Eleanor of Aquitaine, one of the most powerful and influential women in medieval Europe. Eleanor was first married to King Louis VII of France, but after their marriage was annulled, she married Henry, who later became King Henry II of England. Their marriage significantly expanded English territories and influence in France.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.714e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3985","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: With which Greek philosopher would you associate the phrase, \"I know that I know nothing\"?\n\nOptions: \nA. Aristotle\nB. Pythagoras\nC. Socrates\nD. Plato\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The phrase \"I know that I know nothing\" is most commonly associated with Socrates, reflecting his Socratic irony and method of questioning to expose ignorance. Although the exact wording does not appear in ancient texts, it captures the essence of Socratic wisdom as described in Plato's dialogues. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":103,"output_tokens":65}},"cost":5.239e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_170","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Spain was formed in 1469 with the marriage of Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of what other Iberian kingdom?\n\nOptions: \nA. Navarre\nB. Aragon\nC. Galicia\nD. León\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The marriage of Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II in 1469 united the two most powerful kingdoms on the Iberian Peninsula, laying the foundation for modern Spain. Ferdinand was the king of Aragon, making this union between Castile and Aragon pivotal in Spanish history. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":117,"output_tokens":67}},"cost":5.540999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1641","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What was the name of one of the surviving palaces of Henry VIII located near Richmond, London?\n\nOptions: \nA. St James's Palace\nB. Buckingham Palace\nC. Coughton Court\nD. Hampton Court\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"One of the surviving palaces of Henry VIII located near Richmond, London is Hampton Court Palace. It was expanded significantly by Henry VIII and remains a prominent historical site. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":39}},"cost":3.77e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3667","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Hagia Sophia was commissioned by which emperor of the Byzantine Empire?\n\nOptions: \nA. Theodosius the Great\nB. Justinian I\nC. Constantine IV\nD. Arcadius\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Hagia Sophia was commissioned by Emperor Justinian I in 532 AD after the previous church was destroyed during the Nika riots. It was constructed as a monumental cathedral in Constantinople and became a symbol of Byzantine architecture. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.678e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1436","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The son of which pope supposedly held a lecherous fête involving 50 courtesans in the papal palace?\n\nOptions: \nA. Pius III\nB. Urban II\nC. Innocent V\nD. Alexander VI\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The correct answer is D. Alexander VI, who was a controversial pope during the Renaissance, known for the scandalous behavior of his family, particularly his son Cesare Borgia. There are historical accounts, though sometimes disputed, of a lavish and immoral banquet hosted by Cesare Borgia in the Vatican involving courtesans. This event is often associated with the perceived corruption of the papal court under Alexander VI.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":113,"output_tokens":89}},"cost":6.809e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_332","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Battle of Trafalgar took place on October 23rd, 1805\n\nOptions: \nA. True\nB. False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Battle of Trafalgar actually took place on October 21st, 1805, not October 23rd. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.  \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":43}},"cost":3.828e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1483","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The ontological argument for the proof of God's existence is first attributed to whom?\n\nOptions: \nA. Aristotle\nB. Immanuel Kant\nC. Anselm of Canterbury\nD. René Descartes\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The ontological argument for the existence of God was first formulated by Anselm of Canterbury in the 11th century. He argued that God, defined as \"that than which nothing greater can be conceived,\" must exist in reality because existence is greater than mere conception. This argument is presented in his work *Proslogion*.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":109,"output_tokens":73}},"cost":5.7969999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_541","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In what dialogue did Socrates defend himself to the court of Athens? \n\nOptions: \nA. The Apology\nB. The Laws\nC. The Republic\nD. The Euthyphro\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Socrates defended himself in the dialogue known as The Apology, which recounts his speech during his trial in Athens. This work was written by Plato and is not an apology in the modern sense, but rather a defense of Socrates' actions and beliefs. The other options are dialogues on different topics and do not depict Socrates' trial.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":75}},"cost":5.878e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2594","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following ancient Near Eastern peoples still exists as a modern ethnic group?\n\nOptions: \nA. Assyrians\nB. Elamites\nC. Hittites\nD. Babylonians\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Assyrians are the only ancient Near Eastern people listed who still exist today as a modern ethnic group, primarily living in parts of Iraq, Syria, Turkey, and Iran. They have maintained a distinct cultural and linguistic identity, particularly through the use of Eastern Aramaic languages and Christian religious traditions. The Elamites, Hittites, and Babylonians are all extinct as ethnic groups.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":104,"output_tokens":85}},"cost":6.452e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2707","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What did the first vending machines in the early 1880's dispense?\n\nOptions: \nA. Sodas \nB. Cigarettes\nC. Alcohol\nD. Post cards\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The first vending machines in the early 1880s were installed in England and dispensed postcards, primarily used in post offices to sell postal cards and envelopes. These machines were invented by Percival Everitt and became popular for automating the sale of small postal items. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":105,"output_tokens":64}},"cost":5.205e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3423","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In what year was the video game company Electronic Arts founded?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1981\nB. 1999\nC. 2005\nD. 1982\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Electronic Arts was founded in 1982 by Trip Hawkins. The company was established with the goal of creating high-quality video games for personal computers. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":38}},"cost":3.71e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2331","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: United States President Ronald Reagan was the first president to appoint a woman to the Supreme Court. \n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Ronald Reagan appointed Sandra Day O'Connor to the Supreme Court in 1981, making her the first woman to serve on the Court. This appointment fulfilled a campaign promise and marked a historic milestone. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.5090000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3954","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The seed drill was invented by which British inventor?\n\nOptions: \nA. Jethro Tull\nB. Isaac Newton\nC. Charles Babbage\nD. J.J Thomson\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The seed drill was invented by Jethro Tull, an 18th-century British agriculturalist. This invention significantly improved the efficiency of planting crops by ensuring seeds were sown at the correct depth and spacing. \n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":101,"output_tokens":51}},"cost":4.373e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_411","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In what year was the famous 45 foot tall Hollywood sign first erected?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1933\nB. 1923\nC. 1913\nD. 1903\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Hollywood sign was originally erected in 1923 as \"Hollywoodland\" to promote a real estate development. It was a temporary structure but became an iconic landmark over time. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":114,"output_tokens":45}},"cost":4.182e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3283","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The pantheon in Rome was used to worship what god?\n\nOptions: \nA. Zeus\nB. Athena\nC. Any god they wanted\nD. Both Athena and Zeus\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Pantheon in Rome was originally built as a temple to honor all the gods of ancient Rome, not just one specific deity. Its name comes from the Greek words meaning \"temple of all the gods.\" Therefore, it was used to worship any god the Romans wished.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":61}},"cost":4.96e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_523","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The first televised presidential debate was between Jimmy Carter and Gerald Ford.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The first televised presidential debate actually occurred in 1960 between John F. Kennedy and Richard Nixon, not between Jimmy Carter and Gerald Ford. While Carter and Ford did debate in 1976, they were not the first. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":87,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":4.8509999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_485","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What number does the Roman numeral \"D\" stand for?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1000\nB. 50\nC. 500\nD. 100\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Roman numeral \"D\" stands for 500. This is a standard value in the Roman numeral system, where D represents half of M (1000). \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":106,"output_tokens":43}},"cost":3.9580000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2840","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Oxford University is older than the Aztec Empire. \n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Oxford University was founded in the late 12th century, around 1096, making it one of the oldest universities in the world. The Aztec Empire, on the other hand, was established in 1428, centuries after Oxford's founding. Therefore, Oxford University is indeed older than the Aztec Empire.\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":76}},"cost":5.665000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_64","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Who invented the \"Flying Shuttle\" in 1738; one of the key developments in the industrialization of weaving?\n\nOptions: \nA. John Kay\nB. Richard Arkwright\nC. James Hargreaves\nD. John Deere\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The flying shuttle was invented by John Kay in 1733 (often cited around 1738), significantly advancing the weaving process during the early Industrial Revolution. Richard Arkwright and James Hargreaves were also key figures in textile manufacturing, but they developed later inventions (the water frame and spinning jenny, respectively). John Deere was an American inventor known for agricultural machinery, not weaving.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":117,"output_tokens":89}},"cost":6.861e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_545","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The crown of the Empire State Building was originally built for what purpose?\n\nOptions: \nA. Antennae\nB. Flag Pole\nC. Lightning Rod\nD. Airship Dock\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The crown of the Empire State Building was originally designed as an airship docking station, intended for passenger airships to moor at the skyscraper's top. Although the idea was innovative, it proved impractical due to high winds and safety concerns, and was rarely used. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":5.0459999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_438","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After the 1516 Battle of Marj Dabiq, the Ottoman Empire took control of Jerusalem from which sultanate?\n\nOptions: \nA. Mamluk\nB. Ummayyad\nC. Ayyubid\nD. Seljuq\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"After the Battle of Marj Dabiq in 1516, the Ottoman Empire defeated the Mamluk Sultanate, leading to the incorporation of territories including Jerusalem into Ottoman rule. The Mamluks had controlled Jerusalem since the 13th century, following the Ayyubids. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":122,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":6.206e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_857","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Adolf Hitler was accepted into the Vienna Academy of Fine Arts.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Adolf Hitler applied to the Vienna Academy of Fine Arts but was rejected. He was, however, accepted into the school's architecture program, which he did not pursue. Therefore, the statement that he was accepted into the Academy of Fine Arts is false.\n\n\\boxed{A}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.5379999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1162","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The \"Trail of Tears\" was a result of which United States President's Indian Removal Policy?\n\nOptions: \nA. John Quincy Adams\nB. Andrew Jackson\nC. Harry S. Truman\nD. Martin Van Buren\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Trail of Tears\" was a direct result of the Indian Removal Act of 1830, which was championed by President Andrew Jackson. This policy led to the forced relocation of thousands of Native Americans, particularly the Cherokee, from their ancestral lands in the southeastern United States to territories west of the Mississippi River. The suffering and deaths during this journey gave it the name \"Trail of Tears.\"\n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.649999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3273","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In relation to the British Occupation in Ireland, what does the IRA stand for.\n\nOptions: \nA. Irish Reformation Army\nB. Irish-Royal Alliance\nC. Irish Republican Army\nD. Irish Rebel Alliance\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The IRA stands for Irish Republican Army, which was a paramilitary organization involved in various campaigns for Irish independence from British rule. The name reflects its republican ideals and historical role in resisting British occupation. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":108,"output_tokens":46}},"cost":4.164e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_1054","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which Louis was known as 'The Sun King of France'?\n\nOptions: \nA. Louis XIII\nB. Louis XV\nC. Louis XVI\nD. Louis XIV\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Louis XIV was known as 'The Sun King of France' due to his centralized rule and the grandeur of his reign, symbolized by the sun. He ruled for 72 years, the longest of any European monarch, and epitomized absolute monarchy. \n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":99,"output_tokens":60}},"cost":4.887e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3509","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The United States Department of Homeland Security was formed in response to the September 11th attacks.\n\nOptions: \nA. False\nB. True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The United States Department of Homeland Security was established in 2002 in direct response to the September 11, 2001, terrorist attacks. Its primary purpose is to protect the country from various threats, including terrorism. \n\n\\boxed{B}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.5220000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3972","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Abolitionist John Brown raided the arsenal in which Virginia Town?\n\nOptions: \nA. Harrisonburg\nB. Richmond\nC. Harper's Ferry\nD. Martinsburg\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"John Brown's raid occurred in 1859 when he attempted to seize weapons from the federal arsenal to spark a slave rebellion. The town where this arsenal was located is Harper's Ferry, which was then part of Virginia. This event significantly heightened tensions leading up to the American Civil War.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":65}},"cost":5.2e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_3712","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of these 1900s tanks were designed and built BEFORE the others?\n\nOptions: \nA. M4 Sherman\nB. Cromwell \nC. Renault FT\nD.  Panzer IV\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Renault FT was designed and built during World War I, entering service in 1917, making it the earliest tank among the options. The other tanks—Panzer IV, M4 Sherman, and Cromwell—were developed during the late 1930s and early 1940s for use in World War II. Therefore, the Renault FT predates the others by over two decades.\n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":107,"output_tokens":92}},"cost":6.911e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_924","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following African countries was most successful in resisting colonization?\n\nOptions: \nA. Namibia\nB. Côte d’Ivoire\nC. Ethiopia\nD. Congo\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Ethiopia is the only African country that successfully resisted European colonization, maintaining its independence throughout the colonial period, notably after defeating Italian forces at the Battle of Adwa in 1896. While other nations faced colonization, Ethiopia preserved its sovereignty. \n\n\\boxed{C}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.806e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"OpenTDB_History_2026","prompt":"Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which dictator killed the most people?\n\nOptions: \nA. Joseph Stalin\nB. Kim Il Sung\nC. Adolf Hitler\nD. Mao Zedong\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The most likely correct answer is D. Mao Zedong. Estimates of deaths under his leadership, particularly during the Great Leap Forward, range from 45 to 70 million due to famine and political campaigns. While Stalin and Hitler also caused massive loss of life, Mao's policies resulted in the highest number of fatalities.\n\n\\boxed{D}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":85}},"cost":6.53e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_61","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A suburb of a city with this name included an iron mine that lent its name to the daiquiri.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The suburb is Daiquirí, located near Santiago de Cuba. The iron mine there was named Daiquirí, and the cocktail \"daiquiri\" was named after this location. \n\n\\boxed{Daiquirí}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":48}},"cost":3.933e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_96","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The first recorded use of this river's current name, which may mean \"our boat,\" is by Alvise da Cadamosto (\"all-VEE-say da kah-dah-MOH-stoo\"), who met the damel of Cayor along the coast just to its south.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to a West African river whose mouth lies along the coast of modern Senegal, just south of the historic kingdom of Cayor. Venetian explorer Alvise da Cadamosto, sailing for Portugal in the 1450s, left the first written record of the river’s present-day name while describing his meeting with the damel (ruler) of Cayor. Linguistic evidence identifies that name as “Gambia,” plausibly derived from a local phrase meaning “our boat.”\n\n\\boxed{Gambia R. (or Gambie)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":238,"output_tokens":109},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001021,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_141","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A uranium-containing submarine that ran aground near a naval base in this body of water was nicknamed \"Whiskey on the Rocks.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The submarine incident referred to occurred in the Baltic Sea when the Soviet submarine K-129, a Whiskey-class submarine, ran aground near the Swedish naval base at Karlskrona in 1981. This event became famously known as \"Whiskey on the Rocks\" due to the submarine's class and the location of the grounding. The body of water where this happened is the Baltic Sea.\n\n\\boxed{Baltic Sea}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":94}},"cost":6.745e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_223","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The world's longest railway platform is found in this country.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The world's longest railway platform is located in India, at Gorakhpur Railway Station. It measures approximately 1,366 meters (4,480 feet) in length. \n\n\\boxed{India}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":71,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":3.743e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_329","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This city contains many yalis, mansions built immediately adjacent to the sea.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city known for its yalis, which are traditional wooden mansions built along the shoreline, is Istanbul. These historic homes are primarily located along the Bosphorus Strait, offering direct sea access. \n\n\\boxed{Istanbul}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":75,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":3.795e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_357","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this city, a clock with the times of 148 major cities around the globe is topped by a sculpture of an atom and is known as the Urania World Clock.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Urania World Clock, featuring times of 148 major cities and topped by an atomic sculpture, is located in Berlin, Germany. It is a famous landmark situated on Alexanderplatz. \n\n\\boxed{Berlin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":46}},"cost":4.0079999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_387","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 2010, this country drew international attention by passing an environmentalist act called the Law of the Rights of Mother Earth.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The country that passed the Law of the Rights of Mother Earth in 2010 is Bolivia. This landmark legislation granted nature the same rights as humans, recognizing Earth as a living system with legal rights to exist and thrive. The law drew global attention for its innovative environmental approach.\n\n\\boxed{Bolivia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":87,"output_tokens":65}},"cost":5.031e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_388","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This state is home to the second-most densely concentrated surname in the US, Locklear, which comes from the Lumbee people in Robeson County.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Lumbee (a Native American tribe) are indigenous to Robeson County, which is located in southeastern North Carolina; the surname Locklear is a common Lumbee name and is heavily concentrated in that area per US Census data. This directly identifies the state referenced in the query. (No other state matches the geographic and demographic details given.)\n\n\\boxed{North Carolina}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":74},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.9e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_399","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Fauna unique to this region include the llama-like guanaco, the ostrich-like Darwin's rhea, and a rodent called the tuco-tuco.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The region described is South America, specifically Patagonia, which is home to unique wildlife such as the guanaco, Darwin's rhea, and the tuco-tuco. These species are native to the southern parts of South America, particularly in Argentina and Chile. \n\n\\boxed{South America}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":63}},"cost":4.989e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_407","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Ghost towns in this desert include Kelso, formerly known as \"the town without television,\" and the abandoned silver mining town Calico.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The desert described, containing ghost towns like Kelso and Calico, is the Mojave Desert. Both towns are located in Southern California within this desert region. \n\n\\boxed{Mojave Desert}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":42}},"cost":3.638e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_411","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A stacked stone sculpture called an Inuksuk was used as a symbol for one event in this city.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Inuksuk was used as a symbol for the 2010 Winter Olympics, which were held in Vancouver, Canada. This stone sculpture represents guidance and support in Inuit culture and was chosen to reflect the spirit of the Games. \n\n\\boxed{Vancouver}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.533e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_438","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Europe's largest indoor aquarium is located in this city, which is home to the art collection of oil magnate Calouste Gulbenkian.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city described is Lisbon, Portugal. It is home to the Calouste Gulbenkian Museum, which houses the art collection of oil magnate Calouste Gulbenkian, and nearby is the Lisbon Oceanarium, Europe's largest