a controversial 1974 incident in which Swedish royalty were allegedly given a tour of Boston's seedier establishments by actor Chuck Connors, who was humorously dubbed \"Mayor\" of the city. The event became a widely circulated anecdote, though its accuracy has been debated. The place in question is Boston.\n\n\\boxed{Boston}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":77}},"cost":5.7119999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_1835","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Li Ying's 2007 documentary about this site opens with footage of a 90-year-old swordsmith at work.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a documentary by Chinese filmmaker Li Ying that focuses on an ancient Chinese location renowned for its centuries-old tradition of sword-forging. Longquan (in Zhejiang province) is the only site that matches this description, having been a center of sword production since at least the Spring and Autumn period and still home to living master smiths in 2007. No other well-known Chinese historic site (Great Wall, Shaolin, etc.) fits the opening footage of the 90-year-old artisan.\n\n\\boxed{Longquan (China)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":109},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.530000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_Geography_304","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A boundary dispute with Britain over this state's border was resolved in 1903 and involved determining the exact meaning of vague terms such as \"the mountains parallel to the coast.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The boundary dispute involving vague terms like \"the mountains parallel to the coast\" was resolved in 1903 and pertained to the Alaska-British Columbia border. This dispute arose between the United States and Britain (representing Canada) over the interpretation of the boundary set by the 1825 Anglo-Russian Convention. The arbitration tribunal favored the U.S. position, clarifying the border through the Alaska Panhandle.\n\n\\boxed{Alaska}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":93}},"cost":6.827999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_776","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This country's southernmost district of Inagua produces one million tons of salt annually for the Morton Salt company.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question points to a country whose southernmost district (Inagua) is a major salt-producing site. Great Inagua is the southernmost island of the Bahamas and has long hosted a large Morton Salt evaporation facility that yields roughly one million tons per year. This geographic and commercial match identifies the Bahamas as the only country that satisfies every element of the clue.\n\n\\boxed{Bahamas}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":77},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.87e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_987","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This group's staples include the  kumara , a type of sweet potato.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The group referred to is the Māori people of New Zealand, who traditionally cultivated and relied on the kumara as a staple food. Kumara, a type of sweet potato, was brought to New Zealand by Polynesian ancestors of the Māori and has been central to their agriculture and diet for centuries. \n\n\\boxed{Māori}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":76,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.428e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1056","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 1830, this American city was connected to nearby Hamburg by the  Best Friend , the country's first commercially-run passenger steam locomotive.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Best Friend of Charleston was the first commercially operated passenger steam locomotive in the United States, beginning service in 1830. It ran on the South Carolina Canal and Railroad Company line between Charleston and Hamburg, South Carolina. Therefore, the American city connected to Hamburg by this locomotive was Charleston.\n\n\\boxed{Charleston}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.323e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_576","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During the 2008 running of this event, many objected to the use of Randwick Racecourse in Sydney.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a recurring international event whose 2008 edition was staged in Sydney. Historical records confirm that World Youth Day 2008 culminated with a papal Mass at Randwick Racecourse; the choice of venue drew public objections from anti-gambling and secular groups. No other periodic event matches both the location and the documented controversy.\n\n\\boxed{World Youth Day (2008)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":78},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.96e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1154","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This object and its \"spider\" support were brought to San Francisco for the opening of the first Transcontinental Telephone Line.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The object referred to is the Liberty Bell. It was transported to San Francisco in 1915 for the Panama-Pacific International Exposition, where it was displayed alongside the \"spider\" support—a special metal cradle designed to prevent further cracking. The bell was included to symbolize national unity, coinciding with the celebration of the first transcontinental telephone line.\n\n\\boxed{Liberty Bell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":81}},"cost":5.952e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_8","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two rivers named for nearby deposits of this substance provide water to the former trade hub of Hotan.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The two rivers near Hotan are named after jade, as the region is historically famous for its jade deposits. These rivers, the White Jade River and the Black Jade River, carry jade fragments from nearby mountains. The substance is jade.\n\n\\boxed{jade}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":79,"output_tokens":53}},"cost":4.207e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_494","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: People in this country often clean their teeth with a short stick of wood called a mefakia, which serves as a natural toothbrush.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"People in many African countries, particularly in parts of West Africa such as Senegal and Gambia, traditionally use a short stick of wood called a *mefakia* or *chew stick* to clean their teeth. These sticks come from trees like the Salvadora persica and have natural antibacterial properties. The practice is widespread and culturally significant in these regions.\n\n\\boxed{Senegal}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":88,"output_tokens":82}},"cost":6.0639999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_901","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: New York Times  TV critic Jack Gould criticized this person's \"lapse in judgment\" for appearing in a 1959 Good Luck Margarine commercial.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to a specific 1959 TV commercial for Good Luck Margarine that drew public criticism from New York Times critic Jack Gould. The person in question was the respected poet, historian, and Lincoln biographer Carl Sandburg, whose appearance in the ad was viewed as an undignified “lapse in judgment.” This matches the historical record of Gould’s column and Sandburg’s documented commercial work.\n\n\\boxed{Carl Sandburg}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.65e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1380","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 2016, this speci c animal's name was given to a newly discovered spaceship in Conway's Game of Life that is only 28 cells and is the  rst to move at a speed of c over ten.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question pertains to a specific discovery in Conway's Game of Life (a well-known 2D cellular automaton). In 2016, a new orthogonal spaceship was found with a minimum population of 28 live cells; it travels at the previously unattained speed of \\(c/10\\) and was accordingly christened the “copperhead” after the venomous snake of the same name. This matches the description exactly; earlier spaceships (glider, loafer, etc.) differ in speed, size, or discovery date.\n\n\\boxed{copperhead}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":225,"output_tokens":114},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00010200000000000001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_370","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A philandering king belonging to this royal house was married to the Polish Marie Leszczynska, who was the longest-serving queen of her country.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The philandering king referred to is Louis XV of France, who was married to Marie Leszczyńska, the longest-serving queen consort of France. He belonged to the House of Bourbon. \n\n\\boxed{Bourbon}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":91,"output_tokens":47}},"cost":4.0029999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_473","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: These laws severely limited the number of town hall meetings to one per year.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question is an identification prompt for a specific set of British parliamentary measures from 1774. Those measures (the Massachusetts Government Act in particular) explicitly capped town meetings at one annual session unless the royal governor granted prior approval. In standard U.S. history curricula the acts are collectively known as the Intolerable Acts (or, less commonly, the Coercive Acts).\n\nThus the most common and correct answer is the Intolerable Acts.\n\n\\boxed{Intolerable Acts (MA Govt Act)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":195,"output_tokens":101},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.95e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_598","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This group's anthem was a song called \"Mury\" (MOO-rih), or \"Walls.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anthem \"Mury\" (Walls) was associated with the Polish anti-communist movement, particularly popularized by the rock band Perfect. It became a symbol of resistance during the Solidarity movement in the 1980s. The group most commonly linked to this anthem in that context is Solidarity.\n\n\\boxed{Solidarity}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":83,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.279e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1030","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Rebecca Lukens was a pioneering female executive in this industry.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Rebecca Lukens was a pioneering female executive in the steel industry. She took over her family's iron and steel business in the early 19th century, becoming one of the first female industrialists in the United States. Her leadership helped sustain the company through difficult economic periods.\n\n\\boxed{steel}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":71,"output_tokens":63}},"cost":4.7029999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_11","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During a referendum on moving this state's capital, Marcus Daly's firm campaigned to keep it in a company town.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to a historical event in Montana, where copper magnate Marcus Daly supported keeping the state capital in Helena, a company-influenced town, during a referendum. His rival, William A. Clark, backed moving the capital to his own company town, Anaconda. The capital remained in Helena.\n\n\\boxed{Montana}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":82,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.206e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1618","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This body of water occupies the only surviving fragment of the Dura-Europos route map, which was drawn onto the leather portion of a Roman shield.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Dura-Europos shield map (ca. 260 CE) is the sole surviving fragment of a Roman itinerarium pictum drawn on leather; its surviving portion depicts a stretch of coastline together with labeled ports and the associated Roman road network. Those ports (Odessos, Byzantion, Heraclea, etc.) lie along the western and northern shores of a single large body of water that the map itself labels as the Pontus Euxinus. That body of water is therefore the Black Sea.\n\n\\boxed{Black Sea}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":110},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.72e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_581","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 1950, Hilary Koprowski tested an early version of this thing at Letchworth Village.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Hilary Koprowski tested an early version of the polio vaccine at Letchworth Village in 1950. This was one of the first human trials of a live attenuated polio vaccine. The correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{polio vaccine}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.512e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_913","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This non-Greek city was briefly ruled by the Duke of Athens, Walter of Brienne. \"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The non-Greek city briefly ruled by Walter of Brienne, Duke of Athens, was Thebes. However, Thebes is actually a Greek city, so it does not fit the \"non-Greek\" criterion. The correct non-Greek city associated with Walter of Brienne’s rule is actually not accurate in this context—Walter ruled parts of Greece, particularly Athens and Thebes, which are Greek. There is likely confusion in the question, but no prominent non-Greek city was ruled by him. Given common interpretations, the intended answer might be **Thebes**, despite it being Greek.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Thebes}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":79,"output_tokens":130}},"cost":8.827e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_233","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The composition of these items was regulated by the 1906 Berne Convention.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The 1906 Berne Convention (formally the Berne Convention on the Prohibition of the Use of White Phosphorus in the Manufacture of Matches) was an international treaty aimed at ending \"phossy jaw\" and other industrial diseases. It therefore regulated the chemical composition of matches by banning the toxic white/yellow phosphorus formerly used in their heads. No other common category of manufactured item fits the 1906 date or the treaty's subject matter.\n\n\\boxed{matches}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":196,"output_tokens":94},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.62e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1288","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the 1930s, this state's governor proclaimed, \"I am not a liberal.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote is a truncated version of a well-known 1930s remark by Huey Long (“I am not a liberal … I am a radical”), delivered while he was governor of Louisiana and promoting his “Share Our Wealth” agenda. No other state executive of that decade is associated with the line. Therefore the state referenced by the clue is Louisiana.\n\n\\boxed{Louisiana}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":199,"output_tokens":77},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.83e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_694","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A banned album in this country whose title translates as Keep the Gate Closed,\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to a specific banned rock album from the communist era (under Enver Hoxha), whose Albanian title \"Mbyllye portat\" literally translates as \"Keep the Gate Closed.\" This makes Albania the only country that fits the phrasing \"in this country.\" No other nation has a documented case matching the translated title and banning incident.\n\n\\boxed{Albania}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":195,"output_tokens":75},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.649999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_264","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The deadliest building collapse in the United States before the collapse of the Twin Towers occurred in one of these buildings when a walkway collapsed onto a crowded dance floor.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question describes the 1981 Hyatt Regency Kansas City disaster, in which suspended walkways collapsed onto a tea-dance floor, killing 114 people. This remained the deadliest non-deliberate structural failure in U.S. history until the 9/11 attacks on the World Trade Center towers. No other pre-2001 American building collapse matches the described circumstances or casualty count.\n\n\\boxed{Hyatt Regency KC (walkway collapse)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":212,"output_tokens":90},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.740000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1836","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: According to tradition, a 炤re in this city that fused metal statues together originated its namesake high-quality bronze.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a legendary fire that melted and fused various metal statues, thereby creating an exceptionally hard and lustrous alloy. Historical sources (Pliny, Plutarch) locate this event in ancient Corinth and credit the resulting metal with the name “Corinthian bronze.” Consequently the city whose name is attached to that bronze is Corinth.\n\n\\boxed{Corinth}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":205,"output_tokens":72},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.7e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_125","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A holder of this title commissioned a set of miniatures to accompany the story collection Tales of a Parrot.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The holder of the title who commissioned a set of miniatures for *Tales of a Parrot* was likely a Mughal noble or ruler interested in Persian literature and manuscript illustration. The most common answer associated with such patronage is Emperor Akbar, who supported the production of illustrated manuscripts, including animal fables and stories in the Persian tradition. \n\n\\boxed{Akbar}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.793e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1505","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A venue named for this family employed Oscar Tschirky, a celebrity chef who popularized Thousand Island dressing.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to the maître d'hôtel Oscar Tschirky, who worked for decades at the Waldorf-Astoria Hotel in New York and is credited with popularizing Thousand Island dressing. That hotel was constructed and named for the Astor family (specifically William Waldorf Astor and John Jacob Astor IV). Thus the family referenced by the question is the Astors.\n\n\\boxed{Astor(s)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":202,"output_tokens":82},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.14e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1740","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During World War I, Hog Island became the largest center for this industry in the world.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"During World War I, Hog Island, located near Philadelphia, was developed as a major shipbuilding site to support the war effort. It became the largest shipbuilding center in the world at the time, focused on constructing cargo ships to transport troops and supplies. The industry in question is shipbuilding.\n\n\\boxed{shipbuilding}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":77,"output_tokens":66}},"cost":4.961e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_731","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Many buildings in this country incorporate the quote \"Boys, be ambitious!\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote \"Boys, be ambitious\" originates from William S. Clark, an American educator who helped found Sapporo Agricultural College (now Hokkaido University) in 1876. It quickly became a well-known inspirational motto in Japan, and is frequently inscribed on buildings, monuments, and public structures (especially in Hokkaido). No other country has this level of cultural association with the phrase.\n\n\\boxed{Japan}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":194,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.93e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_543","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This man's son, Peter, built a house over his father's childhood home, which was known as \"The Locusts.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a specific piece of trivia about a well-known modern figure whose family home bore the distinctive name “The Locusts.” Warren Buffett grew up in that house in Omaha; his son Peter later purchased the property and erected a new residence directly on the original foundation. No other prominent individual matches the combination of the son’s name, the house name, and the act of building over the childhood site.\n\n\\boxed{Warren Buffett}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.48e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1191","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A group of people in this country undergoes the  Soyal  ritual on the winter solstice to \"awaken\" the Sun from its \"sleep\".\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Soyal (or Soyalangw) ceremony is a traditional winter-solstice rite performed by the Hopi people to ritually “awaken” the sun and ensure the return of longer days. The Hopi are an indigenous nation whose villages lie within the present-day borders of the United States (primarily northeastern Arizona). No other country is associated with this specific ritual.\n\n\\boxed{United States}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.27e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_618","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Jeanne Chall, a notable researcher of this ability, names a test for gauging it along with Edgar Dale.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Jeanne Chall is best known for her research on reading development and stages. Together with Edgar Dale she created the well-known Dale-Chall readability formula, a quantitative test that scores the comprehension difficulty of a passage. The entity being measured by that test is therefore readability.\n\n\\boxed{readability}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":202,"output_tokens":59},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":6.99e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1709","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During the papacy of Paul III, the ancient tomb of this Roman's daughter was discovered to contain a well-preserved body and a still-burning lamp.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question alludes to a well-known Renaissance-era anecdote about an ancient Roman tomb opened in the 1540s (during the reign of Pope Paul III) that still contained an apparently intact body and a lamp whose flame had continued to burn for centuries. Contemporary accounts and later retellings consistently identify the occupant as Tullia (or Tulliola), the daughter of Marcus Tullius Cicero. Consequently the “Roman” referenced in the question is Cicero himself.\n\n\\boxed{Cicero}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":98},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.12e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_391","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After the final defeat in the Sepoy Mutiny, the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah II, was exiled to this country.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"After the final defeat in the Sepoy Mutiny, the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah II, was exiled to Rangoon, which was in British-controlled Burma (now Myanmar). At the time, Burma was a British colony, making it the destination of his exile. \n\n\\boxed{Burma}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":5.2499999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_245","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The \"Hjortspring\" (\"YOTE-spring\") example of these objects is similar to many Bronze Age petroglyphs.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes the Hjortspring find, a well-preserved prehistoric vessel whose hull shape, raised prow and stern, and crew arrangement closely match the boat motifs repeatedly carved on Scandinavian rock surfaces during the Nordic Bronze Age. Those motifs are therefore understood by archaeologists as representations of the same class of watercraft. Consequently, the objects referenced by the clue are boats (or ships).\n\n\\boxed{boats (Hjortspring boat)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":85},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.37e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1317","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A preacher from this city's New Bethel Baptist Church became known as The Man with the Million Dollar Voice a ter recording and selling his sermons, such as \"The Eagle Stirreth Her Nest.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to famed gospel preacher C.L. Franklin (Aretha Franklin’s father), whose best-known sermons were recorded and sold commercially. He served for decades as pastor of New Bethel Baptist Church, which is located in Detroit.  \n\nThus the city named in the clue is Detroit.\n\n\\boxed{Detroit}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":62},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.44e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_638","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Most of the companies owned by Errett Lobban Cord were in this industry.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Most of the companies owned by Errett Lobban Cord were in the automotive industry. He was a prominent figure in American automobile manufacturing and transportation during the early 20th century, acquiring and leading several well-known car companies.\n\n\\boxed{automotive}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":75,"output_tokens":53}},"cost":4.155e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_262","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This country was dominated for more than a century by the True Whig Party, which once infamously \"won\" an election with fifteen times more votes than eligible voters.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The country described is Liberia, where the True Whig Party maintained political dominance for over a century. The party's control included fraudulent elections, such as one in which votes far exceeded the number of eligible voters. This occurred in the early 20th century, highlighting the lack of democratic integrity during that period.\n\n\\boxed{Liberia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":71}},"cost":5.482e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1084","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: AT&amp;T executive Frederick Kappel reformed this organization after its public failure in Chicago during Christmas in 1966.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to the 1966 Chicago mail crisis (a massive Christmas-season backlog that publicly exposed the breakdown of manual mail sorting). AT&T chairman Frederick Kappel was then named to lead the Presidential Commission that produced the 1968 Kappel Report; its recommendations were enacted in the Postal Reorganization Act of 1970, converting the old Post Office Department into the independent U.S. Postal Service.  \n\nThus the organization in question is the United States Postal Service (USPS).  \n\n\\boxed{US Postal Service (USPS)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":108},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.46e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1090","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: While running for President, a man in this position released an unprecedented 12 years of his tax returns.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to a historical first during a presidential campaign. George Romney (Mitt Romney’s father) was serving as Governor of Michigan when he released twelve years of tax returns while seeking the 1968 Republican nomination—an action then regarded as unprecedented. The position that satisfies the phrasing “a man in this position” is therefore Governor of Michigan.\n\n\\boxed{Gov. of Michigan (G. Romney)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":81},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.07e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_230","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This leader let filmmaker Barbet Schroeder record his cabinet's daily proceedings for a documentary subtitled \"A Self Portrait.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The leader who allowed filmmaker Barbet Schroeder to document his cabinet's daily proceedings in a film subtitled \"A Self Portrait\" was Iranian Prime Minister Amir-Abbas Hoveyda.\n\nThe resulting documentary, \"General Idi Amin Dada: A Self Portrait,\" however, features Ugandan dictator Idi Amin, not Hoveyda—Schroeder gained Amin's cooperation to film his regime.\n\nThus, the correct answer is Idi Amin.\n\n\\boxed{Idi Amin}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":83,"output_tokens":105}},"cost":7.379e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_622","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A presidential candidate in this year did a series of advertisements featuring him informally talking to former baseball player Joe Garagiola.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to a notable U.S. presidential campaign ad series. These informal, conversational spots (sometimes called “Coffee with Nixon”) were produced for Richard Nixon’s 1968 run and featured him chatting with former catcher and broadcaster Joe Garagiola. No other election year matches this specific advertising format.\n\n\\boxed{1968}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":67},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.43e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_657","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After this man was given a cup worth fifty pounds at Peterborough Abbey, he refused to accept the gift unless his friend was given an equal gift.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The anecdote describes an act of loyalty between companions during an 11th-century episode at Peterborough Abbey. The only figure consistently linked to that location and that precise gesture (refusing a costly cup until an equal gift was given to his comrade) is the Anglo-Saxon outlaw Hereward. Later medieval sources that recount Hereward’s career therefore identify him as the man in question.\n\n\\boxed{Hereward the Wake}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":82},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.280000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_791","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 1900, Charles Morse unsuccessfully attempted to raise the price of this good after monopolizing it in New York City.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"In 1900, Charles Morse attempted to corner the market on ice, which he had largely monopolized in New York City, by raising its price. His scheme involved controlling the supply of ice during a particularly hot summer, but it ultimately failed due to public outrage and intervention. The good was ice.\n\n\\boxed{ice}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.2579999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_167","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Washington Post claimed that Barack Obama's head of this agency considered resigning because \"there are days where I have literally nothing to do.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question is a reference to a 2012 Washington Post profile of the director of the Selective Service System (the agency responsible for maintaining records of potential military draftees). Because there has been no active draft since 1973, the director at the time reported having essentially no day-to-day responsibilities, prompting the quoted remark about considering resignation. This matches the clue exactly; no other Obama-era agency fits the description.\n\n\\boxed{Selective Service System}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":91},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.69e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_44","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Ken Light sued Richard Taylor over his performance of this action, which led voters to associate John Kerry with Jane Fonda during the 2004 presidential election.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a notorious 2004 election dirty trick in which a photograph of John Kerry sharing a stage with Jane Fonda was fabricated and widely circulated. The original image had been taken decades earlier by photojournalist Ken Light; graphic artist Richard Taylor was later sued by Light for digitally inserting Kerry into it. That manipulation is the action referred to, universally known as photoshopping.\n\n\\boxed{photoshopping (Kerry/Fonda photo)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.67e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1019","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Homeowners in this city were granted a special bread ration called the  panes aedium .\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The statement describes a historical detail from the Roman Empire under Emperor Aurelian (r. 270–275 CE). As part of reforms to the grain dole (annona), he instituted a daily distribution of bread loaves specifically to homeowners, referred to in Latin sources as panes aedium. This policy is attested only for Rome itself.\n\nThus, the city in question is Rome.\n\n\\boxed{Rome}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":198,"output_tokens":80},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.960000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_49","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An outside supporter of this cause, Frank Abney Hastings, encouraged its proponents to adopt steam technology.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The cause referred to is the Greek War of Independence. Frank Abney Hastings, a British Philhellene, supported the Greek revolutionaries and advocated for the use of steam-powered ships to modernize their naval efforts. The correct answer is the Greek War of Independence.\n\n\\boxed{Greek War of Independence}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":96,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.988e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_404","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a paper with Christos Papadimitriou, this man came up with an algorithm for pancake sorting that was the fastest such algorithm for thirty years.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The man referred to is Bill Gates. In 1979, Bill Gates co-authored a paper with Christos Papadimitriou on pancake sorting, presenting an algorithm that held the record for the fastest solution for decades. \n\n\\boxed{Bill Gates}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":92,"output_tokens":56}},"cost":4.5560000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1216","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a dream from this text, two homosocial companions are represented as a cow who loses her horn and a hornless bull.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a very specific oneiric symbol from Old Norse literature, in which a pair of sworn foster-brothers (fóstbræðr) appear as a cow that loses a horn and a hornless bull. This emblematic dream occurs in Fóstbræðra saga, the saga devoted to precisely those two companions (Þorgeirr and Þormóðr). No other medieval text matches the combination of homosocial protagonists and the exact bovine imagery.\n\n\\boxed{Fóstbræðra saga}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.27e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1190","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A city-state on this island sent settlers to Lycia to found Phaselis.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city-state of Rhodes, located on the island of Rhodes, sent settlers to Lycia to establish the city of Phaselis. This occurred in the 7th century BCE as part of Rhodian maritime expansion. The island of Rhodes is therefore the correct answer.\n\n\\boxed{Rhodes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":77,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":4.721e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_882","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An archbishop of this city who presided over and was responsible for implementing the Council of Trent was named Charles Borromeo.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Council of Trent was a significant ecumenical council of the Catholic Church, and its implementation was overseen in part by Archbishop Charles Borromeo in the Archdiocese of Milan.\n\nAlthough the council was held in Trent (now Trento, Italy), Borromeo was the Archbishop of Milan and played a key role in enacting its reforms there.\n\n\\boxed{Milan}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.845e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_106","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a scene first shown in this publication, a man states \"Oh no, my Lord, I assure you that parts of it are excellent!\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote is a classic reference to the \"curate's egg\" anecdote, in which a junior clergyman is too timid to admit his breakfast egg is spoiled and instead offers the euphemistic reply given in the query. That scene first appeared as a full-page cartoon captioned \"True Humility\" in the British humor magazine Punch (9 November 1895). Consequently the publication named by the query is Punch.\n\n\\boxed{Punch}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.58e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1109","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This man founded a whiskey distillery named after a local strain of moonshine, Valley Tan.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question is a historical trivia item pointing to a 19th-century Utah figure. Valley Tan was the colloquial name for the locally distilled whiskey (and wool cloth) produced in the Utah Territory in the 1850s–60s. Orrin Porter Rockwell, the well-known Mormon frontiersman and lawman, operated a Salt Lake City tavern and distillery that commercialized this product under that name.\n\nThus the man referenced is Orrin Porter Rockwell.\n\n\\boxed{Orrin Porter Rockwell}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":199,"output_tokens":99},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.93e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1230","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One form of this resource was governed by the precarium, a type of law derived from the word preces meaning \"request.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The resource referred to is land, specifically in the context of early medieval tenure where the precarium was a legal arrangement allowing someone to hold land at the will of the owner, based on a request. This system evolved from the Roman practice where \"preces\" (requests) were made for temporary use of property. The most common answer to the question is land.\n\n\\boxed{land}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":79}},"cost":5.845e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_593","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A recent book about this man's \"Black Jesus\" notes the brief but powerful influence of Adam Clayton Powell Sr\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Black Jesus\" reference and the mention of Adam Clayton Powell Sr. point to Adam Clayton Powell Jr., a prominent African American minister and politician. The book in question is likely discussing his legacy and influence, tying in his father's impact. The most common answer is Adam Clayton Powell Jr.\n\n\\boxed{Adam Clayton Powell Jr.}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":82,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.206e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_114","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When this group was hit with tear gas, the 12-week-old baby Bernard Myers was killed.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific historical incident from July 1932, when U.S. Army troops under Douglas MacArthur forcibly evicted the Bonus Army (also called the Bonus Expeditionary Force or BEF) from its Washington, D.C., encampments and used tear gas in the process. Contemporary accounts confirm that two infants, including 12-week-old Bernard Myers, died from the effects of the gas. This event is a standard clue in quiz-bowl or trivia contexts for identifying the Bonus Army.\n\n\\boxed{Bonus Army (marchers/BEF)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":200,"output_tokens":111},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.55e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1859","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this city, a democratic government under Giuseppe Galletti won popularity by abolishing the macinato tax.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to events in the Papal States during the 1848–49 revolutions. After Pius IX fled Rome, a short-lived constitutional regime operated under Prime Minister Giuseppe Galletti; one of its first popular measures was the repeal of the macinato (milling tax). That regime was seated in Rome, which therefore satisfies the phrasing “in this city.”\n\n\\boxed{Rome}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":78},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.92e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_490","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This country gained the provinces of Kexholm and Ingria by the terms of the Treaty of Stolbovo.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The country that gained the provinces of Kexholm and Ingria by the Treaty of Stolbovo was Sweden. This treaty, signed in 1617, ended the Ingrian War between Sweden and Russia, significantly expanding Swedish territory in the Baltic region. Russia was forced to cede these territories, marking a major shift in regional power.\n\n\\boxed{Sweden}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":92,"output_tokens":87}},"cost":6.416e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1113","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For one of these events, Taro Okamoto created the sculpture  Tower of the Sun , whose interior depicts a Tree of Life  that represents the evolution of animals.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue directly references the iconic Tower of the Sun sculpture (with its interior Tree of Life motif) that Taro Okamoto designed as the symbol of a major international exposition. This work was created exclusively for the 1970 World Exposition held in Osaka, Japan (commonly called Expo ’70), which celebrated “Progress and Harmony for Mankind.” No other event matches the described artwork or its evolutionary theme.\n\n\\boxed{Expo '70 (Osaka World's Fair)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":214,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.93e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1572","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Barry Popik's research revealed that the earliest instances of a phrase pairing these two words were in Christopher Bullock's Cobbler of Preston and Edward Ward's Dancing Devils.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement is a quiz clue (in the style of Jeopardy) pointing to the idiomatic phrase that pairs two specific numbers. Etymological research by Barry Popik and others has established that the earliest printed appearances of “at sixes and sevens” occur in Bullock’s 1716 farce and Ward’s 1724 poem. Consequently the two words referenced by the clue are “sixes” and “sevens.”\n\n\\boxed{at sixes and sevens}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":98},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.16e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1028","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Objects used to interact with this substance are often inscribed with adinkra symbols by the Akan people and are called mrammou.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a traditional Akan (Ghanaian) practice involving objects called mrammou. These are the decorated spoons, scoops, or measures used to handle and portion gold dust (the former regional currency), and they are frequently incised with adinkra symbols. Hence the substance in question is gold dust.\n\n\\boxed{gold dust}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":71},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.69e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_658","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Adolph Ochs coined a slogan for a business that he owned in this industry, and moved its headquarters to Longacre Square.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Adolph Ochs was the publisher of The New York Times, which he moved to Longacre Square (later renamed Times Square). He coined the slogan \"All the News That's Fit to Print\" for the newspaper. The industry is newspaper publishing.\n\n\\boxed{newspaper}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":58}},"cost":4.585e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_712","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The second president of this organization was a former comparative literature professor at Columbia University dismissed by school president Nicholas Murray Butler.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to Joel Elias Spingarn, the Columbia comparative-literature professor whose contract was not renewed by Nicholas Murray Butler in 1911 after Spingarn publicly defended academic freedom. Spingarn subsequently became a leading officer of the NAACP; after the death of its founding president Moorfield Storey he served as the organization’s second president (1930–1939). No other national organization fits both biographical details simultaneously.\n\n\\boxed{NAACP}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":92},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.66e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1039","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A kingdom of this people constantly moved between their summer capital of Peiting and their winter capital of Qocho.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The kingdom described is the Uyghur Khaganate, which established a seasonal court moving between Peiting (in the north) and Qocho (in the Tarim Basin) during the 9th century after their migration from Mongolia. This practice reflected their adaptation to the climate and geography of the region. The people are the Uyghurs.\n\n\\boxed{Uyghurs}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":82,"output_tokens":82}},"cost":5.986e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_470","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One of the highest-ranking positions in this party, Gauleiter, commanded an entire regional branch of it.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Gauleiter was a high-ranking position in the Nazi Party, responsible for leading a regional division known as a Gau. This role was integral to the party's organizational structure in Nazi Germany. The correct answer is the Nazi Party.\n\n\\boxed{Nazi Party}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":55}},"cost":4.3530000000000005e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_938","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During this event, parties to the Southeast Mutual Protection Treaty agreed not to obey official decrees.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The event referred to is the Boxer Rebellion, during which regional Chinese officials in the southeast refused to enforce the Qing government's support of the Boxers. The Southeast Mutual Protection Treaty was an agreement among these officials to maintain order and protect foreign interests. They agreed not to obey imperial decrees that supported the Boxer uprising.\n\n\\boxed{Boxer Rebellion}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":78,"output_tokens":73}},"cost":5.3939999999999995e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1007","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In this location, President Eisenhower gave a July 4, 1950, speech to Boy Scouts gathered for the first post-war Jamboree.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The correct answer is the site of the first post-war Boy Scout Jamboree, which was held at Valley Forge, Pennsylvania. President Eisenhower gave a speech there on July 4, 1950, addressing the gathered Scouts. This location is historically significant for its association with both the Revolutionary War and Scouting events.\n\n\\boxed{Valley Forge}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":92,"output_tokens":75}},"cost":5.6960000000000004e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1696","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At a beauty pageant in this city that was held in a basement, a group of women li ed up a banner that read \"don't let them kill us!\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The event described refers to the 1968 Miss America protest in Atlantic City, where feminist activists demonstrated against the objectification of women. The protest took place in the basement of the boardwalk hall where the pageant was held, and activists symbolically crowned a sheep and displayed a banner that included the message \"Women's Liberation: Don't Let Them Kill Us!\" This protest became a landmark moment in the women's rights movement.\n\n\\boxed{Atlantic City}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":102,"output_tokens":120}},"cost":8.526000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1549","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Biblioteca Ambrosiana in Milan holds a lock of this woman's hair from which Lord Byron stole a single strand.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a preserved lock of hair in Milan's Ambrosiana Library, from which Byron is documented to have taken a single strand during a visit. Historical records confirm this lock belonged to Lucrezia Borgia (enclosed with one of her letters), matching the description exactly. No other figure fits the specific combination of the library artifact and Byron's documented theft.\n\n\\boxed{Lucrezia Borgia}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":202,"output_tokens":82},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.14e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_579","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A character with this name is tasked by the Devil to plow 60 acres in half a day, but is helped by the Devil's daughter and his future wife, Lilith.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue describes a folkloric pact-with-the-devil episode in which the protagonist must complete an enormous agricultural task (plowing 60 acres in half a day). The Devil’s own daughter secretly aids him and later becomes his bride; that daughter is explicitly named Lilith.  \n\nThe single character who consistently appears in European (chiefly German and Jewish-legenda) variants of this exact narrative is Faust.  \n\nThus the name required by the question is Faust.\n\n\\boxed{Faust}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":98},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.2e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_149","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a book about this politician's \"intimate world,\" gender researcher Clarence A. Tripp claimed that he had homosexual encounters throughout his life.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The politician referenced in the question is Lyndon B. Johnson. Clarence A. Tripp, in his book *The Intimate World of Abraham Lincoln*, made controversial claims about Lincoln, not Johnson. However, the correct answer based on the context of Tripp's work and the subject of his book is Abraham Lincoln.\n\n\\boxed{Abraham Lincoln}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":89,"output_tokens":72}},"cost":5.476999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1689","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The U.S. negotiated a treaty with this tribe to allow the construction of a mail route that was called, like a more northerly route, the Federal Road.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The U.S. negotiated a treaty with the Creek Nation to allow the construction of a mail route known as the Federal Road. This road facilitated communication and travel through Creek territory and was similar to another Federal Road further north. \n\n\\boxed{Creek}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":52}},"cost":4.329e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1828","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Despite having a court order, Autherine Lucy was kicked out of this place.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Autherine Lucy was the first African American student admitted to the University of Alabama in 1956, following a federal court order to desegregate the institution. She was suspended and ultimately expelled after only three days amid violent protests by segregationists, despite the legal mandate for her enrollment. This event is a well-documented milestone in the civil rights movement, later leading to her readmission in 1980.\n\n\\boxed{Univ. of Alabama (Tuscaloosa)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":197,"output_tokens":96},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.740000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_664","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 2009, a man in this country crashed his car into a parade in an attempt to hit a bus carrying the royal family.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The incident described matches the 2009 Koninginnedag attack in Apeldoorn, where a lone driver deliberately rammed a parade float in an unsuccessful attempt to strike the royal family's coach carrying Queen Beatrix. No other country experienced a comparable vehicular assault on its monarchy that year.  \n\nThus the country in question is the Netherlands.\n\n\\boxed{Netherlands}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":73},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.81e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1797","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Minnesota Congressman John T. Bernard cast the only dissenting vote against a law banning US intervention in this conflict.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The reference is to the US Neutrality Act of 1937 (specifically its arms-embargo provisions), which Congress applied to the Spanish Civil War. Minnesota Representative John T. Bernard was the sole dissenting vote against that embargo, consistent with his public support for the Loyalist/Republican side. No other contemporaneous conflict matches the documented vote.\n\n\\boxed{Spanish Civil War}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":75},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.77e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1736","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This leader faced scandal when his mistress, B-movie actress Dovie Beams, played their sex tape during a press conference.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The leader in question is Ferdinand Marcos, the former President of the Philippines. The scandal involving his mistress, Dovie Beams, became public when she played a recording of their intimate encounter during a press event. This incident contributed to the growing controversy surrounding his regime.\n\n\\boxed{Ferdinand Marcos}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":85,"output_tokens":62}},"cost":4.8249999999999994e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1420","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Loren Graham took the perspective of a member of this profession, Peter Palchinsky, in a history of the early Soviet Union titled for his \"ghost.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Loren Graham's book *The Ghost of the Executed Engineer* tells the story of Peter Palchinsky, a mining engineer and critic of Soviet industrial policies. Palchinsky is portrayed as a figure whose ideas and warnings were ignored, leading to disastrous consequences. The profession central to the narrative is engineering.\n\n\\boxed{engineer}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":90,"output_tokens":69}},"cost":5.3099999999999996e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_933","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For this event, a line from \"The Song of the Bell\" was inscribed on a giant bell commissioned by Theodor Lewald.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a giant bell commissioned by German Olympic official Theodor Lewald and bearing an inscription drawn from Schiller’s “Das Lied von der Glocke.” Lewald oversaw preparations for the 1936 Games; the bell in question was cast for the Berlin Olympic Stadium and carried the line “Fest steht und treu die Wacht, die Schwart” together with the motto “Ich rufe die Jugend der Welt.” No other event matches this precise combination of commissioner, poem, and artifact.\n\nThus the event is the 1936 Summer Olympics (Berlin).\n\n\\boxed{1936 Summer Olympics (Berlin)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":122},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001022,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_433","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Gatow disaster exacerbated tensions prior to this event.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Gatow air disaster (April 1948) was a mid-air collision between British and Soviet aircraft that sharply raised East-West tensions in occupied Berlin. This directly preceded and helped precipitate the Soviet imposition of the Berlin Blockade two months later. The clue therefore points to the Berlin Blockade (or, less precisely, the ensuing Berlin Airlift) as the event in question.\n\n\\boxed{Berlin Blockade (1948)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":191,"output_tokens":88},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.22e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_706","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The adjective baluch (bah-LOOCH) sometimes describes varieties of these objects made in response to Soviet and American interventions in Afghanistan.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue points to a specific class of woven textiles whose traditional patterns were altered by 20th-century conflict. Baluch (or Baluchi) weavers, working in the Afghanistan–Pakistan border region, produced rugs whose field designs began to incorporate tanks, helicopters, rifles, and dates of Soviet or American incursions; these pieces are therefore called “Baluch war rugs.” Consequently the objects referenced are simply rugs (or carpets).\n\n\\boxed{rugs (war rugs)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":206,"output_tokens":94},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.82e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1087","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Because tagua nuts resemble this substance, the palms on which they are found are named for it.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Tagua nuts are often referred to as \"vegetable ivory\" because they closely resemble elephant ivory in texture and appearance. As a result, the palms that produce them are commonly called ivory palms. The substance they resemble is ivory.\n\n\\boxed{ivory}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":79,"output_tokens":53}},"cost":4.207e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_28","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An essay by Gerald Berreman analyzes this institution's conflicts with pushes for \"community development.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The essay by Gerald Berreman analyzes the caste system's conflicts with efforts toward \"community development\" in India. Berreman highlights how social stratification and inequality inherent in the caste system hinder inclusive development. The correct answer is the caste system.\n\n\\boxed{caste system}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":78,"output_tokens":57}},"cost":4.434e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_34","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An uprising sparked by poor policy responses to this event involved the first use of a tricolor flag whose central white color represent religious reconciliation between two other colors.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described tricolor (green-white-orange) matches the modern Irish flag, whose central white stripe was explicitly adopted to symbolize peace/reconciliation between Catholic (green) and Protestant (orange) populations. That flag made its first public appearance during the Young Irelander Rebellion of 1848. The rebellion itself was a direct reaction to the British government’s inadequate relief measures during the Irish Potato Famine of 1845–49.\n\nThus the event referenced in the query is the Irish Potato Famine.\n\n\\boxed{Irish Potato Famine (1845-49)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":211,"output_tokens":112},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.82e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_52","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an instance of this kind of political action, Kiyoshi Kuromiya spread a rumor that he would kill a dog in public, only to show a pamphlet reading \"Congratulations, you saved the life of a dog\" when thousands showed up.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The political action described is an example of a \"die-in,\" a form of nonviolent protest often used to draw attention to a cause. Kiyoshi Kuromiya used this tactic creatively to simulate a shocking act and then reveal a message promoting empathy and awareness. The correct answer is: \\boxed{die-in}.","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":110,"output_tokens":65}},"cost":5.33e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_102","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Max Jones's biography of this man portrays him as a \"martyr to science\" whose death united political radicals.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question is a reference to a specific historical biography, Max Jones's 2003 work *The Last Great Quest*. That book is a scholarly study of Robert Falcon Scott and the Terra Nova expedition; Jones explicitly frames Scott's demise (and the decision to haul scientific specimens to the end) as a \"martyrdom to science\" whose public commemoration briefly bridged Britain's class and political divides, including among radicals. No other figure matches both the biographer and the quoted interpretive language.\n\nThus the man in question is Robert Falcon Scott.\n\n\\boxed{Robert Falcon Scott (Capt. Scott)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":117},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.91e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_133","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Before his work on the Enlightenment, progressive historian Carl L. Becker proposed a \"dual\" interpretation of this event.\"Radical\" levelling effects of this event are assessed in a 1992 book by Gordon Wood.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The event referred to is the American Revolution. Carl L. Becker proposed a \"dual\" interpretation emphasizing both the struggle for liberty from Britain and the internal social conflict among Americans. Gordon Wood's 1992 book *The Radicalism of the American Revolution* assesses its transformative and leveling effects.\n\n\\boxed{American Revolution}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":108,"output_tokens":68}},"cost":5.484e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_171","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A writer from this present-day country was said to have created \"the strongest narcotic\" of his century by saying that history should be written \"as what actually happened.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted phrase about writing history \"as what actually happened\" is a direct reference to Leopold von Ranke's famous methodological dictum (\"wie es eigentlich gewesen\"). Ranke was a 19th-century Prussian historian, so the present-day country in question is Germany. The \"strongest narcotic\" remark is an allusion to Nietzsche's critique of Rankean historicism in his essay \"On the Use and Abuse of History for Life.\"\n\n\\boxed{Germany}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":213,"output_tokens":89},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.71e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_179","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Thomas Espenshade described the use of the \"repeated trials model\" in this area, which was analyzed as an example of how \"low intensity conflict\" doctrine \"comes home\" in a book on it by Timothy Dunn.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clues point to a specific academic/policy topic: Espenshade modeled the probability of eventual successful entry as a series of independent crossing attempts (the “repeated trials”/geometric distribution), while Dunn’s monograph explicitly treats the same setting as the domestic application of low-intensity conflict doctrine. Both authors focus on the same concrete domain—the enforcement regime along the southern boundary—so the referent of “this area” is the U.S.–Mexico border (or, equivalently, U.S. Border Patrol operations there).\n\nThus the most concise and commonly accepted answer is the U.S.–Mexico border.\n\n\\boxed{US-Mexico border (patrol/enforcement)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":225,"output_tokens":129},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001095,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_183","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Nowell Myres's archaeological investigations of this kingdom's origins concluded that it was settled from the north, using Nennius's accounts to establish a rough date range.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Nowell Myres studied the origins of the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Wessex and concluded, based on archaeological evidence and Nennius's historical accounts, that it was settled from the north. His work suggested a migration pattern and helped establish a tentative timeline for early West Saxon settlement. The kingdom in question is Wessex.\n\n\\boxed{Wessex}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.649e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_200","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: It's not Babylon, but by the false account in Walter Raleigh's History of the World, this city was called Campsor before it was rebuilt by its founder, an opponent of the Bactrian king Zoroaster and husband of the warrior queen Semiramis.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The city referred to is Nineveh. According to the apocryphal account in Walter Raleigh's *History of the World*, it was once called Campsor and was rebuilt by its founder, Nimrod, who opposed the Bactrian king Zoroaster and was associated with the legendary queen Semiramis. This identification aligns with mythological and pseudo-historical traditions of the time.\n\n\\boxed{Nineveh}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":114,"output_tokens":88}},"cost":6.761999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_293","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A few months before the outbreak of this war, John Slidell was sent on a diplomatic mission to its eventual losers, who rebuffed him.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"John Slidell’s 1845 mission was an attempt by President Polk to resolve border disputes and purchase California/New Mexico from Mexico. Mexico’s refusal to receive the envoy helped precipitate open hostilities the following year. This sequence of events therefore identifies the Mexican-American War (1846–1848).\n\n\\boxed{Mexican-American War}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":67},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.549999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_416","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Richard Russell led a Senate committee investigating whether a major career setback for this man was constitutional.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to the 1951 Senate investigation (chaired by Richard Russell) into President Truman’s relief of command of Gen. Douglas MacArthur during the Korean War. The central constitutional question examined by the Russell committee was whether the president possessed the authority to dismiss a five-star general without congressional consent. No other figure matches both the Russell committee’s remit and the description of a “major career setback” whose constitutionality was at issue.\n\n\\boxed{Douglas MacArthur}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":198,"output_tokens":96},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.76e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_504","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This man's son held a Christmas Day feast at Saalfeld where he began to consolidate a rebellion against his father.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to a well-known episode from Salian dynasty history. In December 1104/1105 the future Henry V hosted a Christmas court at Saalfeld expressly to rally German princes for the deposition of his father, Emperor Henry IV; the ensuing civil war ended with Henry IV’s forced abdication in 1105. No other medieval ruler matches both the location and the Christmas timing of the filial revolt.\n\nThus the person alluded to is Henry IV.\n\n\\boxed{Henry IV (HRE)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":203,"output_tokens":103},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.21e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_537","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Kenneth Westhues said this event was caused by \"workplace mobbing\" and disputed the conclusions of the Massengill Report.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The given statement is a clue (in the style of a quizbowl tossup) identifying a specific real-world incident. Kenneth Westhues, a sociologist specializing in workplace mobbing, explicitly applied that framework to the Virginia Tech shootings and criticized the official Massengill Report for overlooking faculty mistreatment of the perpetrator. No other well-known incident matches both the Westhues attribution and the Massengill reference.\n\nThus the event in question is the Virginia Tech massacre.\n\n\\boxed{Virginia Tech massacre (2007)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":106},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.44e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_612","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A company operating in the valley of the Kings River built a 62-mile structure to transport these things.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to the historic Sanger Lumber flume (completed in 1890) along California’s Kings River. That 62-mile wooden trough was purpose-built to float freshly cut timber from high Sierra stands down to the valley floor. Hence the things being transported are logs (or equivalently, lumber).\n\n\\boxed{logs (lumber)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":68},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.42e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_784","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A pope with this name opted to remain home after seeing a locust, which sounds similar to the Latin for \"stay in place.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to Pope John XXII, who reportedly chose to stay in Avignon rather than move to Rome after seeing a locust, a creature whose name resembles the Latin word \"locus,\" meaning \"place.\" The similarity between \"locust\" and \"locus\" plays on the idea of \"staying in place.\" However, the intended answer is likely a wordplay on \"John,\" as \"Johannes\" in Latin sounds like \"Ioannes,\" but the best fit for the riddle is \"John.\"\n\n\\boxed{John}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":86,"output_tokens":112}},"cost":7.838e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_812","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Many historians believe that this event was exacerbated by the incompetence of Dr. Willard Bliss.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question refers to a specific historical incident in which Dr. Willard Bliss’s medical decisions directly worsened the outcome. After President James A. Garfield was shot by Charles Guiteau on 2 July 1881, Bliss led the treatment team; his repeated probing of the wound with unsterilized instruments and fingers introduced fatal infections that Garfield would likely have survived. Historians therefore identify the event as Garfield’s death (or, equivalently, the fatal outcome of his assassination).\n\n\\boxed{Garfield's death (post-assass. infection)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":197,"output_tokens":108},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.34e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_864","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The man delivering this speech sarcastically asked \"Are fleets and armies necessary to a work of love and reconciliation?\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The man delivering this speech was Patrick Henry, and he said this during his \"Give Me Liberty or Give Me Death\" speech in 1775. He used sarcasm to question British intentions, implying that the presence of military forces contradicted any claim of peaceful reconciliation. The correct answer is Patrick Henry.\n\n\\boxed{Patrick Henry}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":81,"output_tokens":70}},"cost":5.253e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_926","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A writer from this country wrote about the \"Tiger of the Plains\" in a work arguing for civilization over barbarism.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The \"Tiger of the Plains\" refers to a metaphor used by Domingo Faustino Sarmiento in his work *Facundo*, where he contrasts civilization and barbarism in 19th-century Argentina. Sarmiento, an Argentine writer and president, used the term to describe the violent, authoritarian caudillos who threatened societal progress. The country is Argentina.\n\n\\boxed{Argentina}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":84,"output_tokens":82}},"cost":6.012e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_928","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A violent advocate of this cause interpreted a tornado striking Kansas as God's approval for her first-ever protests in Kiowa in 1900.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to Carry Nation (or Carrie A. Nation), whose first saloon-smashing raids occurred in Kiowa, Kansas, in 1900. She explicitly interpreted a tornado that struck Kansas earlier that year as divine endorsement for direct-action protests against alcohol. Her lifelong cause was therefore temperance (the organized movement that later produced national Prohibition).\n\n\\boxed{temperance (mov./Prohibition)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":207,"output_tokens":80},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.14e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_978","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: W. C. Handy wrote his first popular blues song for one of these organizations, which was rebuffed by GIs with dynamite charges in the 1946 Battle of Athens.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clue refers to political machines (i.e., corrupt urban party organizations controlled by a boss). W. C. Handy’s “Memphis Blues” (originally titled “Mr. Crump”) was commissioned for Edward H. Crump’s 1909 mayoral campaign in Memphis; Crump went on to dominate Tennessee politics for decades through exactly this kind of machine. The same Crump-backed machine in McMinn County, Tennessee, was forcibly ousted by returning GIs in the 1946 “Battle of Athens,” an armed confrontation that used dynamite to dislodge deputies barricaded in the county jail.\n\nThus the organizations in question are political machines.\n\n\\boxed{political machines (Crump machine)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":217,"output_tokens":145},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001159,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1058","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Irvine Welsh claimed that one of these things was \"ill-conceived nostalgia\" that was \"wrenched from the rancid prostates of senile, gibbering hippies\".\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quote criticizes an event steeped in 1960s counter-culture nostalgia. Its phrasing (“senile, gibbering hippies,” “rancid prostates”) points directly at the 1994 anniversary staging of the original Woodstock festival, which Irvine Welsh publicly derided in exactly those terms. Hence the referent is Woodstock ’94 (or, more broadly, the series of Woodstock revivals).\n\n\\boxed{Woodstock '94 (revival fest.)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":93},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":8.95e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1135","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Hans Trobst's article \"Fifteen Gallows\" was one of the only foreign reports on purges undertaken by this leader that were justified the following year in his 36-hour speech delivered over six consecutive days.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific set of events in early 1959: summary executions of Batista-era officials that drew limited foreign coverage, most notably the article “Fifteen Gallows” by German journalist Hans Trobst. Those executions were publicly defended the next year by the Cuban leader in an exceptionally long, multi-day address (a 36-hour speech spread over six consecutive days) that became one of his signature marathon performances. No other twentieth-century figure matches this precise combination of documented purges, the named foreign report, and the distinctive speech length and format.\n\nThus the leader in question is Fidel Castro.\n\n\\boxed{Fidel Castro}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":224,"output_tokens":127},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001083,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1243","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In 1966, John Gerassi argued that an oligarchical power play was behind a conspiracy exploiting this attitude during which town hall speeches were given by John L. Butler and future senator Frank Church.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes events from mid-1950s Boise, Idaho, in which a morals scandal targeting gay men was deliberately inflamed for political ends. John Gerassi’s 1966 book *The Boys of Boise* explicitly frames the episode as an oligarchic power play that exploited widespread societal prejudice against homosexuality; the cited town-hall appearances by Butler and Church occurred in that context. The attitude being invoked is therefore homophobia (sometimes labeled the “Lavender Scare”).\n\n\\boxed{homophobia (Boise scandal)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":104},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.6e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1378","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Carolyn Dean analyzed how the Corpus Christi festival in this modern-day country became a site of resistance as it syncretized with summer solstice celebrations.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Carolyn Dean is a specialist in colonial Andean art history whose key monograph analyzes the Corpus Christi festival in Cuzco. In that city the Catholic feast (movable, but falling near the June solstice) was deliberately superimposed on the Inca Inti Raymi solar festival; the resulting processions and images became a locus of indigenous resistance and negotiated identity under Spanish rule. Because Cuzco lies in the modern nation of Peru, that is the country referenced by the question.\n\n\\boxed{Peru}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":101},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.230000000000001e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1435","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Richard Kohn's article on \"The Inside Story\" of this event argues that it was largely stage-managed to bolster the case for central government.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a specific historical interpretation of an event from the Confederation period in U.S. history. Richard Kohn’s 1970 article “The Inside History of the Newburgh Conspiracy” (William and Mary Quarterly) explicitly advances the thesis that the 1783 officers’ protest was deliberately exaggerated or stage-managed by nationalists to strengthen the case for a more powerful central government under the Articles of Confederation. No other episode—Shays’ Rebellion, the Whiskey Rebellion, or the Philadelphia Mutiny—matches both the article’s title phrasing (“Inside Story/History”) and Kohn’s argument.\n\nThus the event in question is the Newburgh Conspiracy.\n\n\\boxed{Newburgh Conspiracy (1783)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":137},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.00011009999999999999,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1439","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Jonathan Winkler's Nexus explains how the a7termath of this event led to unprecedented integration of private business and government research, while Nicholas Lambert argued that its protagonists were unwilling to threaten their own economic networks.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clues point to a specific pre-WWII historical episode whose economic and communications ramifications have been analyzed by the two named historians. Nicholas Lambert’s *Planning Armageddon* examines British planning for economic warfare and shows that decision-makers ultimately refused to sever the global financial and shipping networks on which Britain itself depended. Jonathan Winkler’s *Nexus* demonstrates that the communications failures exposed by the same conflict produced, in its immediate aftermath, an unprecedented fusion of private-sector infrastructure with U.S. government signals-intelligence programs. Both arguments therefore converge on the First World War as the referent event.\n\n\\boxed{WWI (or equiv.)}\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":221,"output_tokens":126},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001072,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1449","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Alan Olmstead and Paul Rhode argued that new varieties of this commodity were responsible for its rise in production, against a controversial book with chapters like \"Feet,\" \"Breath,\" and \"Tongues.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Alan Olmstead and Paul Rhode challenged the arguments made in the controversial book *Time on the Cross* by Robert Fogel and Stanley Engerman, which used unconventional methods to analyze slavery's economics. They argued that improvements in cotton varieties, not slave labor efficiency, drove increased cotton production. The commodity in question is cotton.\n\n\\boxed{cotton}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":118,"output_tokens":96}},"cost":7.293999999999999e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1528","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Daniel Arasse argued that this event amounted to an inverted baptismal rite.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The phrasing of the query is characteristic of quiz-bowl “description” questions, which point to a single, well-known historical or artistic event. Daniel Arasse’s analyses of Jacques-Louis David’s iconic painting center on the bathtub setting, the fatal wound, and the substitution of blood for water; he therefore reads the assassination of Jean-Paul Marat as a deliberate inversion of the baptismal rite. No other episode in Arasse’s scholarship (guillotine executions, Poussin’s canvases, etc.) is described in these exact terms.\n\n\\boxed{assass. of Marat (by Corday)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":195,"output_tokens":123},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001005,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1616","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A poem criticizing this government program declares that \"the millet is scattered over the ground, the leaves of the sweet potato are withered,\" which was used as evidence to ✴re a defense minister.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The poem refers to the \"Great Leap Forward\" in China, during which agricultural policies led to widespread famine. The described imagery of scattered millet and withered sweet potato leaves reflects the crop failures and mismanagement under this program. The defense minister criticized was likely Peng Dehuai, who voiced concerns about the policy's failures.\n\n\\boxed{Great Leap Forward}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":100,"output_tokens":74}},"cost":5.74e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1637","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An oil painting of this battle displayed at the British Museum inaccurately shows an empress on horseback wielding a pistol and depicts Saint George throwing a spear at the losing army.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The described painting refers to the Battle of Waterloo, famously depicted in an inaccurate romanticized version by artist Lady Butler, though not displayed at the British Museum. The painting in question is \"The Black Brunswicker\" or a similar work, but the most accurate identification based on the description of inaccuracies and imperial figures is the Battle of Sedgemoor. However, the only battle commonly associated with such legendary embellishments involving Saint George and an empress is the fictionalized portrayal of the **Battle of Towton**.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Battle of Towton}}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":94,"output_tokens":116}},"cost":8.182e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1654","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Richard Slotkin's Gun᥸ghter Nation contrasts the Turner thesis with this man's theory of frontier history, concluding that this man's \"progressive\" narrative was more in᥸uential.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query concerns a specific argument in Richard Slotkin's *Gunfighter Nation* (1992), the final volume of his \"frontier myth\" trilogy. Slotkin juxtaposes Frederick Jackson Turner's famous 1893 frontier thesis with Theodore Roosevelt's multi-volume *The Winning of the West* (1889-1896). He labels Roosevelt's account the more \"progressive\" (and ultimately more culturally influential) narrative because it frames westward expansion as a story of racial conquest, national regeneration, and imperial destiny rather than Turner's emphasis on democratic renewal.\n\nThus the person whose theory is contrasted with Turner's is Theodore Roosevelt.\n\n\\boxed{Theodore Roosevelt}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":131},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001095,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1664","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A ruler of this dynasty whose lineage was questioned took out his sword, shouted \"here is my pedigree!,\" and threw gold coins into a crowd; that ruler of this dynasty commissioned scientists to invent the world's first fountain pen.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The clues match the Fatimid dynasty in 10th-century North Africa/Egypt. The ruler in question is the caliph al-Muʿizz li-Dīn Allāh, who quelled doubts about his claimed descent from Fāṭima by brandishing his sword (“Here is my pedigree!”) and scattering gold coins; the same caliph ordered the construction of the first reservoir-pen (an early fountain pen) that would not leak. No other Islamic dynasty fits both anecdotes simultaneously.\n\n\\boxed{Fatimid (dyn./caliphate)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":225,"output_tokens":110},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1684","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A city that fought to protect these people built a trebuchet that squared off against a machine called the \"cat,\" inspiring a work by Raimon Escrivan (\"rye-MOAN\" ess-kree-VAHN) in which the two siege engines engage in a mock debate.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query describes a historical episode from the Albigensian Crusade. Toulouse, a center of Cathar (“Albigensian”) sympathy, deployed a large trebuchet against Simon de Montfort’s siege engine nicknamed “La Cat”; the ensuing duel inspired Raimon Escrivan’s sirventes in which the two machines debate in verse. The people therefore being protected are the Cathars.\n\n\\boxed{Cathars (Albigensians)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":238,"output_tokens":90},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.26e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1721","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a speech given in the year of this election, a woman recounted getting beaten in the Montgomery County jail and said \"I question America.\"\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The quoted speech is Fannie Lou Hamer's testimony to the Credentials Committee at the 1964 Democratic National Convention, in which she described her beating in the Montgomery County jail and asked, \"Is this America?\" (sometimes rendered as \"I question America\").  \nBecause the speech occurred during the presidential campaign season, the election year referenced by the clue is 1964.  \n(The absence of any preceding context does not change the historical referent.)\n\n\\boxed{1964 pres. election (F. Lou Hamer)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":102},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.26e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1804","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One of these events came as a surprise to noblemen who fought against supporters of the Constitution of May 3rd.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The Constitution of May 3 (1791) was Poland’s response to the First Partition; its defenders fought (and lost) the 1792 Polish–Russian War against the Targowica Confederation. The victorious confederates expected the restoration of the old noble “Golden Freedoms,” but Russia and Prussia instead imposed the Second Partition (1793), which they had not anticipated. Hence the event that “came as a surprise” is the Second Partition of Poland.\n\n\\boxed{2nd Partition of Poland (1793)\n}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":204,"output_tokens":106},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9.38e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1871","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Samuel Eliot Morison's history of this document argued that Article III, which provided tax support for \"Protestant teachers of piety,\" slipped through without a valid two-thirds vote.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The question describes a specific provision (Article III) related to tax support for Protestant religious instructors, which is a distinctive feature of the 1780 Massachusetts Constitution (drafted primarily by John Adams). Historian Samuel Eliot Morison's writings on Massachusetts, including his analysis of the constitutional convention and ratification process, explicitly noted procedural irregularities with that article's two-thirds vote requirement. No other foundational American document (e.g., the U.S. Constitution, Articles of Confederation, or other state constitutions) matches this combination of details.\n\n\\boxed{Mass. Const. (1780)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":114},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"QANTA_History_1894","prompt":"Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A speech given about this personal attribute cites how \"Fuentes and McCafiferty\" died side by side with \"Bowie and Crockett\" at the Alamo.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The query refers to a famous 1988 Democratic National Convention keynote address (by Texas Governor Ann Richards) that explicitly invokes the cited Alamo defenders as an illustration of the single personal quality they all shared. The surrounding rhetorical context makes clear that the attribute being praised is courage (or bravery), not ethnicity, nationality, or any other trait. This is the only reading that fits both the wording of the question and the historical speech.\n\n\\boxed{courage (bravery)}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":215,"output_tokens":94},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":9e-05,"accuracy":null}]